window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/14_intervention"] = {"key": "vol4/14_intervention", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Shared Authority"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "Human Authority, Reaction Time, and Clearance"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Capability matrices and the intervention-enforcement split"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Initial condition matching and integrator pre-biasing"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Mapping operator input to arm motion"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "Bilateral teleoperation and haptic feedback passivity"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Admitting the remote-assistant session"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 2, "title": "Authority Handover Failure Modes"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 2, "title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 2, "title": "Summary"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Supervisory Intervention</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can handing a moving robot to a human operator cause the collision the handover was meant to prevent?</em></p>\n<p>Most deployed robots keep a person in reserve for the situations their learned policies cannot handle, and that reserve is usually treated as the final safeguard. A person, however, needs a large fraction of a second to respond even while watching closely and several seconds after only monitoring. A machine moving at walking pace covers meters in that time, so a request for help can consume the very clearance the help was meant to protect. Taking over also creates hazards of its own. Two controllers that each believe they hold a joint can command it at once, a controller that believes the other still holds it can leave it unattended, and an abrupt switch between their commands jolts the drivetrain and the load it carries. A takeover that grants unchecked control lets a startled or lagging operator drive the machine into a rack. A log that does not mark who commanded each instant can neither settle what caused an incident nor keep the machine's own near-failures out of the demonstrations it later learns from. Authority can change hands within a single control cycle, but the momentum already delivered across the causal boundary cannot be recalled, so a takeover must be engineered as a control action with its own deadline, its own limits, and its own record.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Specify operator permissions through capability matrices that bind each authenticated role's operations to physical channels, preemption tiers, and clearance-derived deadlines</li>\n<li>Select a state-dependent local fallback that remains physically feasible while human intervention is pending</li>\n<li>Calculate the latest fallback time and takeover speed ceilings from human reaction latency and remaining physical stopping clearance</li>\n<li>Evaluate the inherited chunk lease and the chosen manual lease against their modes' stopping reserves and available fallback mechanisms</li>\n<li>Design authority handshakes that keep one owner on each actuator output and size fixed-endpoint $\\Glyph{C}^2$ command blends within each channel's rate limit</li>\n<li>Diagnose mode confusion in split-channel handovers and specify the per-channel annunciation that keeps an operator's model of ownership current</li>\n<li>Evaluate whether an authority record provides the cryptographic and kinematic evidence needed to reconstruct post-incident fault causality</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Shared Authority</h2>\n<p>When a pallet blocks an aisle and the warehouse mobile manipulator requests a remote assistant to take over, the transfer introduces a delay that no test on the chassis can bound, because it belongs to a person. Part III left the running machine with a stopping budget whose every term belongs to the hardware, each with a declared bound and a loaded test that could refute it, even where the test has not yet run, as for the dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ that @sec-placement-dispatch-admission allocated but did not measure. Part IV begins where a person enters that loop.</p>\n<p>Supervisory intervention transfers the right to propose actions from an autonomous policy to a human operator, and eventually returns that authority to the machine. Nearly every embodied machine learning system operating in the physical world relies on this exchange. Remote teleoperation assistants clear blocked aisles and resolve scenes that warehouse robots and autonomous vehicles cannot interpret on their own. Safety drivers and teach-pendant operators step in whenever an operational policy drifts outside its design envelope, and their corrections protect the hardware and supply demonstration data for later retraining. Engineering block diagrams often reduce this supervision to a single switch or an external arrow pointing into the plant. In the physical world, however, kinetic energy does not pause while a multiplexer toggles inputs. Authority transfer is a control maneuver with its own deadlines, and it must be executed in coordination with the slowest and least predictable element in the loop, the human operator.</p>\n<p>How often that exchange happens on a mature fleet is a matter of public record in one jurisdiction. For the reporting years shown here, California required companies testing autonomous vehicles on its public roads to file annual reports of autonomous miles and of the disengagements, transfers of control to a person, that occurred during them [@cadmv2026disengagement]. @Fig-14-disengagement-record plots the filings of one long-running fleet, Waymo's.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ A DECADE OF REPORTED DISENGAGEMENTS (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-14-disengagement-record and the paragraph after it.\n# │ Goal: Show that human takeover remains a recurring event on a mature fleet,\n# │       and that its reported rate is not a monotone capability curve.\n# │ Show: Miles per disengagement in the first and latest report years; the\n# │       2020-to-2021 swing; the latest driverless-testing rate.\n# │ How:  Waymo&#x27;s annual rows of data/ca_dmv_disengagements.csv.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_multiple\nfrom binder.tools.figures import style as book_style\n\nclass DisengagementRecord:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants) ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _rows = [\n        r\n        for r in book_style.load_series(&quot;vol4/14_intervention/data/ca_dmv_disengagements.csv&quot;).entries\n        if r[&quot;company&quot;] == &quot;Waymo&quot; and r[&quot;filer_type&quot;] in (&quot;annual&quot;, &quot;first_report&quot;)\n    ]\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (The Compute) ────────────────────────────────────────\n    _drv = sorted((r for r in _rows if r[&quot;permit_type&quot;] == &quot;drivered_AVT&quot;), key=lambda r: int(r[&quot;report_year&quot;]))\n    _dls = sorted((r for r in _rows if r[&quot;permit_type&quot;] == &quot;driverless_AVDT&quot;), key=lambda r: int(r[&quot;report_year&quot;]))\n    first, latest, dl = _drv[0], _drv[-1], _dls[-1]\n    _r2020 = next(r for r in _drv if int(r[&quot;report_year&quot;]) == 2020)\n    _r2021 = next(r for r in _drv if int(r[&quot;report_year&quot;]) == 2021)\n    drop = float(_r2020[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;]) / float(_r2021[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;])\n    miles_growth = float(_r2021[&quot;autonomous_miles&quot;]) / float(_r2020[&quot;autonomous_miles&quot;])\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(drop &gt; 3 and miles_growth &gt; 3, &quot;Prose: the rate fell over threefold while miles grew over threefold&quot;)\n    check(dl[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;] &gt; latest[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;], &quot;Prose: driverless testing logs fewer disengagements per mile&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    first_str = fmt_int(round(first[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;], -2))\n    latest_str = fmt_int(round(latest[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;], -2))\n    latest_n_str = fmt_int(latest[&quot;disengagements&quot;])\n    drop_mult_str = fmt_multiple(round(drop, 1), precision=1)\n    growth_mult_str = fmt_multiple(round(miles_growth, 1), precision=1)\n    dl_str = fmt_int(round(dl[&quot;miles_per_disengagement&quot;], -2))\n    dl_n_str = fmt_int(dl[&quot;disengagements&quot;])\n]```\n\n:::: {#fig-14-disengagement-record fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**A decade of reported takeovers**: Waymo&#x27;s annual filings to the California DMV. (a) Autonomous miles driven on public roads under the safety-driver testing permit and the driverless testing permit. (b) Miles per reported disengagement under each permit, on a logarithmic axis. Each company defines which disengagements are reportable, and the regulator notes that the reports are not designed for comparison across companies; commercial deployment miles are excluded. Early years are transcribed from the filed PDFs. Data from @cadmv2026disengagement.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two panels with report years 2015 to 2025. Panel a is a stacked bar chart of autonomous miles in millions, safety-driver testing in blue and driverless testing in green, rising from under half a million miles to over 7 million in 2025. Panel b plots miles per disengagement on a logarithmic axis: a blue line for safety-driver testing rises from about 1,000 in 2015 to about 30,000 in 2020, falls to about 8,000 in 2021, and ends near 19,000 in 2025; a green line with square markers for driverless testing runs from 2023 to 2025, the last point near 72,000.&quot;}\n\n\n\n::::\n\nOver the decade the safety-driver rate rose from about `[DisengagementRecord.first_str]` to about `[DisengagementRecord.latest_str]` miles per disengagement, yet the fleet still reported `[DisengagementRecord.latest_n_str]` takeovers in its latest year, and the series is far from monotone. Between 2020 and 2021 the rate fell `[DisengagementRecord.drop_mult_str]` while the miles driven grew `[DisengagementRecord.growth_mult_str]`. Driverless testing reports one disengagement every `[DisengagementRecord.dl_str]` miles, `[DisengagementRecord.dl_n_str]` in its latest year. Two consequences follow for the machine. Human authority is not a phase a capable system grows out of; even the most experienced fleet exercises it every year, so the transfer mechanism is a permanent part of the design. The reported rate also depends on where the fleet drove and on what its operator chose to count, which is why an intervention count tells a reviewer how often the exchange happened but not, by itself, how well either party performed it.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The two permit series as two stages of permission to operate, and how a machine leaves one stage for the next, are developed in @sec-responsibility-staged-permission.\n:::\n\nThe central engineering problem of supervisory intervention is how authority can change hands on a moving machine without exhausting stopping clearance, letting two controllers drive the same actuator at once, or losing track of who commanded each instant. The fourth law, permission must be earned (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-evidence-bounds-authority}), frames the answer, and here Part IV applies it for the first time to a person. Like an autonomous policy, a human operator receives control authority only while valid evidence supports that grant, and loses it the moment that evidence lapses. Until the platform&#x27;s permission path checks it, every command from an operator is an untrusted request that cannot cross the causal boundary. A safe transfer must therefore bind each authenticated role to explicit actuator channels and timing bounds, commit ownership atomically on a control tick, blend reference changes within drivetrain rate limits, and log who held authority at every cycle for incident review and policy retraining.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ OPENING SCENE: A TWO-SECOND PAUSE AT AISLE SPEED</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-shared-authority, first paragraph.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that an out-of-loop pause outruns the rack-end clear distance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Aisle speed, design out-of-loop takeover delay, travel, clear distance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed: the design takeover</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          is the 90th percentile condition-mean take-over time plus transport</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (AISLE.t_takeover_out_of_loop); takeover_travel = v * T_takeover.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_velocity, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionSharedAuthorityScene: \"\"\"Travel during an out-of-loop takeover pause at the aisle speed.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>pause = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # out-of-loop takeover at the aisle speed v = pause.speed  # category: chosen (AISLE.v_aisle) t_pause = pause.takeover  # category: derived (sourced p90 take-over time + transport) d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative permission_rate = RM.permission_rate  # category: chosen tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen (from RM.permission_rate)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>travel = pause.result.takeover_travel.to(meter) travel_ratio = (travel / d_clear).to(\"\").magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(travel.magnitude - 5.8045) &lt; 5e-4, f\"travel during the pause: {travel}\") check(travel &gt; 5 <em> d_clear, \"The pause must carry the machine more than five times the clear distance.\") check(abs((tick </em> permission_rate).to(\"\").magnitude - 1.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"The permission tick is one period of the permission loop.\") check(abs(tick.to(millisecond).magnitude - 1.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Canon: 1 kHz permission loop, 1 ms tick.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) t_pause_str = fmt_latency(t_pause, unit=second, precision=1) travel_str = fmt_length(travel, unit=meter, precision=1) d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2) chunk_lease_str = fmt_latency(AISLE.t_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0) permission_rate_str = fmt_frequency(permission_rate, precision=0) tick_str = fmt_latency(tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>For the warehouse mobile manipulator, this timing constraint is direct and unforgiving. When the blocked aisle prompts the machine to request a remote assistant, at its chosen <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.v_str]</code> aisle speed a <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.t_pause_str]</code> pause before braking, the design out-of-loop takeover time that @sec-intervention-reaction-drift derives from driver take-over studies, carries the machine <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.travel_str]</code>, more than five times its <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.d_clear_str]</code> rack-end clear distance. An operator's intervention request reaches a chassis that is already in motion under an admitted command; human intent cannot erase physical momentum or make an actuator respond in zero time. The permission path resolves this transition in two stages. The arbiter that selects among the fallback ladder's rungs also decides, for each channel, which source holds the right to propose: the learned policy, the operator, or, once that authority lapses, a fallback rung. The enforcer then admits or rejects each setpoint from the selected source against physical envelope invariants. Both stages run deterministically on a dedicated safety microcontroller (MCU). Source selection precedes the enforcer's check, and rung selection follows it whenever the check fails, so human intervention adds a new input to the arbiter, a person, rather than a new component. Throughout this process, the plant must keep enough clearance to bring itself to a safe halt. Switching setpoints in a single step demands a sudden torque jump that strains motor drives, gearboxes, and payload stability. Delaying the transfer, in turn, uses up the remaining stopping margin even when both the learned policy and the human supervisor produce sensible trajectories on their own.</p>\n<p>↪ Whether a human role that needs this much time can count as a safety measure at all, and who is blamed when it cannot, is developed in @sec-responsibility-meaningful-oversight.</p>\n<p>Because momentum and kinetic energy sit on the physical side of the causal boundary, the machine keeps moving under the last admitted setpoint while the remote operator pieces together what is happening in the aisle. That mental reconstruction is slow. It is the paradox Lisanne Bainbridge called the <em>ironies of automation</em> [@bainbridge1983ironies]: engineers automate routine work to remove human error, yet leave human operators to handle the hardest, unmodeled edge cases that the automation cannot solve, where plant dynamics are abnormal and time is shortest. An operator who only monitors a screen also loses both manual feel and a current mental model of how the plant is moving. When an unexpected hazard forces a sudden takeover, that operator must act under tight time pressure with little context. Human vigilance fades quickly when watching for rare faults, and automation complacency makes the problem worse whenever secondary tasks compete for the supervisor's attention [@parasuraman2010complacency]. Decades of human factors research confirm that passive monitoring erodes situation awareness and adds long decision delays when automation fails without warning [@endsley1995outofloop]. The machine must therefore protect its own collision-free stopping envelope throughout the takeover window. Faster networks can trim milliseconds from packet transport, but they cannot shorten the time a person needs to see a hazard, decide what to do, and move a hand control.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Situation Awareness Levels</strong>: @endsley1995toward set out situation awareness as three levels: perceiving the elements of a scene, comprehending what they mean together, and projecting their state forward. The levels fail separately. A remote assistant can see the pallet without projecting that the base will not stop short of it, which is why a console that reports where the operator is looking cannot shorten the response budget (@sec-intervention-failure-modes).</p>\n<p>↩ The causal boundary, across which admitted motion cannot be recalled, is established in @sec-boundary-causal-boundary.</p>\n<p>Cutting power to a moving base lets it coast, whereas cutting power to a loaded manipulator arm without a holding brake drops the payload. Each stage of an intervention therefore needs a fallback matched to the machine's current state: a moving base may need controlled regenerative braking followed by a mechanical brake once stopped, while an arm carrying a tote needs an active, gravity-compensated position hold. Each response depends on the specific sensors and power rails qualified to carry it out. Because local damping, torque inhibition, and full power isolation produce different physical trajectories, the system cannot treat them as interchangeable steps on a generic escalation ladder; a protective input must invoke the validated fallback built for the plant's current motion state.</p>\n<p>↩ The fallback ladder, from which the arbiter selects a state-matched protective response on every tick, is established in @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder.</p>\n<p>Autonomous and manual proposal rights operate under separate, explicit leases. The learned policy proposes trajectories under the action-chunk lease established in @sec-nervous-lease-sizing, while a teleoperation session receives a distinct, clearance-budgeted manual lease. Rejecting an invalid replacement command from an operator does not invalidate an active, previously admitted setpoint whose evidence, state bounds, and lease duration remain healthy. If either controller exhausts its lease duration or violates its underlying safety premises, the permission path immediately triggers the appropriate local fallback.</p>\n<p>The permission path prevents split-brain command contention by pairing an upstream source arbiter with <strong>exclusive actuator-output ownership</strong>. In the <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.permission_rate_str]</code> control architecture analyzed here, the right to propose on a set of channels is a single object, the <strong>proposal token</strong>, and the arbiter grants it to one source at a time. An authority commit revokes the learned policy's proposal token and latches its last approved setpoint; the admission enforcer remains the sole, non-bypassable writer to the physical actuators before, during, and after the transition. Standard bus protocols such as Controller Area Network (CAN) arbitration serialize message frames from multiple transmitters, but bus-level arbitration does not establish control-layer authority. If two nodes broadcast conflicting yet syntactically valid frames to the same motor drive, the actuator chatters between setpoints and loses stability. Gating the output path in firmware and selecting a single source per tick prevents that contention. Across continuous state spaces, the enforcer executes a bumpless transfer, a command handoff without reference step discontinuities (@sec-intervention-bumpless-transfer), interpolating between validated endpoints while enforcing command-rate limits and honoring measured plant limits.</p>\n<p>Intervention events also directly shape the machine learning lifecycle. If a flight recorder or logging pipeline captures a rescued episode without marking the exact handover instant, imitation algorithms treat the near-failure states that triggered the rescue as if they were intentional expert demonstrations. As @sec-data-intervention-recovery establishes, the states visited by the learner right before a takeover supply training data for recovery policies, but autonomous commands from a failing interval require review rather than automatic admission as expert targets. Downstream learning algorithms, from Dataset Aggregation (DAgger) [@ross2011reduction] to modern human-gated policy correction methods (@sec-intervention-recovery-eligibility), depend on knowing which controller held authority over each logged timestep. To resolve this attribution, the architecture keeps an authority record (the twelfth record in the platform's record chain, specified in @sec-intervention-authority-log), which retains the captured evidence preceding and following the takeover, with explicit gaps and timing bounds. Its per-tick entries timestamp every transition against the safety microcontroller's monotonic clock. An authority commit ends policy ownership, ensuring that autonomous exploration, the enforcer's transition blend, and verified human recovery remain distinct, auditable intervals.</p>\n<p>Every shared-authority design begins by defining which named intervention may act upon which physical channel and establishing the maximum latency the machine can tolerate while awaiting human action. The protocol handshakes, setpoint blending algorithms, and authority records that follow all rest upon this initial grant of authority.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">Human Authority, Reaction Time, and Clearance</h2>\n<p>An assistant guiding the arm can move the chassis if its credential also permits base-drive commands. A command admitted under that permission performs mechanical work on the plant before software can revoke it. Authority must therefore identify the physical channels a person may affect, rather than grant unrestricted access through a generic operator role.</p>\n<p>The remote assistant who clears a blocked aisle and the coworker who takes an item from the gripper act on the same machine, and neither should hold the other's authority. Each grant therefore names an operation together with its timing budget, physical degrees of freedom, and preemption rule. The permission path keeps these grants in an explicit <strong>capability matrix</strong>, a permissions table mapping individual operations (such as an emergency power cutoff, a velocity trim adjustment, a trajectory path veto, or manual drive of the base and arm) to authenticated roles. The design reflects classic taxonomies of human supervisory control [@sheridan1992telerobotics] and multi-level automation [@parasuraman2000model]. Each entry in this matrix defines the exact actuator channels the operation governs, the preemption tier it carries over the learned policy, and the maximum propagation latency permitted from physical input to actuator response.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Supervisory Control Levels</strong>: @sheridan1978human formulated a ten-level taxonomy of supervisory control, running from a human who performs the whole task without computer assistance to a computer that decides and acts while ignoring the human.</p>\n<p>Sheridan also describes the supervisor's work as five steps in sequence: plan the task off-line, teach the plan to the computer, monitor the automatic action, intervene when it fails or the goal changes, and learn from the episode [@sheridan2021supervisory]. Intervening in flight and learning from the episode require finer granularity than a single autonomy level assigned to the whole task. The mobile manipulator needs an authority assignment per channel and per instant: during a takeover, the arm can sit near the manual end of the scale while the base still runs at the automatic end, and the enforcer holds the final admission gate on every channel throughout because it checks the operator's commands just as it checks the policy's. Levels that differ across channels are what a supervisor can lose track of, the mode confusion of @sec-intervention-failure-modes, so a channel left out of a transfer keeps its autonomous owner only while the console announces that it does, and otherwise holds. Each entry in the capability matrix therefore names the exact operation and channel it governs.</p>\n<p>Naming the operations separates intervention from <strong>safety enforcement</strong>, two mechanisms that system architectures routinely conflate. Enforcement withholds permission from a candidate action proposed by a learned model, modifying, clamping, or projecting the setpoint while leaving the machine under autonomous control. Intervention transfers authority away from the model to an external entity, terminating autonomous operation and giving the supervisor the right to propose, while every command still passes the permission path. An <strong>enforcement filter</strong> sits in the fast inner control loop (<code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.permission_rate_str]</code> / $\\le$ <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.tick_str]</code>) to prevent the policy from demanding an over-current maneuver or violating a barrier certificate [@ames2019control] during nominal tracking. An intervention mechanism strips the policy of its proposal token when an operational boundary is breached or an operator demands control. A system equipped with enforcement but lacking intervention cannot be redirected when human intent changes or external conditions fall outside the policy design envelope, leading to conservative deadlock when barrier constraints collapse the feasible control set. Conversely, a system equipped with intervention but lacking enforcement permits a lagging operator to drive the plant into structural failure during nominal manual operation. @Tbl-14-enforcement-vs-intervention sets the two mechanisms side by side, from trigger and authority holder to typical failure.</p>\n<p>↩ The actuator current and thermal limits an enforcement filter must respect are established in @sec-body-five-budgets.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Enforcement filter</th><th>Intervention state machine</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Primary trigger</td><td>Invariant boundary approach ($\\BarrierFunctionRate + \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction &lt; 0$) or torque limit</td><td>Human takeover assertion, monotonic lease timeout, or operational design domain (ODD) exit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Authority holder</td><td>Autonomous policy retains proposal rights; enforcer modifies setpoints</td><td>Authority transferred to external entity or fallback controller</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Execution loop</td><td>Fast inner control loop (<code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.permission_rate_str]</code> / $\\le$ <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.tick_str]</code>)</td><td>Same permission tick; protocol exchange spans multiple ticks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Safety lease role</td><td>Enforces continuous barrier satisfaction on admitted commands</td><td>Lease expiry invokes the state-dependent validated fallback</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical failure mode</td><td>Feasible set collapse; conservative deadlock or oscillation</td><td>Opposing commands cancel, mode confusion, or late human reaction</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Architectural boundaries of safety enforcement and supervisory intervention</strong>: Enforcement operates in-loop as an active constraint on proposed actions; intervention transitions authority between discrete proposers. {#tbl-14-enforcement-vs-intervention tbl-colwidths=\"[20,40,40]\"}</p>\n<p><em><strong>Supervisory intervention</strong></em> is the transfer of the right to propose commands on named actuator channels between a learned policy and an authenticated person, committed on a control tick and bounded by a lease, while every command continues to pass the same permission check.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The transfer is a delay on a moving machine, so it is priced in the stopping budget $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ rather than in the user interface, and the waiting before braking, not the braking itself, decides whether the machine stops in time.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Enforcement changes a proposal and leaves the proposer in place; intervention changes the proposer and leaves the check in place. A machine needs both, because neither can do the other's job.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that a takeover hands the operator the actuators. The operator gains only the right to propose, and the machine still needs a local stop that does not wait for the operator to act.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Capability matrices and the intervention-enforcement split</h3>\n<p>On the warehouse mobile manipulator, a coworker taking a mug from the gripper at the packing station would be at risk if their station credential also allowed them to command the arm's joints or drive the base. The capability matrix prevents that cross-channel hazard by letting the remote assistant redirect motion while denying any motion authority to the coworker accepting an item.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ COWORKER HANDOVER SPEED UNDER accept_item\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-intervention-human-authority, the capability example\n# │          (veto_path and manual_drive for the remote assistant, accept_item\n# │          for the coworker).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Recall the handover cap that the enforcement record&#x27;s handover\n# │          entry holds (@sec-enforcement-telemetry owns it) and the contact\n# │          ceilings derived in @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact\n# │          (@eq-release-unbraked-force); no derivation here.\n# │ Show:    Chosen TCP handover speed; the free-hand and clamped-hand ceilings.\n# │ How:     calc_transient_contact_speed_limit with the ISO/TS 15066 Annex A hand\n# │          body model (AISLE.k_contact_human, AISLE.m_contact_human) and the\n# │          machine&#x27;s effective mass, the same MLSysIM call the enforcement\n# │          chapter&#x27;s calc-enforcement-contact-force makes.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, kilogram, newton, millimeter\nfrom mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_transient_contact_speed_limit\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_velocity, fmt_force, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionCoworkerHandover:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Arm approach speed for the coworker&#x27;s accept_item handover.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    v_handover = AISLE.v_handover  # category: chosen\n    k_hand = AISLE.k_contact_human  # category: datasheet (ISO/TS 15066 hand body model)\n    m_hand = AISLE.m_contact_human  # category: datasheet (ISO/TS 15066 hand body model)\n    f_criterion = AISLE.f_contact_criterion  # category: chosen\n    m_eff = RM.arm_effective_contact_mass  # category: derived\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_ceiling = calc_transient_contact_speed_limit(f_criterion, m_eff, k_hand, human_mass=m_hand).to(meter / second)\n    v_ceiling_clamped = calc_transient_contact_speed_limit(f_criterion, m_eff, k_hand).to(meter / second)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(v_ceiling.magnitude - 0.2415) &lt; 5e-5, f&quot;free-hand handover speed ceiling: {v_ceiling}&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_ceiling_clamped.magnitude - 0.0527) &lt; 5e-5, f&quot;clamped-hand handover speed ceiling: {v_ceiling_clamped}&quot;)\n    check(abs(m_eff.to(kilogram).magnitude - 12.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;arm effective contact mass: {m_eff}&quot;)\n    check(v_handover &lt; v_ceiling, &quot;The chosen handover speed must sit under the free-hand ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(v_handover &gt; v_ceiling_clamped, &quot;The chosen handover speed must exceed the clamped ceiling, so clamping must be excluded.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_handover_str = fmt_velocity(v_handover, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    v_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(v_ceiling, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    v_ceiling_clamped_str = fmt_velocity(v_ceiling_clamped, unit=meter / second, precision=3)\n    force_str = fmt_force(f_criterion)\n]```\n\nIn that matrix, the remote assistant who supervises the mobile manipulator may hold a signed **trajectory veto** capability for the base&#x27;s planner channel: role `remote_assistant`, operation `veto_path`, channel `planner_proposal`, preemption tier above the learned planner but below the local enforcer, authenticated session and nonce, and a clearance-derived acknowledgment deadline. The enforcer responds to a valid veto with a checked local hold or stop. The same session carries a second entry, `manual_drive`, on the base-velocity and arm-joint channels, which becomes active only through a handover that commits within the clearance-derived deadline, and lapses with the manual lease of @sec-intervention-authority-transitions. Every command it carries still passes the permission check. A wheel drive-torque packet from that credential is denied, because neither entry grants the base&#x27;s wheel drive-torque channel.\n\nThe coworker at the packing station holds a different entry, `accept_item`, which lets the coworker take from the gripper the mug the arm picked from the takeaway conveyor. That entry grants no motion authority. The item-handover channel of the enforcement record caps the arm&#x27;s approach to the coworker&#x27;s hand at `[InterventionCoworkerHandover.v_handover_str]`, and no capability lifts that cap. The handover is a collaborative application in the sense of ISO 10218-2:2025 [@iso10218part2], and the body model of the hand that bounds it, with the contact force that model predicts, is set out once in @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact. ISO 10218-2 leaves out the hazards of mounting the arm on a mobile platform, which ANSI/A3 R15.08-2 addresses for the running machine [@ansi1508part2].\n\nThe ceiling behind that cap comes from the contact-force model (@eq-release-unbraked-force). For a free hand, which recoils, the selected `[InterventionCoworkerHandover.force_str]` criterion admits up to `[InterventionCoworkerHandover.v_ceiling_str]`, and the cap sits under that ceiling. A hand clamped against a rack upright or the arm itself cannot recoil, and the same criterion then admits only `[InterventionCoworkerHandover.v_ceiling_clamped_str]`, below the cap. The cap therefore holds only if the handover geometry excludes clamping, a premise the release case must evidence. The ceiling is conditional on these inputs and does not by itself establish a pressure or injury limit for a particular contact geometry; the site&#x27;s risk assessment qualifies the effective mass, the force criterion, and the contact geometry before the cap is qualified.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The release criterion that holds the handover&#x27;s contact force under its assessed limit is developed in @sec-release-question.\n:::\n\nEach capability entry binds actor, operation, channel, deadline, authentication, preemption tier, and fallback; a valid signature alone grants no motion authority. Physical clearance sets the tolerable deadline; the operator&#x27;s response time determines whether a human can meet it.\n\n### Human reaction time and travel before braking {#sec-intervention-reaction-drift}\n\nThe remote assistant must notice the blocked aisle, decide how to recover, move the controls, and deliver a checked request. Summed in sequence, the total supervisory takeover latency $\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ is:\n$$\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency = \\OperatorSensoryTime + \\OperatorCognitiveTime + \\OperatorMotorTime + \\ConsoleCommunicationTime$$ {#eq-intervention-reaction-floor}\nThe first three terms belong to the person and depend on engagement and expectation; the last belongs to the console and the link. Human response times are distributions with long right tails, so a stopping budget needs a value that nearly every request meets, not the typical one. The following calculation compares a continuously engaged operator with an out-of-loop assistant. Its communication term includes transport and the protocol exchange, so that exchange must not be charged again.\n\nWaiting consumes distance before the selected stop begins. The finished stopping budget from @sec-enforcement-composite-budget supplies the machine delay, resident stop, and fixed allowances. Adding the human waiting interval exposes whether remote rescue can fit at the current speed; it does not authorize waiting when the local stop is already required.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE DISPATCH-TO-ADMISSION ALLOCATION CHARGED AGAINST THE TAKEOVER BUDGETS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-reaction-drift (the Delta_da paragraph),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines (the in-loop redesign),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-intervention-session-admission, @sec-intervention-authenticating-intervention,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the verifier-flooding pitfall, and the summary.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Charge the allocated Delta_da of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission to the chapter's</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          partial budgets and show which verdicts change.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Delta_da and its aisle-speed ceiling; the waiting it leaves at the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          aisle speed; the retired 0.8 m/s slow-down's remainder before and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          after the charge (it missed by 5.0 ms); the Delta_da-charged in-loop</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          ceiling (0.797 m/s) and the derived slow-down under it (0.75 m/s)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          with its remainder; the out-of-loop ceiling with Delta_da added;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the travel Delta_da costs at the slow-down.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Delta_da is AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission (MLSysIM), the same value</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          calc-placement-transport-budget imports (@sec-placement-dispatch-admission,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          nbk-placement-transport-budget). The slow-down is</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          AISLE.v_takeover_in_loop: the charged ceiling rounded down to</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          AISLE.v_takeover_resolution. Deadlines, remainders, and ceilings</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          from the Scenarios.Warehouse*Takeover* budgets (TakeoverBudgetModel:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the finished stop with T_Sigma, and Delta_da, before braking).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionAdmissionAllocation: \"\"\"The allocated dispatch-to-admission interval charged to the takeover budgets.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # in-loop takeover at the aisle speed at_request = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRequestSpeed  # the same after the derived slow-down at_retired = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRetiredSlowdown  # the same at the retired 0.8 m/s slow-down ool_at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed v = at_aisle.speed  # category: chosen v_in = at_request.speed  # category: derived (charged ceiling rounded down to the resolution) v_step = AISLE.v_takeover_resolution  # category: chosen v_in_old = at_retired.speed  # category: chosen (the retired slow-down, shown to explain the change) v_ool = AISLE.v_takeover_out_of_loop  # category: chosen</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">The @sec-placement-dispatch-admission allocation (calc-placement-transport-budget), from MLSysIM</h1>\n<p>delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation, not a measurement)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_ceiling = at_aisle.result.deadline.to(millisecond)  # the most Delta_da the aisle-speed spare can pay for left_aisle = at_aisle.result.deadline_after_admission.to(millisecond) left_old = at_retired.result.time_left.to(millisecond) short_old = (-at_retired.result.time_left_after_admission).to(millisecond) left_in = at_request.result.time_left.to(millisecond) left_in_da = at_request.result.time_left_after_admission.to(millisecond) travel_da_in = at_request.result.admission_travel.to(millimeter) ceil_in_da = at_aisle.result.with_takeover_and_dispatch.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) ceil_ool_da = ool_at_aisle.result.with_takeover_and_dispatch.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(delta_da.to(millisecond).magnitude - 63.31072) &lt; 1e-6, f\"allocation must match @sec-placement: {delta_da}\") check(abs(t_ceiling.magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, f\"aisle-speed ceiling on Delta_da: {t_ceiling}\") check(abs(left_aisle.magnitude - 37.464) &lt; 5e-3, f\"waiting left at the aisle speed: {left_aisle}\") check(abs(left_old.magnitude - 58.275) &lt; 5e-3, f\"0.8 m/s in-loop remainder before the charge: {left_old}\") check(abs(short_old.magnitude - 5.036) &lt; 5e-3, f\"the retired 0.8 m/s misses by 5.0 ms once charged: {short_old}\") check(abs(ceil_in_da.magnitude - AISLE.v_takeover_in_loop_ceiling.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Charged ceiling matches the registry.\") check(abs(ceil_in_da.magnitude - 0.797) &lt; 5e-4, f\"Delta_da-charged in-loop ceiling: {ceil_in_da}\") check(v_in &lt; ceil_in_da &lt; v_in + v_step, \"The slow-down is the charged ceiling rounded down to the resolution.\") check(left_in_da.magnitude &gt; 0, \"Charged with Delta_da, the derived in-loop slow-down now meets its deadline.\") check(v_ool &lt; ceil_ool_da, \"The crawl must survive the Delta_da charge.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_ceiling_str = fmt_latency(t_ceiling, unit=millisecond, precision=1) left_aisle_str = fmt_latency(left_aisle, unit=millisecond, precision=1) left_old_str = fmt_latency(left_old, unit=millisecond, precision=1) short_old_str = fmt_latency(short_old, unit=millisecond, precision=1) left_in_str = fmt_latency(left_in, unit=millisecond, precision=1) left_in_da_str = fmt_latency(left_in_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1) travel_da_in_str = fmt_length(travel_da_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1) ceil_in_da_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_in_da, unit=meter / second, precision=3) ceil_ool_da_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_ool_da, unit=meter / second, precision=3) v_in_old_str = fmt_velocity(v_in_old, unit=meter / second, precision=1) v_step_str = fmt_velocity(v_step, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_in_str = fmt_velocity(v_in, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_ool_str = fmt_velocity(v_ool, unit=meter / second, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>The budgets that follow are illustrative design comparisons, not measured takeover performance. Each of them must also pay for the dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, which @sec-placement-dispatch-admission allocated but has not yet measured. A takeover does not remove that interval. The policy must still infer on the frame that shows the blocked aisle before it can request help, and its request, like any proposal, reaches the arbiter only through the safety microcontroller, so the base keeps rolling through $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ before the human response even begins. This chapter calls a budget without the interval the <em>partial budget</em> and a budget with it the <em>complete budget</em>. The stops and overruns that follow use the complete budget. The speed ceilings and deadlines give the partial value first and then the charged one, because the gap between the two is itself a design lesson.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ OPERATOR TAKEOVER AGAINST THE MACHINE&#x27;S STOPPING BUDGET\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-intervention-human-authority, the two takeover modes that\n# │          follow @eq-intervention-reaction-floor, and the notebook\n# │          &quot;Takeover budget and clearance margin&quot;.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Price an operator&#x27;s sourced takeover delay on the warehouse mobile\n# │          manipulator at its aisle speed.\n# │ Show:    Spare margin at 1.3 m/s, before and after the dispatch-to-admission\n# │          travel; the in-loop decomposition (Model Human\n# │          Processor at the slow end of each cycle, video glass-to-glass\n# │          latency, four-phase exchange); the out-of-loop design value\n# │          (90th-percentile condition-mean take-over time plus the same\n# │          transport); travel during each; the stop with each delay added;\n# │          the speed ceilings.\n# │ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed and\n# │          WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed (TakeoverBudgetModel): the\n# │          finished stopping budget from the WarehouseAisle scenario\n# │          (jerk-limited resident stop, all overheads; the jerk bound is the\n# │          peak jerk of the reference C2 polynomial at the drive limit,\n# │          @sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix); T_Sigma adds to tau_delay;\n# │          the stops and overruns also charge Delta_da (AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission),\n# │          the complete budget of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget; ceil_in\n# │          and ceil_ool are the partial-budget ceilings.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond\nfrom mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_scurve_stop_distance\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import (\n    fmt_velocity,\n    fmt_length,\n    fmt_latency,\n    fmt_magnitude,\n    fmt_multiple,\n    fmt_display_math,\n    fmt_acceleration,\n    fmt_jerk,\n    check,\n)\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionTakeoverBudget:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Operator takeover delay spent against the finished stopping budget.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    in_loop = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # in-loop takeover at the aisle speed\n    out_of_loop = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # out-of-loop takeover at the aisle speed\n    v = in_loop.speed  # category: chosen\n    d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative\n    tau_delay = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived\n    a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative\n    j_brake = AISLE.j_brake  # category: derived (stipulated jerk bound: C2 polynomial peak jerk at the drive limit)\n    overhead = AISLE.fixed_overhead  # category: illustrative / chosen\n    t_sensory = AISLE.t_human_perceptual_slow  # category: sourced (Card, Moran, Newell 1983)\n    t_cognitive = AISLE.t_human_cognitive_slow  # category: sourced\n    t_motor = AISLE.t_human_motor_slow  # category: sourced\n    t_video = AISLE.t_video_glass_to_glass  # category: sourced (wired best case)\n    t_handshake = AISLE.t_handshake  # category: chosen (four-phase budgets)\n    t_comm = AISLE.t_takeover_transport  # category: derived (video + four-phase exchange)\n    t_expected = AISLE.t_brake_response_expected  # category: sourced (Green 2000)\n    t_surprise = AISLE.t_brake_response_surprise  # category: sourced (Green 2000)\n    t_tot_mean = AISLE.t_takeover_driving_mean  # category: sourced (Zhang et al. 2019)\n    t_tot_p90 = AISLE.t_takeover_driving_p90  # category: sourced (computed from its data)\n    t_in = in_loop.takeover  # category: derived\n    t_ool = out_of_loop.takeover  # category: derived\n    delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission)\n    # UN Regulation No. 157 (ALKS), para. 5.4.4.1: a minimum risk manoeuvre\n    # starts at the earliest 10 s after the transition demand.\n    t_r157 = 10 * second  # category: sourced\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    ib, ob = in_loop.result, out_of_loop.result\n    reaction = ib.machine.delay_travel.to(millimeter)\n    r157_over_ool = (t_r157 / t_ool).to(&quot;&quot;).magnitude\n    travel_r157 = ib.machine.travel_over(t_r157).to(meter)\n    stop = ib.machine.scurve_travel.to(millimeter)\n    budget = ib.machine.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    spare = ib.machine.spare.to(millimeter)  # partial: before dispatch-to-admission travel\n    travel_da = ib.admission_travel.to(millimeter)\n    budget_da = ib.machine_with_dispatch.required_distance.to(millimeter)  # complete budget, the one a design must meet\n    spare_da = ib.machine_with_dispatch.spare.to(millimeter)\n    t_sum = (t_sensory + t_cognitive + t_motor + t_comm).to(millisecond)\n    travel_in = ib.takeover_travel.to(millimeter)\n    travel_ool = ob.takeover_travel.to(millimeter)\n    travel_prebrake_ool = ob.with_takeover_and_dispatch.delay_travel.to(millimeter)  # all coasting before the brake command\n    d_stop_in = ib.with_takeover_and_dispatch.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    d_stop_ool = ob.with_takeover_and_dispatch.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    stop_ratio = (d_stop_ool / d_stop_in).to(&quot;&quot;).magnitude\n    # Napkin-math box &quot;The takeover tax&quot;: the overruns and the waiting-to-spare ratio.\n    overrun_in = (-ib.with_takeover_and_dispatch.spare).to(millimeter)\n    overrun_ool = (-ob.with_takeover_and_dispatch.spare).to(millimeter)\n    travel_over_spare = ib.takeover_over_spare_after_admission\n    ceil_in = ib.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    ceil_ool = ob.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(spare.magnitude - 131.01) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;spare margin at the aisle speed: {spare}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_sum.magnitude - t_in.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The four stages must sum to the in-loop takeover.&quot;)\n    check(abs((t_video + t_handshake - t_comm).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Transport is video plus the four-phase exchange.&quot;)\n    check(abs((t_tot_p90 + t_comm - t_ool).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The out-of-loop takeover is the p90 time plus transport.&quot;)\n    check(travel_in &gt; 5 * spare, &quot;The in-loop takeover must overrun the spare margin several times at the aisle speed.&quot;)\n    check(travel_ool &gt; 5 * d_clear, &quot;The out-of-loop travel must exceed five times the clear distance.&quot;)\n    check(abs(spare_da.magnitude - 48.70) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;spare after dispatch-to-admission travel: {spare_da}&quot;)\n    check(abs(d_stop_in.magnitude - 1837.80) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;stop with in-loop takeover: {d_stop_in}&quot;)\n    check(abs(d_stop_ool.magnitude - 6855.80) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;stop with out-of-loop takeover: {d_stop_ool}&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceil_in.magnitude - 0.8362) &lt; 5e-5, f&quot;in-loop takeover ceiling: {ceil_in}&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceil_ool.magnitude - 0.1911) &lt; 5e-5, f&quot;out-of-loop takeover ceiling: {ceil_ool}&quot;)\n    check(abs(stop_ratio - 3.73) &lt; 0.01, f&quot;out-of-loop to in-loop stop ratio: {stop_ratio}&quot;)\n    check(abs(r157_over_ool - 2.24) &lt; 0.01, f&quot;R157 transition over the design takeover: {r157_over_ool}&quot;)\n    check(abs(travel_r157.magnitude - 13.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;travel at aisle speed during the R157 transition: {travel_r157}&quot;)\n    check(abs(budget.magnitude - 968.99) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;machine-alone stop at the aisle speed: {budget}&quot;)\n    check(abs(overrun_in.magnitude - 737.80) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;in-loop overrun: {overrun_in}&quot;)\n    check(abs(overrun_ool.magnitude - 5755.80) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;out-of-loop overrun: {overrun_ool}&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((travel_prebrake_ool - (d_stop_ool - stop - overhead)).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6,\n        &quot;Pre-brake travel is the out-of-loop stop less the resident stop and allowances.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(abs((stop - calc_scurve_stop_distance(v, a_brake, j_brake)).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Resident stop matches MLSysIM.&quot;)\n    check(abs(travel_over_spare - 16.15) &lt; 0.01, f&quot;in-loop travel over the complete spare: {travel_over_spare}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    spare_da_str = fmt_length(spare_da, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    travel_da_str = fmt_length(travel_da, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    t_in_str = fmt_latency(t_in, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_ool_str = fmt_latency(t_ool, unit=second, precision=1)\n    t_sensory_str = fmt_latency(t_sensory, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_cognitive_str = fmt_latency(t_cognitive, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_motor_str = fmt_latency(t_motor, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_video_str = fmt_latency(t_video, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_handshake_str = fmt_latency(t_handshake, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_comm_str = fmt_latency(t_comm, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_expected_str = fmt_latency(t_expected, unit=second, precision=2)\n    t_surprise_str = fmt_latency(t_surprise, unit=second, precision=1)\n    t_tot_mean_str = fmt_latency(t_tot_mean, unit=second, precision=2)\n    t_tot_p90_str = fmt_latency(t_tot_p90, unit=second, precision=2)\n    travel_in_str = fmt_length(travel_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    travel_ool_str = fmt_length(travel_ool, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    travel_prebrake_ool_str = fmt_length(travel_prebrake_ool, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    d_stop_in_str = fmt_length(d_stop_in, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    d_stop_ool_str = fmt_length(d_stop_ool, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    stop_mult_str = fmt_multiple(stop_ratio, precision=1)\n    ceil_in_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_in, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    ceil_ool_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_ool, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    t_r157_str = fmt_latency(t_r157, unit=second, precision=0)\n    r157_mult_str = fmt_multiple(r157_over_ool, precision=1)\n    travel_r157_str = fmt_length(travel_r157, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    tau_delay_str = fmt_latency(tau_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    a_brake_str = fmt_acceleration(a_brake, unit=meter / second**2, precision=0)\n    j_brake_str = fmt_jerk(j_brake, unit=meter / second**3, precision=2)\n    overhead_str = fmt_length(overhead, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    budget_str = fmt_length(budget, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    overrun_in_str = fmt_length(overrun_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    overrun_ool_str = fmt_length(overrun_ool, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    travel_over_spare_mult_str = fmt_multiple(travel_over_spare, precision=1)\n\n    reaction_value_str = fmt_magnitude(reaction, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    travel_da_value_str = fmt_magnitude(travel_da, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    stop_value_str = fmt_magnitude(stop, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    overhead_value_str = fmt_magnitude(overhead, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    travel_in_value_str = fmt_magnitude(travel_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    travel_ool_value_str = fmt_magnitude(travel_ool, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    d_stop_in_value_str = fmt_magnitude(d_stop_in, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    d_stop_ool_value_str = fmt_magnitude(d_stop_ool, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n\n    haptic_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;d_{{\\text{{stop}},1}} = v\\,\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} + v\\,\\DispatchAdmissionInterval + v\\,T_{{\\Sigma,1}} + d_{{\\text{{suffix}}}}(v) + \\delta = &quot;\n        rf&quot;{reaction_value_str} + {travel_da_value_str} + {travel_in_value_str} + {stop_value_str} + {overhead_value_str} = {d_stop_in_value_str}\\text{{ mm}}&quot;\n    )\n    ool_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;d_{{\\text{{stop}},2}} = v\\,\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} + v\\,\\DispatchAdmissionInterval + v\\,T_{{\\Sigma,2}} + d_{{\\text{{suffix}}}}(v) + \\delta = &quot;\n        rf&quot;{reaction_value_str} + {travel_da_value_str} + {travel_ool_value_str} + {stop_value_str} + {overhead_value_str} = {d_stop_ool_value_str}\\text{{ mm}}&quot;\n    )\n    machine_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;d_{{\\text{{stop}},0}} = v\\,\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} + v\\,\\DispatchAdmissionInterval + d_{{\\text{{suffix}}}}(v) + \\delta = &quot;\n        rf&quot;{reaction_value_str} + {travel_da_value_str} + {stop_value_str} + {overhead_value_str} = {fmt_magnitude(budget_da, unit=millimeter, precision=2)}\\text{{ mm}}&quot;\n    )\n]```\n\nThe stopping budget of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget shows what @eq-intervention-reaction-floor costs on the mobile manipulator. At the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` aisle speed it leaves `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_str]` of the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_clear_str]` rack-end clear distance before dispatch-to-admission travel, and `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` once the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_da_str]` of travel during $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ is charged. A takeover delay adds to the machine&#x27;s own pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$, and every millisecond of it becomes travel at the aisle speed:\n\n1. **Mode 1: Continuous in-loop teleoperation**: An attending operator with hands on the controls of a synchronized leader arm or a real-time console, responding to the stimulus the task has led them to expect, makes a simple reaction. The Model Human Processor [@card1983psychology] prices that reaction as one perceptual, one cognitive, and one motor cycle [@jastrzembski2007model]. At the slow end of each cycle&#x27;s range, $\\OperatorSensoryTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_sensory_str]`, $\\OperatorCognitiveTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_cognitive_str]`, and $\\OperatorMotorTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_motor_str]`. The console adds $\\ConsoleCommunicationTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_comm_str]`: the scene reaches the operator after a video glass-to-glass latency of `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_video_str]`, measured for a wired teleoperation link [@schimpe2022opensource], and the request returns through a `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_handshake_str]` protocol exchange, the four-phase handshake that @sec-intervention-handshake-protocol builds and budgets phase by phase. By @eq-intervention-reaction-floor, $\\TakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]`. The machine travels `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_in_str]` during that delay, several times the spare margin. Solving the complete budget for speed, with $\\TakeoverResponseTime$ and $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ added to $\\PrebrakeDelay$, bounds the machine to `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.ceil_in_da_str]`. The value holds only for the expected stimulus. A driver who is waiting for a brake signal needs up to `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_expected_str]` to detect it and reach the pedal, and a surprise raises that to about `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_surprise_str]` [@green2000stop]. @Sec-intervention-speed-ceilings prices those cases.\n2. **Mode 2: Passive out-of-the-loop supervisory takeover**: An assistant who monitors the telemetry of several machines passively does not make a simple reaction, and its stages cannot be priced separately. Driver take-over studies measure the whole interval, from the take-over request to the first control input. Across 520 experimental conditions in 129 studies, the mean take-over time averaged `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_tot_mean_str]`, and the distribution of those condition means is skewed to the right [@zhang2019takeover]. For this illustrative comparison, the selected design value is the 90th percentile of the condition means, `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_tot_p90_str]`, computed from the study&#x27;s published data. With the same `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_comm_str]` console term, the takeover delay is $\\DisengagedTakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_ool_str]`, during which the machine covers `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_ool_str]`, more than five times the clear distance, before any brake command. The same solution bounds the machine to `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.ceil_ool_da_str]`.\n\n: **Model Human Processor**: @card1983psychology modeled a person responding to a stimulus as three processors in series, perceptual, cognitive, and motor, and gave each cycle time a typical value and a range rather than a single number. The range is the design input. A budget built on the typical cycles admits a speed that a slower but still normal operator overruns, so this chapter&#x27;s design row takes the slow end of every range.\n\n: **Glass-to-Glass Latency**: The interval from light entering the remote camera&#x27;s lens to the matching image leaving the operator&#x27;s display, covering exposure, encoding, transport, decoding, and display refresh. It is the only video delay the operator experiences, and every stage of that path contributes to it, so a value measured on a wired link does not carry over to a warehouse wireless link. The site must measure its own link before the value enters the takeover budget.\n\nIn Mode 2 at the aisle speed, the machine would reach a static obstacle at the rack end at full speed, because contact occurs before braking ever begins. Stronger braking can raise a finite stopping-speed ceiling, but cannot recover distance already spent waiting. As braking distance tends toward zero, reaction travel still sets a limiting ceiling. A percentile of condition means also understates the tail an individual operator can produce, because each condition&#x27;s own spread grows with its mean; the site must measure its operators, console, and task load before any of these values admits a speed. Road regulation sizes the same interval generously for a vehicle that can keep driving while it waits. For an unanswered transition demand in the cited 2021 version of UN Regulation No. 157, an automated lane-keeping system begins its minimum risk manoeuvre no earlier than `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_r157_str]` after it issues the demand [@unece2021r157], `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.r157_mult_str]` the design takeover here. Severe system or vehicle failures permit an immediate minimum risk manoeuvre, and an obstacle can require an emergency response during the transition phase. The vehicle can afford that wait because it stays in its lane under automated control throughout. At the aisle speed the same wait would carry the mobile manipulator `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_r157_str]`, so the machine cannot borrow the road&#x27;s interval, only its structure of a demand, a bounded wait, and an automatic fallback. @Sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines turns the two design values into the speeds at which the machine may request each kind of takeover.\n\nEven the much shorter in-loop interval fails at aisle speed; drawn to scale, the Mode 1 budget shows where the clearance goes (@fig-14-in-loop-takeover-strip). The `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` spare of the complete budget corresponds to `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle_str]` at the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` aisle speed, so the machine has spent it long before the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_video_str]` video stage ends and before the operator has perceived anything. The perceptual, cognitive, and motor cycles and the handshake that follow are all travel the budget cannot absorb, `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_in_str]` in total.\n\n::: {#fig-14-in-loop-takeover-strip fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Where an in-loop takeover spends its time**: The `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]` in-loop takeover drawn to scale from the request, stage by stage: video transport to the console, the operator&#x27;s perceptual, cognitive, and motor cycles at the slow end of the Model Human Processor, and the four-phase handshake through commit notification. Local commit starts the enforcer-owned blend before notification; the full exchange remains the conservative waiting allocation. The distance row converts the same axis into travel at the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` aisle speed; the bracket at its left marks the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` spare of the complete budget, which the machine exhausts within the video stage.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Timing strip with three lanes over 0 to about 630 milliseconds. The console lane holds a video interval from 0 to 104 milliseconds. The operator lane holds perceive 200, decide 170, and act 100 milliseconds end to end, ending at 574. The permission lane holds a 31 millisecond handshake ending at commit notification at 605 milliseconds. Local commit precedes notification. A bracket above spans the whole 605 millisecond takeover. A distance row below, labeled travel at 1.30 meters per second, carries a bracket of 786 millimeters across the whole interval and a short bracket of 49 millimeters of spare ending near 37 milliseconds.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\n::: {#nbk-intervention-operator-takeover-budget-spatial-clearance-margin .callout-notebook title=&quot;The takeover tax&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: At the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` aisle speed, does the finished stopping budget still fit the rack-end clear distance once an in-loop or an out-of-loop takeover delay is inserted before braking, and by how much does each overrun it?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* $\\SpeedAtDetection =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` (Chosen) and $\\ClearDistance =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_clear_str]` (Illustrative).\n* $\\PrebrakeDelay =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.tau_delay_str]`, the machine&#x27;s own pre-brake delay (Derived; @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget), which excludes the dispatch-to-admission interval, and $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.delta_da_str]`, the allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission (Illustrative), which a takeover still spends before braking.\n* $\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) = \\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration) + \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/(2\\JerkLimit)$, the jerk-limited resident stop of @eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix, with $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.a_brake_str]` (Illustrative) and $\\JerkLimit =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.j_brake_str]` (Derived).\n* $\\FixedClearanceAllowance = \\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin + \\TrackingErrorBound =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.overhead_str]` (Illustrative and Chosen).\n* $\\TakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]` in-loop and $\\DisengagedTakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_ool_str]` out-of-loop (Derived, from the measured human and transport terms of Modes 1 and 2 plus the chosen handshake).\n\n**Math**: Every term is in millimeters. The machine alone, charged with the dispatch-to-admission travel, leaves a `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` spare:\n\n`[InterventionTakeoverBudget.machine_display_math]`\n\nEach takeover adds $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ before the same stop:\n\n`[InterventionTakeoverBudget.haptic_display_math]`\n\n`[InterventionTakeoverBudget.ool_display_math]`\n\n**Result**: The in-loop takeover overruns the clear distance by `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.overrun_in_str]` and the out-of-loop takeover by `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.overrun_ool_str]`; the second stop is `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.stop_mult_str]` the first.\n\n**Systems insight**: The waiting term, not the braking term, decides the verdict. The in-loop takeover alone covers `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_over_spare_mult_str]` the spare, so no stronger brake can make $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ hold at this speed; only a lower $\\SpeedAtDetection$ or a stop before the request can. The delay, braking, and allowance terms follow the stopping-distance model of @sec-appendix-systems-stopping.\n\n:::\n\n### Takeover speed ceilings from available clearance {#sec-intervention-speed-ceilings}\n\nBecause no braking force can recover distance spent waiting, the machine must bound its operating speed before an intervention is ever requested. One inequality sets both bounds that this section and the next derive. If the machine continues at speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ for a waiting duration $\\ExternalWait$ while awaiting the operator&#x27;s response, the finished stopping budget of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget gains one term, the travel during that wait. The pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$, the jerk-limited resident stop $\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ (@eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix), and the fixed allowances keep the meanings defined there, and the sum must still not exceed $\\ClearDistance$ measured at the same reference event:\n$$\\StoppingDistance(\\ExternalWait) = \\SpeedAtDetection(\\ExternalWait + \\PrebrakeDelay) + \\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) + \\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin + \\TrackingErrorBound \\le \\ClearDistance.$$ {#eq-intervention-stopping-inequality}\nSetting $\\ExternalWait = \\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ and solving for speed gives the takeover speed ceiling of this section. Fixing the speed and solving for $\\ExternalWait$ gives the latest fallback deadline of @sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines.\n\nThe three stops of notebook \\ref{nbk-intervention-operator-takeover-budget-spatial-clearance-margin} differ only in how long the machine coasts before an identical jerk-limited resident stop begins (@fig-14-takeover-timeline). Every trace ends with the same braking curve, so the whole difference between stopping `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` short of the clear distance and stopping at `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_stop_ool_str]` is coasting.\n\n::: {#fig-14-takeover-timeline fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Two takeovers against one clear distance**: Clearance consumed at the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]` aisle speed, starting from the fixed allowances. Each trace coasts through its delay, including the dispatch-to-admission interval, and then follows the same jerk-limited resident stop. The machine alone stops `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]` short of the `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_clear_str]` clear distance. A `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]` in-loop takeover inserted before braking carries the stop to `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_stop_in_str]`, and a `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_ool_str]` out-of-loop takeover carries it to `[InterventionTakeoverBudget.d_stop_ool_str]`. The waiting, not the braking, spends the clearance.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two stacked panels of clearance consumed in meters against time in seconds. Three traces start at a shaded band of fixed allowances, rise linearly while the machine waits, and flatten during an identical braking curve. A dashed horizontal line marks the rack-end clear distance. The top panel shows the first two seconds: the machine-only trace ends below the line with a bracket marking the spare margin, and the in-loop takeover trace ends above it. The bottom panel shows six seconds and seven meters, where the out-of-loop takeover trace rises far above the line before braking.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ SPEED CEILING AGAINST THE SOURCED HUMAN RESPONSE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @tbl-14-teleop-stability and the paragraph that introduces it.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Price each sourced human response time as a speed ceiling.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Per row, the human response, the total with the console term</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (video glass-to-glass plus the four-phase exchange), the added</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          travel at the aisle speed, and the speed at which the finished</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          budget still fits. Rows: Model Human Processor simple reaction</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (nominal and slow bound); perception-brake times for an expected,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          an unexpected, and a surprise signal; percentiles of condition-mean</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          take-over time.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseTakeoverCeilingLadder: each total adds to</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          tau_delay of the WarehouseAisle finished budget; the ceiling is the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          partial-budget S-curve root with the stipulated jerk bound.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionTakeoverLadder: \"\"\"Speed ceiling for each sourced human response time.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>ladder = Scenarios.WarehouseTakeoverCeilingLadder  # one takeover budget per sourced response v = ladder.base.speed  # category: chosen (AISLE.v_aisle) t_comm = ladder.transport  # category: sourced video + chosen exchange human = ladder.responses  # category: sourced (Card, Moran, Newell 1983; Green 2000; Zhang et al. 2019 data)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">Plain loops: class-body comprehensions cannot see class-level names.</h1>\n<p>rows = ladder.result.rows total, travel, ceiling = {}, {}, {} for _k, _r in rows.items(): total[_k] = _r.takeover.to(millisecond) travel[_k] = _r.takeover_travel.to(millimeter) ceiling[_k] = _r.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) order = list(human) ceiling_list = list(ceiling.values())  # insertion order follows human</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs((total[\"mhp_slow\"] - AISLE.t_takeover_in_loop).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Design in-loop row is the registry total.\") check(abs((total[\"p90\"] - AISLE.t_takeover_out_of_loop).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Design out-of-loop row is the registry total.\") check(abs(ceiling[\"mhp\"].magnitude - 1.0094) &lt; 5e-5, f\"nominal in-loop ceiling: {ceiling['mhp']}\") check(abs(ceiling[\"mhp_slow\"].magnitude - 0.8362) &lt; 5e-5, f\"design in-loop ceiling: {ceiling['mhp_slow']}\") check(abs(ceiling[\"surprise\"].magnitude - 0.4504) &lt; 5e-5, f\"surprise ceiling: {ceiling['surprise']}\") check(abs(ceiling[\"p90\"].magnitude - 0.1911) &lt; 5e-5, f\"design out-of-loop ceiling: {ceiling['p90']}\") check(all(hi &gt; lo for hi, lo in zip(ceiling_list, ceiling_list[1:])), \"Ceilings must fall as the response grows.\") check(max(ceiling.values()) &lt; v, \"No operator state fits at the aisle speed.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">Expectation costs more speed than the console term does.</h1>\n<p>console_cost = (ladder.base.variant(takeover=human[\"mhp_slow\"]).result.with_takeover.speed_ceiling - ceiling[\"mhp_slow\"]).magnitude check((ceiling[\"expected\"] - ceiling[\"surprise\"]).magnitude &gt; console_cost, \"Expectation must outweigh the console term.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) t_comm_str = fmt_latency(t_comm, unit=millisecond, precision=0) mhp_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"mhp\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mhp_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"mhp\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mhp_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"mhp\"], unit=meter, precision=2) mhp_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"mhp\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) mhp_slow_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"mhp_slow\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mhp_slow_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"mhp_slow\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mhp_slow_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"mhp_slow\"], unit=meter, precision=2) mhp_slow_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"mhp_slow\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) expected_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"expected\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) expected_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"expected\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) expected_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"expected\"], unit=meter, precision=2) expected_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"expected\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) unexpected_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"unexpected\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) unexpected_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"unexpected\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) unexpected_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"unexpected\"], unit=meter, precision=2) unexpected_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"unexpected\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) surprise_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"surprise\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) surprise_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"surprise\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) surprise_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"surprise\"], unit=meter, precision=2) surprise_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"surprise\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) p50_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"p50\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p50_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"p50\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p50_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"p50\"], unit=meter, precision=2) p50_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"p50\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) mean_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"mean\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mean_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"mean\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) mean_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"mean\"], unit=meter, precision=2) mean_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"mean\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) p90_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"p90\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p90_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"p90\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p90_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"p90\"], unit=meter, precision=2) p90_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"p90\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) p99_human_str = fmt_latency(human[\"p99\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p99_total_str = fmt_latency(total[\"p99\"], unit=millisecond, precision=0, commas=True) p99_travel_str = fmt_length(travel[\"p99\"], unit=meter, precision=2) p99_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling[\"p99\"], unit=meter / second, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>@Tbl-14-teleop-stability prices each sourced response time. Every row adds the <code>[InterventionTakeoverLadder.t_comm_str]</code> console term to the human response, adds the total to $\\PrebrakeDelay$, and solves the finished stopping budget for the largest speed at which the machine still stops inside the rack-end clear distance. Before charging the missing admission delay, the ceiling falls from <code>[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_ceiling_str]</code> for an attending operator who meets the expected stimulus at the nominal pace to <code>[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p99_ceiling_str]</code> for the slowest percentile of takeover conditions. No row fits at the <code>[InterventionTakeoverLadder.v_str]</code> aisle speed. Two facts dominate the table. Expectation matters more than the console, because moving from an expected to a surprising stimulus costs more speed than the whole console term does. The tail matters more than the average, because a speed chosen for the mean take-over time is overrun by every condition slower than the mean.</p>\n<p>A ceiling above the resident stop's transition speed $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit$ is the positive root derived for the machine's own stop in @eq-enforce-speed-ceiling, where the half jerk ramp acts as an added delay before full braking (the rigid-stop form is @eq-body-velocity-ceiling). A takeover adds one more such interval, so setting $\\ExternalWait = \\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ in @eq-intervention-stopping-inequality folds the human delay into the same root: $$\\SpeedBound(\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency) = \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration\\left[\\sqrt{\\FullSpeedTravelTime^2 + \\frac{2\\,(\\ClearDistance-\\FixedClearanceAllowance)}{\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration}} - \\FullSpeedTravelTime\\right],\\qquad \\FullSpeedTravelTime = \\PrebrakeDelay + \\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency + \\frac{\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration}{2\\JerkLimit}.$$ {#eq-intervention-speed-ceiling} The term $\\FullSpeedTravelTime$ collects every interval during which the machine still moves at $\\SpeedAtDetection$: its own delay, the human's, and the half jerk ramp before full braking. Below $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit$ the stop never reaches full deceleration and the cubic branch of @eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix applies instead, which is why the table's slower rows come from the solver rather than from this root.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE TAKEOVER SPEED CEILING SOLVED BY HAND\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @eq-intervention-speed-ceiling and the notebook &quot;The speed a\n# │          human buys&quot;.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show the root of the budget quadratic for the design in-loop row,\n# │          so the table&#x27;s ceiling can be redone with a pencil.\n# │ Show:    b, D_clear - delta, the root, the branch threshold a^2/j, the\n# │          limiting ceiling as braking becomes instantaneous, and the root\n# │          with the allocated Delta_da added to T_Sigma.\n# │ How:     Closed-form root of v^2/(2a) + b v - (D - delta) = 0: the terms\n# │          b, the root, a^2/j, and the rigid bound from the in-loop takeover\n# │          budget (Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed), guarded\n# │          against calc_scurve_max_permitted_velocity (the table&#x27;s solver).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millisecond\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_velocity, fmt_latency, fmt_length, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionCeilingRoot:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Closed-form takeover speed ceiling for the design in-loop response.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    in_loop = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # in-loop takeover at the aisle speed\n    a = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative\n    j = AISLE.j_brake  # category: derived (stipulated jerk bound)\n    tau = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived\n    t_in = in_loop.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport)\n    d_avail = AISLE.d_clear - AISLE.fixed_overhead  # category: derived (D_clear - delta)\n    delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission)\n    v_aisle = in_loop.speed  # category: chosen\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    partial = in_loop.result.with_takeover  # the budget with T_Sigma in tau_delay\n    complete = in_loop.result.with_takeover_and_dispatch  # the same with Delta_da charged\n    half_ramp = partial.ramp_delay.to(second)\n    b = partial.ceiling_lead_time.to(second)\n    radicand = (partial.ceiling_root**2).to(second**2)\n    v_max = partial.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    v_knee = partial.jerk_plateau_speed.to(meter / second)\n    v_rigid = partial.rigid_speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)  # ideal zero-distance braking bound\n    b_da = complete.ceiling_lead_time.to(second)\n    v_max_da = complete.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(\n        abs((a * (partial.ceiling_root - b)).to(meter / second).magnitude - v_max.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Root must equal the solver&#x27;s in-loop ceiling.&quot;\n    )\n    check(abs(v_max.magnitude - InterventionTakeoverBudget.ceil_in.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Root must equal the takeover cell&#x27;s in-loop ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(abs((b - tau - t_in - half_ramp).to(second).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;b is the delay, the takeover, and half a jerk ramp.&quot;)\n    check(abs((b_da - b - delta_da).to(second).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;Charging Delta_da adds it to b.&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs(v_max_da.magnitude - AISLE.v_takeover_in_loop_ceiling.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Root with Delta_da must equal the registry&#x27;s charged ceiling.&quot;\n    )\n    check(v_max &gt; v_knee, &quot;The design in-loop row must sit on the quadratic branch.&quot;)\n    check(InterventionTakeoverBudget.ceil_ool &lt; v_knee, &quot;The out-of-loop row must sit on the cubic branch.&quot;)\n    check(v_rigid &lt; v_aisle, &quot;Even instantaneous braking must not admit the aisle speed.&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_rigid.magnitude - 1.232) &lt; 5e-4, f&quot;rigid-braking ceiling: {v_rigid}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    a_str = fmt(a.to(meter / second**2).magnitude, precision=0, commas=False)\n    j_str = fmt(j.to(meter / second**3).magnitude, precision=2, commas=False)\n    tau_str = fmt(tau.to(second).magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    t_in_str = fmt(t_in.to(second).magnitude, precision=3, commas=False)\n    half_ramp_str = fmt(half_ramp.magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    b_str = fmt(b.magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    d_avail_str = fmt(d_avail.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2, commas=False)\n    radicand_str = fmt(radicand.magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    root_str = fmt(radicand.magnitude**0.5, precision=4, commas=False)\n    v_max_str = fmt_velocity(v_max, unit=meter / second, precision=3)\n    v_knee_str = fmt_velocity(v_knee, unit=meter / second, precision=3)\n    v_rigid_str = fmt_velocity(v_rigid, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    v_max_da_str = fmt_velocity(v_max_da, unit=meter / second, precision=3)\n    delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-intervention-takeover-speed-ceiling .callout-notebook title=&quot;The speed a human buys&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: What is the highest speed at which the mobile manipulator may request an in-loop takeover from an attending operator, and does any brake, however strong, admit the aisle speed?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.a_str]` m/s² (Illustrative) and $\\JerkLimit =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.j_str]` m/s³ (Derived).\n* $\\PrebrakeDelay =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.tau_str]` s (Derived) and $\\TakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.t_in_str]` s (Derived, from the measured human and transport terms of Mode 1 plus the chosen handshake).\n* $\\ClearDistance - \\FixedClearanceAllowance =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.d_avail_str]` m (Illustrative, less the fixed allowances).\n\n**Math**: $\\FullSpeedTravelTime =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.tau_str]` $+$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.t_in_str]` $+$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.a_str]` $/ (2 \\times$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.j_str]`$) =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.tau_str]` $+$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.t_in_str]` $+$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.half_ramp_str]` $=$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.b_str]` s. Then $\\FullSpeedTravelTime^2 + 2 \\times$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.d_avail_str]` $/$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.a_str]` $=$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.radicand_str]` s², whose square root is `[InterventionCeilingRoot.root_str]` s, so by @eq-intervention-speed-ceiling $\\SpeedBound =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.a_str]` $\\times$ (`[InterventionCeilingRoot.root_str]` $-$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.b_str]`). The root lies above $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.v_knee_str]`, so the quadratic branch was the right one. If both the deceleration and jerk bounds are removed so that the resident stopping distance tends to zero, the limiting ceiling is $(\\ClearDistance - \\FixedClearanceAllowance)/(\\PrebrakeDelay + \\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency)$.\n\n**Result**: $\\SpeedBound =$ `[InterventionCeilingRoot.v_max_str]`, the design in-loop row of @tbl-14-teleop-stability; instantaneous braking would raise it only to `[InterventionCeilingRoot.v_rigid_str]`.\n\n**Systems insight**: Even a perfect brake leaves the ceiling below the `[InterventionCeilingRoot.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, because the travel during $\\PrebrakeDelay + \\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ alone exceeds the clear distance at that speed. The waiting term of $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ sets the request speed, so faster brakes cannot buy back a slow human; adding the `[InterventionCeilingRoot.delta_da_str]` admission allocation to $\\FullSpeedTravelTime$ lowers the root to `[InterventionCeilingRoot.v_max_da_str]`.\n\n:::\n\n| Operator state and response                         |                                             Human response |                                          With console term |                                 Added travel at aisle speed |                                                Speed ceiling |\n|:----------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------:|------------------------------------------------------------:|-------------------------------------------------------------:|\n| In-loop, expected stimulus, nominal pace            |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_human_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_total_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_travel_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_ceiling_str]` |\n| **In-loop, expected stimulus, slow bound (design)** |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_slow_human_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_slow_total_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_slow_travel_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mhp_slow_ceiling_str]` |\n| Expected brake signal                               |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.expected_human_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.expected_total_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.expected_travel_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.expected_ceiling_str]` |\n| Unexpected but common signal                        | `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.unexpected_human_str]` | `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.unexpected_total_str]` | `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.unexpected_travel_str]` | `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.unexpected_ceiling_str]` |\n| Surprise event                                      |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.surprise_human_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.surprise_total_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.surprise_travel_str]` |   `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.surprise_ceiling_str]` |\n| Out-of-loop takeover, median condition              |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p50_human_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p50_total_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p50_travel_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p50_ceiling_str]` |\n| Out-of-loop takeover, average condition             |       `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mean_human_str]` |       `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mean_total_str]` |       `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mean_travel_str]` |       `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.mean_ceiling_str]` |\n| **Out-of-loop takeover, 90th percentile (design)**  |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p90_human_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p90_total_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p90_travel_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p90_ceiling_str]` |\n| Out-of-loop takeover, 99th percentile               |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p99_human_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p99_total_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p99_travel_str]` |        `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.p99_ceiling_str]` |\n\n: **Takeover speed ceiling against the sourced human response**: The first two rows are Model Human Processor simple reactions, one perceptual, cognitive, and motor cycle at nominal and slow-bound cycle times [@jastrzembski2007model]. The next three are driver perception-brake times for an expected, an unexpected, and a surprise signal [@green2000stop]. The last four are percentiles of the mean take-over time across 520 experimental conditions, computed from the published data of @zhang2019takeover; they describe conditions, not individual operators, whose tail is longer. Each total adds the console term (video glass-to-glass latency [@schimpe2022opensource] plus the four-phase exchange) and excludes the dispatch-to-admission interval, so every ceiling is optimistic; charging that allocation lowers the design in-loop ceiling to `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.ceil_in_da_str]`. Bold rows are the design values. {#tbl-14-teleop-stability tbl-colwidths=&quot;[32,16,17,17,18]&quot;}\n\nThe table also locates the other interfaces a site might offer. Continuous velocity guidance or supervisory waypoint dispatch places the operator somewhere between an expected-stimulus reaction and an out-of-loop takeover, and only a measured response distribution for that interface, console, and task says where. A hardwired protective input removes the console term but not the human response, so its operator still needs the local stop to hold the machine safe while that response runs.\n</code></pre>\n<p>\\Needspace{0.62\\textheight}</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n### Clearance-derived fallback deadlines {#sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines}\n\nIf a machine reaches the edge of its operating domain at speed and requests a takeover, an operator who is distracted, asleep, or incapacitated will fail to complete the handshake before the machine runs out of clearance. An autonomous policy&#x27;s handover request can therefore never wait indefinitely, nor can its timeout be a fixed software constant: a single fixed window that is safe at a crawl will overrun the clearance at transport speed (@sec-intervention-fallacies-pitfalls prices such a window at both speeds). Safe timeouts must instead be derived at runtime from the machine&#x27;s current speed, line-of-sight clearance, and braking deceleration.\n\nThe deadline is the second reading of the stopping inequality. The speed ceiling fixed the wait and solved for speed; the deadline fixes the current speed and solves for the wait. Setting equality in @eq-intervention-stopping-inequality ($\\StoppingDistance(\\ExternalWait) = \\ClearDistance$) and solving for the maximum permissible waiting time yields the latest fallback deadline $\\FallbackDeadline$:\n$$\\FallbackDeadline = \\frac{\\ClearDistance-\\LocalizationBound-\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin-\\TrackingErrorBound-\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)}{\\SpeedAtDetection}-\\PrebrakeDelay,\\qquad \\HandoverTimeout=\\max\\!\\left(0,\\min(\\HandoverTimeoutCeiling,\\FallbackDeadline)\\right).$$ {#eq-intervention-latest-fallback}\nFor a live check, the safety microcontroller dispatches fallback **no later than** the last permission tick before the resulting deadline. A nonpositive $\\FallbackDeadline$ calls for immediate fallback and may mean stopping is already infeasible. The configured protocol ceiling cannot extend that physical deadline or the expiry of active permission.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ HANDOVER DEADLINE AND TAKEOVER SPEEDS ON THE MOBILE MANIPULATOR</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-authority-transitions, the T_latest paragraph and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the takeover-speed paragraphs that follow it.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Spend the finished budget's spare margin as a handover deadline and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          derive the speeds at which each takeover may be requested.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    T_latest at the aisle speed (the unallocated timing allowance of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the finished budget), its tick-rounded dispatch, the handshake that</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          fits inside it, and the sourced in-loop and out-of-loop takeover</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          ceilings; T_latest at a 1.2 m/s slow-down, which the sourced</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          in-loop response overruns; the redesigned slow-down and crawl,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          T_latest at each and the time left after the takeover; the crawl</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          against the 99th-percentile response.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     @eq-intervention-latest-fallback with the jerk-limited resident</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          stop (jerk bound = peak jerk of the reference C2 polynomial at the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          drive limit) and all three overheads: the deadline (delay spare),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the clipped timeout, and the partial ceilings of the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.Warehouse*Takeover* budgets (TakeoverBudgetModel).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionTimeoutBudget: \"\"\"Latest fallback dispatch after a handover request, and the speeds it forces.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed  # in-loop request at the aisle speed ool_at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed at_fast = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtFastSlowdown  # a slow-down sized for a much faster response at_request = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRequestSpeed  # the redesigned in-loop slow-down at_crawl = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtCrawl  # the redesigned out-of-loop crawl at_crawl_p99 = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtCrawlP99  # the crawl against the 99th percentile v = at_aisle.speed  # category: chosen tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen t_in = at_aisle.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport) t_ool = ool_at_aisle.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport) t_ool_p99 = at_crawl_p99.takeover  # category: derived v_in = at_request.speed  # category: derived (Delta_da-charged in-loop ceiling, rounded down) v_ool = at_crawl.speed  # category: chosen (under the sourced out-of-loop ceiling) t_request = AISLE.t_handshake_request  # category: chosen t_acknowledge = AISLE.t_handshake_acknowledge  # category: chosen t_confirm = AISLE.t_handshake_confirm  # category: chosen t_handshake = AISLE.t_handshake  # category: derived (four-phase sum) v_fast_slowdown = at_fast.speed  # category: illustrative (a slow-down that fits only a much faster human response) config = at_aisle.configured_timeout  # category: chosen (configured protocol ceiling) manual_lease = at_aisle.manual_lease  # category: chosen (loss-of-engagement lease)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>aisle = at_aisle.result t_latest = aisle.deadline.to(millisecond) spare = aisle.machine.spare.to(millimeter) t_latest_fast = at_fast.result.deadline.to(millisecond) short_fast = (-at_fast.result.time_left).to(millisecond) t_latest_in = at_request.result.deadline.to(millisecond) spare_in = at_request.result.machine.spare.to(millimeter) left_in = at_request.result.time_left.to(millisecond) t_latest_ool = at_crawl.result.deadline.to(millisecond) left_ool = at_crawl.result.time_left.to(millisecond) short_ool_p99 = (-at_crawl_p99.result.time_left).to(millisecond) timeout = aisle.timeout.to(millisecond) timeout_ticks = aisle.timeout_ticks admitted = (timeout_ticks * tick).to(millisecond) ceil_in = aisle.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) ceil_ool = ool_at_aisle.result.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second) ceil_ool_p99 = at_crawl_p99.result.with_takeover.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_latest.magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, f\"T_latest at the aisle speed: {t_latest}\") check(abs(spare.magnitude - 131.01) &lt; 5e-3, f\"spare margin at the aisle speed: {spare}\") check(abs((spare - v * t_latest).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The spare is T_latest at the aisle speed.\") check(abs(t_latest_fast.magnitude - 184.11) &lt; 5e-3, f\"T_latest at 1.2 m/s: {t_latest_fast}\") check(t_in &gt; t_latest_fast, \"The sourced in-loop response must overrun the allowance at 1.2 m/s.\") check(abs(t_latest_in.magnitude - 751.608) &lt; 5e-3, f\"T_latest at the in-loop slow-down: {t_latest_in}\") check(abs(spare_in.magnitude - 563.706) &lt; 5e-3, f\"spare margin at the in-loop slow-down: {spare_in}\") check(abs(left_in.magnitude - 146.608) &lt; 5e-3, f\"T_latest left after the in-loop takeover: {left_in}\") check(left_in &gt; AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission, \"The remainder must also pay the allocated Delta_da.\") check(left_ool.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The crawl must cover the design out-of-loop takeover.\") check(short_ool_p99.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The crawl must not cover the 99th-percentile takeover.\") check(admitted.magnitude == 100, f\"tick-rounded fallback dispatch: {admitted}\") check(t_latest &lt; config, \"The clearance deadline must bind before the configured ceiling.\") check(abs(t_handshake.to(millisecond).magnitude - 31.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"four-phase handshake: {t_handshake}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_takeover_allowance_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 31, 605, 100.8, 751.6 ms, 1.3 and 0.75 m/s</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_in.to(millisecond).magnitude - 605.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"in-loop takeover in the fig-alt: {t_in}\") check(abs(v_in.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.75) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(v.to(meter / second).magnitude - 1.3) &lt; 1e-9, \"speeds in the fig-alt\") check(t_handshake &lt; t_latest, \"The four-phase handshake must fit inside T_latest.\") check(t_in &gt; t_latest, \"An in-loop takeover must not fit at the aisle speed.\") check(abs(ceil_in.magnitude - 0.8362) &lt; 5e-5, f\"in-loop takeover ceiling: {ceil_in}\") check(abs(ceil_ool.magnitude - 0.1911) &lt; 5e-5, f\"out-of-loop takeover ceiling: {ceil_ool}\") check(v_in &lt; ceil_in &lt; v_fast_slowdown &lt; v, \"The slow-down sits under the sourced in-loop ceiling; 1.2 m/s does not.\") check(ceil_ool_p99 &lt; v_ool &lt; ceil_ool, \"The crawl sits under the design ceiling and above the 99th-percentile ceiling.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1) t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=millisecond, precision=1) admitted_str = fmt_latency(admitted, unit=millisecond, precision=0) tick_str = fmt_latency(tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0) config_str = fmt_latency(config, unit=millisecond, precision=0) manual_lease_str = fmt_latency(manual_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_request_str = fmt_latency(t_request, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_acknowledge_str = fmt_latency(t_acknowledge, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_confirm_str = fmt_latency(t_confirm, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_handshake_str = fmt_latency(t_handshake, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_in_str = fmt_latency(t_in, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_ool_str = fmt_latency(t_ool, unit=second, precision=1) t_ool_p99_str = fmt_latency(t_ool_p99, unit=second, precision=1) ceil_in_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_in, unit=meter / second, precision=2) ceil_ool_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_ool, unit=meter / second, precision=2) ceil_ool_p99_str = fmt_velocity(ceil_ool_p99, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_fast_str = fmt_velocity(v_fast_slowdown, unit=meter / second, precision=1) t_latest_fast_str = fmt_latency(t_latest_fast, unit=millisecond, precision=1) short_fast_str = fmt_latency(short_fast, unit=millisecond, precision=1) v_in_str = fmt_velocity(v_in, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_ool_str = fmt_velocity(v_ool, unit=meter / second, precision=2) t_latest_in_str = fmt_latency(t_latest_in, unit=millisecond, precision=1) spare_in_str = fmt_length(spare_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1) left_in_str = fmt_latency(left_in, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_latest_ool_str = fmt_latency(t_latest_ool, unit=second, precision=2) left_ool_str = fmt_latency(left_ool, unit=second, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE WAITING ALLOWANCE SUBSTITUTED BY HAND\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @eq-intervention-latest-fallback and the notebook &quot;The waiting\n# │          allowance&quot;.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Put the operands of T_latest on the page at the aisle speed and at\n# │          the in-loop slow-down, and set the in-loop takeover against each.\n# │ Show:    D_clear - delta, tau_delay, the resident stop at each speed, the\n# │          two T_latest values, the remainder after the in-loop takeover at\n# │          the slow-down, and that remainder less the allocated Delta_da.\n# │ How:     The resident stop (scurve_travel) and T_latest (deadline) of the\n# │          in-loop takeover budgets at both speeds; guarded against\n# │          d_suffix = v^2/(2a) + v a/(2j) (both speeds lie above a^2/j), the\n# │          equation by hand, InterventionTimeoutBudget, and\n# │          InterventionAdmissionAllocation.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millisecond\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_multiple, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionWaitingAllowance:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;T_latest at two speeds with every operand visible.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed\n    at_request = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRequestSpeed\n    v_hi = at_aisle.speed  # category: chosen\n    v_lo = at_request.speed  # category: derived (Delta_da-charged ceiling, rounded down)\n    a = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative\n    j = AISLE.j_brake  # category: derived (stipulated jerk bound)\n    tau = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived\n    d_avail = AISLE.d_clear - AISLE.fixed_overhead  # category: derived (D_clear - delta)\n    t_in = at_aisle.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport)\n    delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    hi, lo = at_aisle.result, at_request.result\n    d_hi = hi.machine.scurve_travel.to(meter)\n    d_lo = lo.machine.scurve_travel.to(meter)\n    t_hi = hi.deadline.to(millisecond)\n    t_lo = lo.deadline.to(millisecond)\n    left_lo = lo.time_left.to(millisecond)\n    left_lo_da = lo.time_left_after_admission.to(millisecond)\n    ratio = (t_lo / t_hi).to(&quot;&quot;).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(v_lo &gt;= a**2 / j, &quot;Both speeds must lie on the quadratic branch of the resident stop.&quot;)\n    check(abs((d_hi - (v_hi**2 / (2 * a) + v_hi * a / (2 * j))).to(meter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;Resident stop at the aisle speed is v^2/2a + v a/2j.&quot;)\n    check(abs((d_lo - (v_lo**2 / (2 * a) + v_lo * a / (2 * j))).to(meter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;Resident stop at the slow-down is v^2/2a + v a/2j.&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((t_hi - ((d_avail - d_hi) / v_hi - tau)).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9,\n        &quot;T_latest at the aisle speed by @eq-intervention-latest-fallback.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(\n        abs((t_lo - ((d_avail - d_lo) / v_lo - tau)).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9,\n        &quot;T_latest at the slow-down by @eq-intervention-latest-fallback.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(abs((t_hi - InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;T_latest at the aisle speed matches the timeout cell.&quot;)\n    check(abs((t_lo - InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_in).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;T_latest at the slow-down matches the timeout cell.&quot;)\n    check(abs((left_lo_da - InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_in_da).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Charged remainder matches the allocation cell.&quot;)\n    check(t_in &gt; 5 * t_hi, &quot;The in-loop takeover must overrun the aisle-speed allowance several times.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_hi_str = fmt(v_hi.to(meter / second).magnitude, precision=1, commas=False)\n    v_lo_str = fmt(v_lo.to(meter / second).magnitude, precision=2, commas=False)\n    v_hi_unit_str = fmt_velocity(v_hi, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    v_lo_unit_str = fmt_velocity(v_lo, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    d_avail_str = fmt(d_avail.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2, commas=False)\n    tau_str = fmt(tau.to(second).magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    d_hi_str = fmt(d_hi.magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    d_lo_str = fmt(d_lo.magnitude, precision=4, commas=False)\n    t_hi_str = fmt_latency(t_hi, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_lo_str = fmt_latency(t_lo, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_in_str = fmt_latency(t_in, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    left_lo_str = fmt_latency(left_lo, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    left_lo_da_str = fmt_latency(left_lo_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(ratio, precision=1)\n    t_hi_s_str = fmt_latency(t_hi, unit=second, precision=4)\n    t_lo_s_str = fmt_latency(t_lo, unit=second, precision=4)\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-intervention-waiting-allowance .callout-notebook title=&quot;The waiting allowance&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: How long may the mobile manipulator wait after a handover request before it must dispatch its own stop, at the `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_hi_unit_str]` aisle speed and at the `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_lo_unit_str]` slow-down, and does the `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_in_str]` in-loop takeover fit either allowance?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* $\\ClearDistance - \\FixedClearanceAllowance =$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_avail_str]` m (Illustrative) and $\\PrebrakeDelay =$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.tau_str]` s (Derived).\n* $\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) = \\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration) + \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/(2\\JerkLimit)$ (Derived; @eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix): `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_hi_str]` m at `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_hi_str]` m/s and `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_lo_str]` m at `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_lo_str]` m/s.\n* $\\TakeoverResponseTime =$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_in_str]` (Derived, measured human and transport terms plus the chosen handshake) and $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval =$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.delta_da_str]` (Illustrative; the unmeasured allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission).\n\n**Math**: By @eq-intervention-latest-fallback, $\\FallbackDeadline =$ (`[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_avail_str]` $-$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_hi_str]`)/`[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_hi_str]` $-$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.tau_str]` $=$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_hi_s_str]` at the aisle speed, and (`[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_avail_str]` $-$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.d_lo_str]`)/`[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_lo_str]` $-$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.tau_str]` $=$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_lo_s_str]` at the slow-down. Reserving the takeover leaves `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_lo_str]` $-$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_in_str]` $=$ `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.left_lo_str]`, and charging $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ leaves `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.left_lo_da_str]`.\n\n**Result**: At the aisle speed the takeover overruns a `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.t_hi_str]` allowance; at the slow-down it fits with `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.left_lo_da_str]` to spare on the complete budget.\n\n**Systems insight**: Slowing from `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_hi_str]` to `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.v_lo_str]` m/s multiplies the waiting allowance `[InterventionWaitingAllowance.ratio_mult_str]`, because both the stop term and the travel per millisecond of $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ shrink with speed. The deadline is a function of the current state, so no single configured timeout can be correct at both speeds.\n\n:::\n\nAt the aisle speed, the `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_str]` of notebook \\ref{nbk-intervention-waiting-allowance}, or `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.spare_str]` of travel, is the unallocated timing allowance of the stopping subtotal in @sec-enforcement-composite-budget, and it is a partial-budget value. Charging the `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.delta_da_str]` dispatch-to-admission allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission completes the budget. At speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ the charge costs $\\SpeedAtDetection\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ of travel, so at the aisle speed only `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle_str]` of waiting survives it. Wherever the rest of the chapter reports a waiting allowance, it gives the partial value and then the charged one, and the charged value is the one a design must meet. Rounded down to whole permission ticks, the partial aisle-speed allowance of `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_str]` becomes `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.admitted_str]`, which illustrates tick rounding rather than an absolute fallback time. The `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.config_str]` configured ceiling is only an outer protocol limit, because the clearance deadline binds first.\n\nApplying that clearance deadline without double-counting elapsed travel means both readings of the stopping inequality also need a clock origin, and the chapter uses the two that @tbl-14-clock-origins sets side by side. The design budget behind every displayed number counts from the observation&#x27;s capture. A live check on the safety microcontroller counts from now.\n\n|                      | Design budget (capture origin)                                                                                                             | Live check (now origin)                                                                                     |\n|:---------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Where it is used     | Every number this chapter displays                                                                                                         | The safety microcontroller, at each permission tick of a pending request                                    |\n| Clear distance       | $\\ClearDistance$ measured at the observation&#x27;s capture event                                                                               | The current tick&#x27;s remaining clearance, already reduced for the motion since capture                        |\n| Delay before braking | $\\PrebrakeDelay + \\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, from capture to the onset of the physical response                                          | Only the delay still ahead: dispatch to response onset plus any unpaid processing, never a fresh full lease |\n| Meaning of the wait  | $\\ExternalWait$ is additional delay inserted into that budget, with any overlap with its processing, lease, or transport intervals removed | The time left before the last permission tick at which a stop still fits                                    |\n\n: **Two clock origins for one stopping inequality**: Either column is correct on its own. Mixing a cell from one column with a cell from the other charges elapsed travel twice or not at all. {#tbl-14-clock-origins tbl-colwidths=&quot;[18,44,38]&quot;}\n\nThe two ways to mix them fail in opposite directions. A live check that takes the current clearance, which already reflects the travel since capture, and then also subtracts the capture-origin delay pays for the elapsed part of that delay twice and dispatches its fallback early, which costs availability. The reverse mix, the capture-time clearance with a delay counted only from now, never pays for the travel since capture and dispatches its fallback late, which costs clearance.\n\nThe `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_handshake_str]` protocol exchange fits the aisle-speed allowance on both budgets. The sourced `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_in_str]` in-loop response does not. It exceeds the aisle-speed allowance several times over, so an in-loop request needs a slower machine, and the human response time, not the machine, sets how much slower. Assuming a faster human gives the wrong answer. A slow-down to `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.v_fast_str]` would suit an operator who responds in a small fraction of a second; there the budget leaves `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_fast_str]`, and the sourced response overruns it by `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.short_fast_str]`. Such a design passes every check that uses an optimistic human and fails the first operator who needs the time the literature measures.\n\n\n\nThe design slows to `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.v_in_str]` before requesting an in-loop takeover. That speed comes from solving the budget with both the sourced response and $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ added to $\\PrebrakeDelay$, which gives a complete-budget ceiling of `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.ceil_in_da_str]`, rounded down to the next `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.v_step_str]` step. At that speed the capture-origin budget leaves `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.spare_in_str]`, or `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_in_str]` of additional delay. Reserving the response leaves `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.left_in_str]`, and charging $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ still leaves `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_in_da_str]`. That is a conservative design allocation, not a claim that every request at that speed receives the same live timeout, and it holds only while the operator meets the expected stimulus. An unexpected hazard moves the operator to the slower rows of @tbl-14-teleop-stability, whose ceilings the slow-down exceeds.\n\nSolving with both intervals matters. The partial-budget ceiling, with the response alone added, is `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.ceil_in_str]`, and a designer who stopped there might choose `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.v_in_old_str]`, which sits under it. At that speed the response leaves `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_old_str]`, less than the `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.delta_da_str]` allocation, so once the omitted interval is charged the slow-down misses its deadline by `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.short_old_str]`. A budget that leaves out one interval of a few tens of milliseconds admits a speed that the complete budget rejects.\n\nThe out-of-loop takeover forces a larger change. Its `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_ool_str]` design response caps the request speed at `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.ceil_ool_str]`, so the crawl before an out-of-loop request is `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.v_ool_str]`, where the budget leaves `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_ool_str]` and the design response leaves `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.left_ool_str]` of it unspent. The crawl survives the $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ charge, which lowers its ceiling only to `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.ceil_ool_da_str]`. It still fails the 99th-percentile condition, whose `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_ool_p99_str]` response admits only `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.ceil_ool_p99_str]`, and an individual operator&#x27;s tail is longer than any condition mean. A crawl slow enough for every operator is no longer motion worth keeping. The robust design therefore stops the machine automatically before handing over. The local stop runs first, and the out-of-loop takeover begins from standstill, where waiting costs time but no clearance. Both moving candidates still require qualified admission delay, current clearance, and a feasible stopping continuation. The hidden pallet leaves no room for an anticipatory slow-down at all, so the machine answers it with its own local stop and requests the takeover from standstill. A planned slow-down or crawl can prepare an anticipated request; neither permits waiting at aisle speed for a newly discovered obstacle.\n\nEvery one of these designs rests on two properties the chapter has so far assumed. Authority, once granted, must not outlive a proposer that falls silent, and it must move between proposers on a single tick without two of them ever commanding the same channel. @Sec-intervention-authority-transitions builds both.\n\n::: {#chk-intervention-takeover-budgets .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Takeover budgets and speed ceilings&quot;}\n\nA takeover is a delay inserted before braking, so every question about it is a question about the stopping budget.\n\n- [ ] **Waiting vs. braking**: Can you explain, from the limiting case of @eq-intervention-speed-ceiling, why no brake however strong admits an in-loop takeover at the aisle speed?\n- [ ] **Ceiling estimate**: An operator who waits for an expected brake signal responds in `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.expected_human_str]`, plus the `[InterventionTakeoverLadder.t_comm_str]` console term. Using the givens of notebook \\ref{nbk-intervention-takeover-speed-ceiling}, can you estimate the speed at which that operator may be asked to take over, and check it against @tbl-14-teleop-stability?\n- [ ] **Clock origins**: Can you explain why a live check that measures clearance from now must not also charge the delay measured from capture?\n\n:::\n\n## Authority Transitions {#sec-intervention-authority-transitions}\n\nWhen a remote assistant takes manual control of the arm after the mobile base has slowed or stopped for a blocked aisle, the handover must commit on a single permission tick and expire automatically if the active proposer falls silent. The transfer names each actuator channel and assigns it to a single owner; linear blending of two competing proposers is no substitute for choosing who is in charge. The local enforcer remains the sole writer to the physical actuator bus, generating a checked transition profile only after an ownership change has committed on a control tick boundary.\n\nDiscrete handover is not the only way to share control. Shared autonomy systems keep both proposers active and blend their actions continuously. In standard policy-blending methods, the robot infers the operator&#x27;s intended goal and arbitrates between automated assistance and manual control; user studies show that this arbitration works only while the robot predicts the human&#x27;s goal with high confidence [@dragan2013policy]. That confidence breaks down when there is more than one path around an obstacle. When an operator steers left around a pallet while the autonomous policy steers right, a convex combination of two sensible commands produces a third trajectory that hits the obstacle. In those cases, a qualified arbiter must select or construct one admissible proposal; the permission path here selects a single source deterministically at each tick. Continuous blending works well when the task has a single clear goal and every intermediate blend is admissible, as when an operator makes fine adjustments to an end-effector pose. Even in purely manual operation, the safety enforcer acts as an invariant filter rather than a proposer, projecting every human command onto the admissible control set regardless of who holds the channel. Before ownership can move between sources, however, each source&#x27;s hold on a channel must be bounded in time so that a proposer that falls silent loses authority instead of leaving its last command in force.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The minimal-intervention principle, which keeps an admissible proposal and applies the smallest feasible correction otherwise, is established in @sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention.\n:::\n\n### Execution leases and three-position enabling hardware {#sec-intervention-enabling-hardware}\n\nA stalled policy and a disconnected teleoperator leave the same immediate problem, because in both cases the moving machine still holds a previously admitted command. An inference stall, lost wireless link, or incapacitated operator must not leave that command active indefinitely. Expiring the proposer&#x27;s authority must also select a local response that brakes the machine or holds the load.\n\nThe permission path bounds autonomous chunks and manual sessions with **execution leases**, enforced on the safety microcontroller&#x27;s clock. The autonomous lease is the running `[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.chunk_lease_str]` chunk lease. The manual lease is a separate value, chosen in @sec-intervention-arbiter-heartbeats and checked against the remaining clearance in @sec-intervention-session-admission. An authenticated renewal must also carry valid state and evidence, because a heartbeat alone cannot refresh old observations. A wireless console that carries these renewals is a cableless control system in the sense of IEC 62745, which specifies the functions and interfaces of radio and infrared links between operator control stations, including portable ones, and a machine&#x27;s control system [@iec62745]. Formally, let $\\LeaseRefreshInstant$ denote the local hardware timestamp of the most recent renewal accepted after authentication, freshness, state, and evidence validation. At physical time $\\TimeInstant$, the lease-age condition is:\n$$\\TimeInstant - \\LeaseRefreshInstant &lt; \\ChunkLease \\ \\text{(autonomous)}, \\qquad \\TimeInstant - \\LeaseRefreshInstant &lt; \\ManualLease \\ \\text{(manual)}.$$ {#eq-intervention-lease-age}\nThe clock advances toward expiry even when packets stop arriving. At expiry, the microcontroller revokes the affected proposal rights and selects the validated response from the current physical state. Authority can return only through the declared recovery and fresh-admission procedure; packet resumption alone cannot restore it.\n\nElectromechanical **enabling devices** provide an additional physical condition for manual motion, one that holds even when every network packet is valid. The industrial teach pendant in @fig-14-teach-pendant is the familiar carrier: a technician standing beside a robot uses it to jog the arm by hand, with a display, a jogging joystick, and a latched emergency-stop button on its face and an enabling grip on its rear handle. On the running machine, an enabling device belongs to a person beside the machine, such as the maintenance technician who jogs one arm joint from a pendant. The remote assistant&#x27;s console need not carry such a grip, because authenticated heartbeats maintain the manual lease, with presence assessed by the console&#x27;s chosen checks, which do not establish cognitive engagement (@sec-intervention-arbiter-heartbeats). A site may add an enabling grip to the console as a second condition. The grip that matters is a three-position one. A startled or distressed operator may release a grip or squeeze it harder, and a two-position device that enables whenever pressed does not detect the latter response. By contrast, a three-position grip enables motion only in its intermediate position (Position 2). Releasing it (Position 1) or squeezing beyond the center detent (Position 3) withdraws the enabling signal; the configured safety circuit determines the resulting stop. These dual-channel contacts request the stop selected by the machine&#x27;s safety circuit, independently of the Linux application processor and its scheduler. Where that response includes Safe Torque Off (STO, IEC 61800-5-2), STO disables torque generation; the configured braking or holding mechanism must still establish the required plant response. The enabling circuit must also prevent a return through Position 2 after an over-squeeze from automatically restoring motion.\n\nThe pendant&#x27;s red mushroom button is a different device with different semantics. Under ISO 13850, the emergency stop is a complementary protective measure, not a substitute for safeguarding or for the stop the risk assessment selects. It takes priority over every other function in every operating mode, it latches until an operator deliberately resets it at the device, and that reset must not restart the machine [@iso13850emergency]. Which physical response the stop invokes is a stop-category choice that @tbl-12-iec60204-stopping-categories maps to its evidence. The latch and the reset rule matter for supervisory control because they make a human stop sticky in exactly the way a network command is not. A remote packet can request a stop and a lease can lapse into one, but only the local reset of a latched device, followed by the fresh admission this chapter requires, returns the machine to motion.\n\n: **Manual Reduced Speed**: ISO 10218-1:2025 limits the tool speed of an industrial robot in manual reduced-speed mode to $250\\text{ mm/s}$ [@iso10218part1], the mode in which the pendant of @fig-14-teach-pendant couples motion to a three-position grip. Releasing or over-squeezing the grip only requests a stop from the safety circuit, and the performance level that circuit must reach, graded from PL a to PL e, is set by the machine&#x27;s risk assessment under ISO 13849-1 [@iso13849part1]. The grip therefore bounds how fast the machine may be moving when a startled operator lets go, not which stop follows.\n\n::: {#fig-14-teach-pendant fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Industrial teach pendant with three-position enabling grip**: Front view of an ABB teach pendant showing the touch display, manual jogging joystick, emergency-stop mushroom button, and industrial fieldbus cable. The three-position enabling grip described in the text sits on the rear handle, out of view. (Credit: [Auledas](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teach_Pendant_ABB.JPG), Wikimedia Commons, [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).)&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Front of an ABB industrial robot teach pendant with a display, keys, red emergency-stop actuator, and attached cable; rear enabling grip and safety wiring are not visible.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nEven while an operator holds an active lease and maintains Position 2 on the deadman grip, the deterministic enforcer evaluates human setpoints against the same barrier certificates, current limits, and kinematic envelopes applied to the learned neural policy. If an operator panics and commands a high-speed steering angle that would induce dynamic chassis rollover, or requests a joint torque that would shear harmonic drive gear teeth, the enforcer clamps or rejects the command. A human command therefore remains a proposal, and the third law, proposal is not permission (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-proposal-permission}), binds human supervisors and neural models alike. Intervention transfers the right to propose away from the learned policy, while the right to admit stays with the permission path. With every owner&#x27;s authority bounded by a lease, what remains to design is how the right to propose changes hands, at which control tick it commits, and what the machine does when a network handover stalls.\n\n### Authority states and single-writer actuator ownership {#sec-intervention-discrete-states}\n\nIf the autonomous policy and the remote console both write to a motor drive while a handover is in flight, the drive receives alternating setpoints on consecutive ticks. To prevent two sources from commanding an actuator at once, the arbiter resolves authority over each channel at every discrete time tick $\\TimeInstant$ to a single, mutually exclusive state:\n$$\\ChannelAuthorityState_t \\in \\{\\text{Autonomous}, \\text{Handover-Pending}, \\text{Blend}, \\text{Human-Manual}, \\text{Fallback}, \\text{Protective}\\}.$$\nThese states separate proposer identity from actuator access. Each carries a short code name, used in the per-tick record and in @fig-14-authority-handshake-fsm, and each names who holds the proposal token on the channel:\n\n- `Autonomous` (`AUTO`): The learned model holds the proposal token and proposes setpoints, while the local enforcer verifies barrier certificates ($\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\ge 0$) on every control cycle before dispatching commands.\n- `Handover-Pending` (`PEND`): A takeover handshake is negotiating across the network; the policy keeps the token and continues proposing commands under a strict clearance-derived countdown timer $\\HandoverTimeout$. Active lease and evidence expiry run concurrently with that timer, so the arbiter dispatches fallback at the earliest of the protocol, lease, evidence, and clearance deadlines.\n- `Blend` (`BLEND`, enforcer-owned bridge): No proposer holds the token. The enforcer assumes exclusive bus ownership, driving a smooth, $\\Glyph{C}^2$-continuous quintic interpolation between the latched autonomous setpoint and the validated human target setpoint.\n- `Human-Manual` (`MAN`): The authenticated teleoperator holds the proposal token, streaming commands that pass through enforcer barrier checks under an active manual lease ($\\ManualLease$).\n- `Fallback` (`FALLBACK`): Entered when an active lease expires, communication drops, or no admissible continuation remains. No proposer holds the token; the arbiter selects the validated HOLD or STOP rung for the measured state without waiting for remote commands. An ordinary least-change correction, by contrast, is enforcement within the current state and leaves the token with the current proposer.\n- `Protective` (`PROTECTIVE`): Entered directly from an independent hardwired protective input or a critical silicon fault. No proposer holds the token. The safety circuit selects the validated protective stop; Safe Torque Off (STO) disables torque generation, and any required load-holding mechanism must be qualified for the current state and surviving resources.\n\nAt no point do two software masters write to an actuator simultaneously. Even during the `Blend` state, the enforcer alone owns the actuator channel, executing a precomputed profile between the latched references and validating dynamic constraints against the current state on each tick. Each edge in @tbl-14-authority-state-matrix therefore names its guard and deadline, its single actuator owner, and the fallback taken when the guard fails or an active lease lapses.\n\n| From → to                                           | Guard and deadline                                                                                      | Actuator owner / command                                                                                                                 | Failure response                                           |\n|:----------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Autonomous → Handover-Pending                       | Policy requests supervision; deadline computed from clearance                                           | Enforcer admits policy proposals while feasible                                                                                          | Validated fallback if deadline reached                     |\n| Autonomous or Handover-Pending → Blend              | Authenticated operator ACK, fresh state, feasible target and stopping reserve; commit on a control tick | At commit, revoke the policy&#x27;s proposal token, latch last approved command and checked manual target; **enforcer alone** writes actuator | Deny invalid target; retain valid active mode or fall back |\n| Blend → Human-Manual                                | Profile completes and manual lease remains valid                                                        | Enforcer admits live human proposals within physical limits                                                                              | Validated fallback on lease or enabling-device loss        |\n| Handover-Pending, Blend, or Human-Manual → Fallback | Timeout, stale lease, or invalid state                                                                  | The permission path executes the validated HOLD or STOP rung and records it                                                              | Re-arm only after validated recovery procedure             |\n| Any state → Protective                              | Independent protective input or qualified critical fault                                                | Safety circuit/controller selects plant-specific protective stop                                                                         | Validated brake or hold, not torque removal alone          |\n\n: **Authority state machine transition matrix**: Each row names the single actuator owner and the state-dependent fallback. The example uses a `[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.tick_str]` permission tick. The `BLEND` state interpolates only latched, checked endpoints, under enforcer ownership. {#tbl-14-authority-state-matrix tbl-colwidths=&quot;[16,28,31,25]&quot;}\n\n### The four-phase takeover handshake {#sec-intervention-handshake-protocol}\n\nIf a single network packet could switch a channel directly from `Autonomous` to `Human-Manual`, a delayed request could hand the actuators to an operator whose view of the aisle is already stale. With the same single packet, a dropped reply could leave the operator and the machine disagreeing on who is driving. Moving a channel between states instead takes a deterministic **four-phase takeover protocol** (`Request` $\\to$ `Acknowledge` $\\to$ `Commit` $\\to$ `Confirm`) run by the safety microcontroller. This four-stage exchange separates the local hardware commit from the physical blend and from the final release of manual proposal rights, checks cryptographic freshness so replayed frames cannot trigger a switch, and changes ownership on a single tick boundary:\n\n1. **Notice (`Request` phase)**: Either the human supervisor notices policy drift and requests an override (for example, by pressing an enabling button or moving a joystick past its calibrated deadband), or the autonomous policy hits an epistemic uncertainty spike and requests teleoperation assistance. Upon receipt, the arbiter initializes the clearance-derived countdown timer $\\HandoverTimeout = \\max(0, \\min(\\HandoverTimeoutCeiling, \\FallbackDeadline))$ of @eq-intervention-latest-fallback.\n2. **Declutch (`Acknowledge` phase)**: The enforcer validates target prerequisites across the system. It verifies that sensor telemetry is fresh, network links are live, and the current plant state $\\MechanicalPlantState(\\TimeInstant)$ retains sufficient stopping reserve to brake safely if the transfer aborts. It then captures a synchronous state snapshot before preparing to revoke the autonomous policy&#x27;s proposal authority.\n3. **Transition (`Commit` phase)**: At the next synchronous control tick boundary, the enforcer executes the state transition. It revokes the policy&#x27;s proposal token on the committed channels, latches the last approved policy setpoint alongside the checked manual target setpoint, and assigns sole write authority to the enforcer in the `Blend` state. The enforcer drives the $\\Glyph{C}^2$-continuous transition profile, continuously checking that the trajectory remains feasible under measured plant dynamics.\n4. **Notification (`Confirm` phase)**: The permission path broadcasts confirmation of the committed owner and the active `Blend` state to the operator console. A dropped confirmation packet alarms the operator interface, but does not undo or stall the local commit on the robot. After the physical profile completes under valid state, sufficient stopping reserve, and an active manual lease, the arbiter separately grants the proposal token on the committed channels to the operator and transitions to `Human-Manual`. This release timing is dictated by physical profile completion rather than the network notification round trip.\n\nEach phase in @tbl-14-handshake-protocol carries a strictly budgeted bound, an explicit guard and owner, and deterministic failure handling. The transaction rejects spoofed override attempts and applies the failure response assigned to its current phase. Lost confirmation does not reverse a committed transfer; lease expiry or a failed physical guard invokes the validated state-dependent fallback.\n\n| Phase / takeover stage    |                                         Budgeted bound | Guard and owner                                                                                                | Failure handling                                                              |\n|:--------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------:|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1. Request (Notice)       |     `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_request_str]` | Authenticated role, checked nonce, and fresh clock-converted timestamp; no owner change                        | Reject invalid packet; keep current qualified mode                            |\n| 2. Acknowledge (Declutch) | `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_acknowledge_str]` | Check state, manual target, and full stop reserve; isolate write path                                          | Reject request; invoke fallback if current mode cannot continue               |\n| 3. Commit (Transition)    |     `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.tick_str]` tick | Revoke the policy&#x27;s proposal token, latch both checked endpoints, assign **enforcer** sole actuator write path | No partial commit within the requested channel mask; fallback on failed guard |\n| 4. Confirm (notification) |     `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_confirm_str]` | Announce committed `BLEND`; later announce `MAN` after profile and lease check                                 | Lost notice alarms operator but does not undo commit                          |\n\n: **Four-phase takeover timing**: The phase budgets sum to `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_handshake_str]`. This sum budgets the exchange through commit notification, not physical blending or release of manual proposal rights. The blend begins at commit and may continue after notification. {#tbl-14-handshake-protocol tbl-colwidths=&quot;[15,16,40,29]&quot;}\n\n### Arbiter execution and manual-lease heartbeats {#sec-intervention-arbiter-heartbeats}\n\nOnce a channel reaches $\\text{Human-Manual}$, a dropped wireless link or a distracted assistant who leaves the console would let the last manual command keep running unless the machine checks that the operator is still present. The safety microcontroller bounds that risk with a hardware-timed **manual lease**. Depending on the console, presence detection may use a capacitive grip sensor, eye tracking, or continuous joystick deflection past a deadband, chosen for their sensor coverage and false-drop rates. Manual authority is sustained exclusively through authenticated keep-alive heartbeats streamed from the operator console to the safety microcontroller. The manual lease $\\ManualLease$ of @sec-intervention-enabling-hardware is the longest silence the microcontroller tolerates between them. Two bounds set its length. From below, the lease must outlast the heartbeat period plus the link&#x27;s jitter and at least one lost packet, or ordinary wireless loss would drop a healthy operator into fallback. That floor is why the heartbeats must run at a rate several times the lease&#x27;s inverse. From above, the lease replaces the chunk lease inside $\\PrebrakeDelay$ while the operator holds `MAN`, so the travel it adds over the chunk lease, $\\SpeedAtDetection(\\ManualLease - \\ChunkLease)$, must fit the clearance left after the takeover. The reference implementation chooses `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.manual_lease_str]`. @Sec-intervention-session-admission checks that value against the upper bound at the in-loop request speed, and only the site&#x27;s measured heartbeat loss can show that it clears the lower one.\n\nEach heartbeat packet carries a strictly increasing 64-bit sequence counter $\\HeartbeatSequenceCounter$, a monotonic hardware timestamp $\\HeartbeatTimestamp_k$, an authenticated operator credential, and a keyed message authentication code (AES-128 CMAC, evaluated in @sec-intervention-authenticating-intervention). In the following age calculation, $\\HeartbeatTimestamp_k$ represents the sender&#x27;s timestamp translated into the local clock domain. The receiver tracks the bounded clock-synchronization uncertainty interval and computes freshness relative to the oldest plausible timestamp; an unqualified clock mapping cannot safely establish packet freshness. The nominal local-clock age is:\n$$\\HeartbeatAge = \\HeartbeatReceiptTime - \\HeartbeatTimestamp_k.$$\nThese fields combine communication checks used in black-channel safety architectures [@iec61784-3-2021] with cryptographic security over an untrusted wireless link. The sequence counter detects transmission loss and replay attacks. The timestamp and bounded lease act as a communication watchdog against excessive latency. The cryptographic tag authenticates the claimed credential. The safety communication checks and the security mechanism require separate qualification.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The proposal header that applies the same black-channel safeguards to every learned proposal is established in @sec-nervous-supervisory-timers.\n:::\n\nA heartbeat packet may renew authority only while its conservative age falls within the configured freshness window. The active manual lease expires deterministically on the local hardware clock at its committed deadline; stale or out-of-order network traffic cannot extend that horizon. Similarly, an autonomous action chunk expires strictly according to its admitted local deadline. A packet that fails cryptographic verification is immediately discarded. It cannot renew the lease, but it does not revoke currently valid authority either; the existing timer runs down on its own. Upon lease expiration, the microcontroller automatically revokes the operator&#x27;s proposal token, drops into `Fallback`, and executes a pre-validated holding or stopping trajectory. Once a lease lapses, the state machine prohibits any immediate return to $\\text{Autonomous}$ or $\\text{Human-Manual}$. The robot must satisfy strict recovery conditions: it must come to a complete mechanical standstill, resolve and acknowledge the relevant faults without erasing their evidence, and undergo an authenticated four-phase re-admission handshake initiated from rest.\n\nWhen multiple control sources propose concurrent commands, the permission path resolves candidate selection through a fixed priority hierarchy evaluated deterministically every `[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.tick_str]` control cycle on the machine side of the proposal boundary. This mechanism adopts the layered priority structure of subsumption architectures [@brooks1986robust], but applies it strictly to input source selection rather than behavioral synthesis. The independent enforcer then admits, modifies, or rejects the winning candidate to ensure compliance with configured physical limits. In the $\\text{Human-Manual}$ state with a valid lease, the human proposal takes precedence over learned model proposals, while remaining bounded by barrier certificates and actuator limits. In $\\text{Autonomous}$, the learned policy generates candidate actions for safety screening. Because fixed-state arbitration requires bounded work per tick ($\\mathcal{O}(1)$ computational complexity), it eliminates runtime negotiation or dynamic bargaining between software proposers.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The proposal boundary, above which a learned component may only propose and below which the permission path alone admits commands, is established in @sec-boundary-machine-model.\n:::\n\nTwo properties of the protocol show only when the states and handshake are drawn together (@fig-14-authority-handshake-fsm). Every active state has an edge to `Fallback`, so no failure of the network or the operator can leave a channel without a defined owner, and the only edge out of `Fallback` is a reset from standstill. The hardwired `Protective` input sits outside the protocol entirely and preempts every state.\n\n::: {#fig-14-authority-handshake-fsm data-explore=&quot;original-v4-14-authority-handshake-fsm&quot; data-explore-key=&quot;vol4:fig-14-authority-handshake-fsm&quot; fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Authority states and their guards**: Each box is one value of the per-channel authority state, with its code name above its plain name. A handover runs from request through acknowledgment to a commit on a control tick; from then on the enforcer owns the blend between latched endpoints, and manual proposals begin only when the profile completes with a live manual lease. Timeout, lease lapse, or an invalid state leads to the validated fallback, whose only exit is a reset from rest with a fresh re-admission. The hardwired protective input preempts every state.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;State diagram with six boxes. Autonomous, Handover-Pending, Blend, Human-Manual, and Fallback sit inside a dashed enclosure. Arrows run from Autonomous to Handover-Pending on request, from Handover-Pending to Blend on acknowledgment and commit, directly from Autonomous to Blend on an operator acknowledgment, and from Blend to Human-Manual when the profile completes with a valid manual lease. Arrows from Autonomous, Handover-Pending, Blend, and Human-Manual lead to Fallback on lease expiry, timeout, invalid state, or lease lapse. A dashed arrow returns from Fallback to Autonomous on reset from rest. One arrow from the enclosure leads to the Protective state on a hardwired protective input or critical fault.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nHere the state machine decouples physical bus ownership from protocol progress. An acknowledgment prepares the transition. The local hardware commit (`REVOKE_PROPOSAL_TOKEN()`) strips the autonomous policy of its proposal token on the committed channels and grants the enforcer sole ownership of the blend. The enforcer admits live manual proposals only after the interpolation profile completes under an active manual lease. An unconfirmed transition therefore leaves only the console uncertain, as the `Confirm` phase states. A delayed handover request or a dropped heartbeat, in contrast, runs down its local timer and invokes the validated fallback without waiting for network consensus.\n\nAssigning a single writer resolves authority unambiguously at each control tick, but an instantaneous switch can still produce hazardous dynamic transients. If the autonomous policy&#x27;s last admitted joint torque diverges significantly from the operator&#x27;s initial target, directly swapping setpoints across a single tick boundary requests an unachievable step change in torque, risking mechanical shock or actuator saturation. The enforcer avoids this discontinuity by using the latched boundary conditions to generate a dynamically feasible interpolation profile that preserves stopping margins throughout the transition.\n\n::: {#chk-intervention-authority-transitions-and-timeout-bounds .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Authority transitions and timeout bounds&quot;}\n\nBefore moving from who owns each channel to how its command changes value, verify your understanding of leases, transition state machines, and timeout dynamics:\n\n- [ ] **State-dependent fallback**: Can you explain why the moving base and the loaded arm need different validated responses when a lease lapses, and why Safe Torque Off alone provides neither?\n- [ ] **Finite-state transition semantics**: Can you trace the deterministic progression among the Autonomous, Handover-Pending, Blend, Human-Manual, and Fallback states, and name the guard and the single actuator owner on each edge?\n- [ ] **Timeout bound**: At the aisle speed the configured protocol ceiling is `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.config_str]` and the clearance-derived deadline after charging the dispatch-to-admission allocation is `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle_str]`. Can you state which one sets $\\HandoverTimeout$, by how many milliseconds it is the tighter, and how many whole `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.tick_str]` permission ticks the arbiter may wait before it dispatches fallback?\n- [ ] **Asymmetric preemption hierarchy**: Can you explain why the qualified local protection path must meet its measured deadline, while restoring autonomous authority requires a fresh admission handshake?\n- [ ] **Fallback on a lapsed handover**: Can you explain why a lapsed or unacknowledged handover goes to the validated stop rung rather than back to the degraded policy that requested it?\n\n*If any transition paths permit unhandled timeouts or undefined intermediate states, the authority protocol is vulnerable to mode confusion and runaway actuation. Review this section before proceeding to smooth command blending.*\n:::\n\n## Transfer Without Discontinuity {#sec-intervention-bumpless-transfer}\n\nAt commit time $\\TimeInstant_0$, the enforcer latches the last admitted policy command $\\ControlCommand_0$ and a checked human target $\\ControlCommand_1$. Switching them in one permission tick may exceed the channel&#x27;s allowed command rate. In classical process control, bumpless transfer between manual and automatic modes is achieved by making the inactive controller track the active one&#x27;s output, so its internal state, including its integrator, already agrees at the switch [@astrom2006advanced]. @Sec-intervention-integrator-prebiasing applies that tracking to the console-side controller that turns operator input into a command. Here **bumpless transfer** also names a second mechanism layered on top, which spreads the remaining command change across a finite window while matching the required endpoint derivatives. The quintic seam bridges of @sec-planning-quintic-splines provide the construction; takeover adds a new admission decision because waiting for the operator has already consumed some clearance.\n\n### Command steps and mechanical drivetrain shock {#sec-intervention-command-steps}\n\nA step change in torque or acceleration excites torsional resonances and snaps gearboxes across their backlash, while a slower blend lowers the command rate at the cost of stopping reserve. Because the $\\PowerRailInductance\\,d\\ConductorCurrent/d\\TimeInstant$ coupling to the DC supply also turns a current step into a voltage sag on the shared rail, the command transition must satisfy the drive&#x27;s rate limits while leaving enough clearance for fallback. A smooth profile therefore needs both a rate bound and a check against the remaining physical envelope.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The $\\PowerRailInductance\\,d\\ConductorCurrent/d\\TimeInstant$ coupling through which a current step sags the shared DC bus is established in @sec-body-power-distribution-impedance.\n:::\n\n::: {#dfn-intervention-c-bumpless-authority-transfer-ramp .callout-definition title=&quot;C² bumpless authority transfer ramp&quot;}\n\n***$\\Glyph{C}^2$ bumpless authority transfer ramp***^2$ bumpless authority transfer ramp!definition} is the enforcer-owned command profile between two checked, latched references $\\ControlCommand_0$ and $\\ControlCommand_1$. It is parameterized by a monotonic weighting function $\\BumplessTransferBlend(\\TimeInstant) \\in [0,1]$ with vanishing first and second boundary derivatives ($\\BumplessTransferBlendRate = \\BumplessTransferBlendAcceleration = 0$) over a finite blending window $\\BlendingTransitionWindow$. The profile keeps that command and its first two derivatives continuous across the fixed-endpoint handoff. When the command is acceleration, its first derivative is commanded jerk; when it is torque, the first derivative is torque rate.\n\n1.  **Significance**: A setpoint step has an unbounded ideal derivative. A $\\Glyph{C}^2$ command profile removes that discontinuity; measured actuator tracking and plant limits still decide physical feasibility.\n2.  **Distinction**: Unlike a linear interpolation ramp whose derivative jumps at both endpoints, a $\\Glyph{C}^2$ quintic spline enforces vanishing first and second derivatives ($\\BumplessTransferBlendRate = \\BumplessTransferBlendAcceleration = 0$) at entry and exit, making the interpolated command and its first two derivatives continuous for stationary endpoint derivatives. The physical meaning of those derivatives depends on the command channel.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Choosing a window shorter than the rate limit allows for a fixed acceleration-command change (the bound @eq-intervention-blend-window derives below) breaches the stated command-jerk limit even though the profile is smooth. Resonance, slip, and stopping distance require plant tests.\n\n:::\n\n### Sizing the blend window and the stopping-distance cost {#sec-intervention-blend-window}\n\nSmoothing an acceleration command from cruising speed to emergency braking delays the onset of maximum deceleration, so bumpless transfer consumes clearance. It trades stopping reserve for lower mechanical stress, and sizing the blend window means balancing the actuator&#x27;s jerk limit against the clearance that remains.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ BUMPLESS TRANSFER ON THE MACHINE'S BASE AND ARM CHANNELS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-bumpless-transfer (the smoothstep bound, the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          base stop example, the one-tick step), the tau_blend sentence of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-intervention-authority-log, and @tbl-14-authority-timing-contract.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the enforcer's blend window on the mobile manipulator and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          price the stopping reserve a blend uses.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Base at the in-loop request speed: a stop blended from cruise to</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          a_brake under the resident stop's jerk bound; window, blend and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          total distance, penalty against a step and against the resident</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          jerk-limited stop. Reserve use: the largest growth, over the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          blend's ticks, of travel plus the resident stop from the blended</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          speed. Arm: a joint-torque change under a torque-rate ceiling.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Fixed-endpoint smoothstep, peak slope 1.875</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (@sec-appendix-control-splines); jerk bound = peak jerk of the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          reference C2 polynomial at the drive limit, the same stipulated</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          bound the resident S-curve stop uses (@sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          resident stop by calc_scurve_stop_distance; arm window by</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          calc_quintic_blend_duration; windows rounded up to whole</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          permission ticks (RM.permission_rate via AISLE.t_tick); all in</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseTakeoverBrakingBlend (BrakingBlendModel).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          InterventionAssistantSession compares the reserve use with the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          remainder left after the in-loop takeover.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond, newton from mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_scurve_stop_distance, calc_quintic_blend_duration from mlsysim.fmt import ( fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, fmt_jerk, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_torque, fmt_torque_rate, check, )</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionBlendBudget: \"\"\"Fixed-endpoint smoothstep blends on the machine's base and arm.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>blend = Scenarios.WarehouseTakeoverBrakingBlend  # base blend at the in-loop request speed, and an arm joint v0 = blend.speed  # category: chosen (speed at which the in-loop takeover commits) a_target = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative (canon a_brake; the base's blended step) j_allowable = AISLE.j_brake  # category: derived (stipulated jerk bound) tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen (from RM.permission_rate) arm_torque_max = RM.arm.max_torque  # category: datasheet arm_delta = blend.torque_change  # category: illustrative (an arm-joint command change) arm_rate = blend.torque_rate_limit  # category: chosen (torque-command rate ceiling)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>r = blend.result d_res = r.scurve_travel.to(meter) t_ramp = r.ramp_time.to(millisecond)  # the resident stop's own jerk ramp</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">Peak smoothstep slope 1.875 for fixed endpoints (@sec-appendix-control-splines).</h1>\n<p>T = r.window.to(second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">The enforcer blends in whole permission ticks, so the window rounds up.</h1>\n<p>T_ticks = r.window_ticks T_budget = r.window_budget.to(millisecond) step_jerk = r.step_jerk.to(meter / second**3)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">Integral alpha ds = 1/2; integral (1-s) alpha ds = 1/7.</h1>\n<p>v1 = r.exit_speed.to(meter / second) d_blend = r.blend_travel.to(meter) d_after = r.after_travel.to(meter) d_total = r.blended_stop.to(meter) d_ideal = r.step_stop.to(meter) d_penalty = r.penalty_over_step.to(meter) d_over_res = r.excess_over_scurve.to(meter) excess_in = r.reserve_use.to(millimeter) arm_blend = r.arm_window.to(millisecond) arm_ticks = r.arm_window_ticks arm_budget = r.arm_window_budget.to(millisecond)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(j_allowable.to(meter / second**3).magnitude - 64 / 9) &lt; 1e-9, f\"stipulated jerk bound: {j_allowable}\") check(abs(T.to(millisecond).magnitude - 527.34375) &lt; 1e-6, f\"base blend window: {T}\") check(T_budget.magnitude == 528, f\"base blend in whole ticks: {T_budget}\") check(T &gt; t_ramp, \"The smoothstep must take longer than the resident stop's linear jerk ramp.\") check(v1.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The base must still be moving when the blend ends.\") check(abs(d_blend.to(millimeter).magnitude - 316.05) &lt; 5e-3, f\"distance during the blend: {d_blend}\") check(abs(d_total.to(millimeter).magnitude - 328.45) &lt; 5e-3, f\"blended stop: {d_total}\") check(abs(d_penalty.to(millimeter).magnitude - 187.82) &lt; 5e-3, f\"penalty against a step: {d_penalty}\") check(abs(d_over_res.to(millimeter).magnitude - 82.35) &lt; 5e-3, f\"blended stop beyond the resident stop: {d_over_res}\") check(d_over_res.magnitude &gt; 0, \"A stop through the blend must be longer than the resident stop.\") check(abs((d_res - calc_scurve_stop_distance(v0, a_target, j_allowable)).to(meter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, \"Resident stop matches MLSysIM.\") check( abs((arm_blend - calc_quintic_blend_duration(arm_delta, arm_rate)).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Arm window matches the quintic blend bound.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_blended_stop_distance_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 140.6, 246.1, 328.4 mm</h1>\n<p>check( round(d_ideal.to(millimeter).magnitude, 1) == 140.6 and round(d_res.to(millimeter).magnitude, 1) == 246.1, f\"stops in the fig-alt: {d_ideal}, {d_res}\", ) check(excess_in.magnitude &gt; d_over_res.to(millimeter).magnitude, \"Reserve use must cover at least the final excess.\") check(abs(arm_blend.magnitude - 37.5) &lt; 1e-9, f\"arm blend window: {arm_blend}\") check(arm_budget.magnitude == 38, f\"arm blend in whole ticks: {arm_budget}\") check(arm_delta &lt; arm_torque_max, \"The arm command change must sit inside the joint torque rating.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v0_str = fmt_velocity(v0, unit=meter / second, precision=2) delta_a_str = fmt_acceleration(a_target, unit=meter / second<strong>2, precision=0) j_allowable_str = fmt_jerk(j_allowable, unit=meter / second</strong>3, precision=2) t_ramp_str = fmt_latency(t_ramp, unit=millisecond, precision=2) T_str = fmt_latency(T, unit=millisecond, precision=2) T_budget_str = fmt_latency(T_budget, unit=millisecond, precision=0) tick_str = fmt_latency(tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0) step_jerk_str = fmt_jerk(step_jerk, unit=meter / second*<em>3, precision=0, commas=True) v1_str = fmt_velocity(v1, unit=meter / second, precision=2) d_blend_str = fmt_length(d_blend, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_after_str = fmt_length(d_after, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_total_str = fmt_length(d_total, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_ideal_str = fmt_length(d_ideal, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_penalty_str = fmt_length(d_penalty, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_res_str = fmt_length(d_res, unit=millimeter, precision=1) d_over_res_str = fmt_length(d_over_res, unit=millimeter, precision=1) excess_in_str = fmt_length(excess_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1) arm_delta_str = fmt_torque(arm_delta, unit=newton </em> meter, precision=0) arm_rate_str = fmt_torque_rate(arm_rate, precision=0) arm_blend_str = fmt_latency(arm_blend, unit=millisecond, precision=1) arm_budget_str = fmt_latency(arm_budget, unit=millisecond, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>The enforcer blends with the quintic smoothstep. For normalized progress $\\BumplessTransferProgress = (\\TimeInstant - \\BumplessTransferStartInstant) / \\BlendingTransitionWindow \\in [0, 1]$, the polynomial weighting function is: $$\\BumplessTransferBlend(\\BumplessTransferProgress) = 10\\BumplessTransferProgress^3 - 15\\BumplessTransferProgress^4 + 6\\BumplessTransferProgress^5.$$ {#eq-intervention-quintic-smoothstep} The polynomial in @eq-intervention-quintic-smoothstep is the fixed-endpoint result derived in @sec-appendix-control-splines. Its derivative $\\BumplessTransferBlendSlope(\\BumplessTransferProgress) = 30\\BumplessTransferProgress^2(1-\\BumplessTransferProgress)^2$ peaks at $\\BumplessTransferProgress = 1/2$ with value $30/16 = 1.875$, so the profile's peak slope is $1.875/\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ and an acceleration change must satisfy: $$\\BlendingTransitionWindow \\ge \\frac{1.875 \\lVert \\FiniteChange \\ControlCommand \\rVert}{\\JerkLimit}.$$ {#eq-intervention-blend-window}</p>\n<p>For a torque command, replace the acceleration difference and jerk ceiling with the torque difference and torque-rate ceiling. The illustrative <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_delta_str]</code> joint-torque change under a <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_rate_str]</code> ceiling therefore requires <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_blend_str]</code>, rounded up to <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_budget_str]</code> on the permission clock. These bounds assume stationary endpoint derivatives; a transfer from a moving ramp must match its boundary state explicitly.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Nonzero Boundary Derivatives</strong>: The $1.875/\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ peak derivative factor assumes stationary endpoint derivatives. A general quintic can match the command value, first derivative, and second derivative at both endpoints, including nonzero values (@sec-planning-quintic-splines). Its interior rate extrema must then be checked; the fixed-endpoint smoothstep factor does not automatically apply.</p>\n<p>Increasing $\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ reduces the profile's peak commanded jerk, but delays the full human target and can lengthen the stopping trajectory. Consider the remote assistant's takeover of the mobile manipulator's base, committed while the machine cruises at the $\\SpeedAtDetection =$ <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.v0_str]</code> it slowed to before requesting it (@sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines). Suppose the assistant commands a stop at the scenario's braking deceleration, a fixed step of $\\lvert \\FiniteChange \\BaseAccelerationCommand \\rvert =$ <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.delta_a_str]</code> from the latched cruise command. The enforcer holds the blend to $\\JerkLimit =$ <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.j_allowable_str]</code>, the jerk bound of the resident jerk-limited stop. That bound is a stipulated property of the loaded base, not of its speed, so the blend window does not depend on the speed at which the takeover commits. Measured load response still requires qualification. The smoothstep bound then sets a minimum blending window of <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.T_str]</code>, longer than the <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.t_ramp_str]</code> the resident stop spends ramping its deceleration at the same jerk bound, because the smoothstep reaches its peak slope only at mid-window. The smoothstep weight averages one half over the window, so the base would leave the blend at <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.v1_str]</code> after traveling <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_blend_str]</code>. Integrating the smoothstep weight a first time subtracts one half of $\\lvert \\FiniteChange \\BaseAccelerationCommand \\rvert\\,\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ from the entry speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$, whereas integrating that speed profile across the window subtracts only one seventh of $\\lvert \\FiniteChange \\BaseAccelerationCommand \\rvert\\,\\BlendingTransitionWindow^2$ from the unbraked travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ because most of the deceleration occurs in the second half of the ramp. Braking from there at the full target would take another <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_after_str]</code>, for <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_total_str]</code> to zero speed in this partial model. It holds full deceleration until rest, then switches acceleration to zero instantaneously. The value is therefore a lower-bound comparison for the ramp followed by ideal braking, not a complete smooth stopping trajectory. A feasible terminal transition needs its own distance and boundary-state check.</p>\n<p>An instantaneous step takeover would stop in <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_ideal_str]</code>, so even this partial ramp-and-brake model adds <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_penalty_str]</code> of travel. The partial model is also <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_over_res_str]</code> longer than the <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.d_res_str]</code> resident stop from the same speed. For the one-dimensional model starting at zero acceleration and ending at rest with zero acceleration, the resident profile is a minimum-distance stop under the stated deceleration and jerk bounds (@sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix), so a different complete transition into braking must be checked against that baseline. This partial comparison lacks the required terminal transition and is not itself a competing complete profile. For a braking command the resident stop is already the bumpless transfer. The base's blend window therefore governs base command changes that do not stop the machine, such as the first manual command after an automatic stop, where the blend starts from rest and the enforcer still checks the resident stop from every blended state. An idealized step stop is mathematically shorter. Executing an unblended emergency stop, however, risks wheel lockup, dynamic rollover, or load shedding (such as throwing a loaded tote off the manipulator rack), which can increase the actual physical stopping distance beyond the ideal theoretical rigid-body stop. Command smoothing limits the requested rate; physical stability and sufficient clearance still require qualification. The enforcer admits each blended tick only while the resident stop from the resulting state still fits. Where no blend fits, the base receives the resident stop itself, the suffix the fallback ladder's stop rung executes. Bypassing the blend window by applying the acceleration step within a single <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.tick_str]</code> permission tick would instead request a command jerk of about <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.step_jerk_str]</code> over that tick. The idealized step is only a comparison baseline.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Initial condition matching and integrator pre-biasing</h3>\n<p>Command blending removes the chosen output discontinuity, but it cannot remove one that a controller upstream of the blend produces. On the remote assistant's side that controller is the console-side tracking loop, the software that turns joystick deflection, or a measured guiding force (the force-guided input that @sec-intervention-impedance-admittance describes next), into a velocity target for the channel. While the policy still owns the channel, that loop runs against a plant it does not drive, and its integrator accumulates the difference between its target and a motion that never follows. At commit it would emit that accumulated correction as a jump in the manual target, a step the blend window was never sized to absorb. <strong>Initial condition matching</strong> pre-biases that loop against the measured state before commit, so its internal controller state matches the output required at transfer rather than releasing accumulated error. It does not force the deliberate human target to equal the last policy command. The enforcer latches both endpoints, sizes the blend for their actual difference, and checks the resulting command and stopping reserve throughout transfer.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Mid-Blend Re-Intervention Abort</strong>: If an operator commands a secondary protective stop while a bumpless transfer blend is actively underway ($0 &lt; \\BumplessTransferProgress &lt; 1$), the enforcer does not wait for $\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ to complete. It uses the measured plant position, velocity, and acceleration, together with the active command and their uncertainty, to select a qualified state-matched fallback. A new polynomial seeded only from the intermediate command does not establish that the physical plant can execute it.</p>\n<p>To verify a transfer, record the command profile, motor current, encoder motion, and chassis inertial response on a shared timebase with bounded alignment error. In the ideal model, a step command has an infinite derivative at the switch, which excites high jerk, unmodeled compliance, and drivetrain ringing in physical hardware. A fixed-endpoint quintic polynomial removes the command discontinuity by zeroing the boundary derivatives, so its command rate stays bounded across the whole window $\\BlendingTransitionWindow$ instead of concentrating in one impulse (@fig-14-bumpless-transfer-dynamics). The measured plant response, not the command profile, establishes whether the handover met its physical limits.</p>\n<p>The two panels distinguish the command's mathematical continuity from the plant's measured response. Removing the step bounds the requested rate under the stated endpoint assumptions; it does not establish that the drive tracks the command or that the load remains stable. Those measurements decide whether the selected blend window is acceptable.</p>\n<p>The enforcer's <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.permission_rate_str]</code> loop gathers the four-phase handshake, integrator pre-biasing, and the $\\Glyph{C}^2$ quintic blend into one protocol on the safety microcontroller. Each tick it samples plant state, stopping reserve, protective inputs, and the active execution lease. It commits an ownership change only on the qualified commit tick that follows a verified acknowledgment, blends only between latched endpoints and checks every blended candidate before dispatch, and falls back on a lapsed lease or a failed clearance check.</p>\n<p>↪ <strong>Protocol:</strong> The enforcer's cadence, memory and write invariants, and per-state execution steps are given in @sec-appendix-systems-authority-protocol.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Mapping operator input to arm motion</h3>\n<p>Once the operator holds the arm channel, commanding a rigid position target into a bin wall or rack upright builds contact force until the enforcer trips, whereas guiding the arm through measured contact force lets the wrist comply. Position streaming supplies kinematic targets directly; force guiding uses measured contact force to generate a motion target through an admittance controller [@hogan1985impedance]. Either mapping remains subject to the arm's qualified limits. The choice also changes the logged learning target, so the record must retain whether a sample is a requested pose, a force measurement, or an admitted command.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Admittance and Impedance Control</strong>: @hogan1985impedance framed interaction control as programming the relation between force and motion at the contact. An impedance controller answers imposed motion with a commanded force; an admittance controller is its dual, turning a measured force into commanded motion, which suits stiff, highly geared arms whose position loops are accurate but which cannot render force directly. On such an arm a force-guided target is still a motion command, so it passes the same enforcer checks as a streamed pose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">Bilateral teleoperation and haptic feedback passivity</h3>\n<p>When the interface also reflects contact wrenches back to the operator instead of sending commands in one direction only, a force-reflecting console adds an actuated device at the operator's hands. Delayed feedback can destabilize that coupled loop even when authority ownership is correct [@anderson1989bilateral]. Such a console therefore needs a separate interaction-energy argument, including its port conventions, controller sampling, delay and loss assumptions, and actuator saturation. The energy-storage condition and wave-variable construction are developed in @sec-appendix-control-passivity; they do not replace the clearance or force limits. The basic warehouse takeover does not require bilateral force reflection. If a site adds it, pre-biasing and ramping the console's force output must prevent a command step at the operator's hand within its own measured limits, because the arm's accepted blend duration does not automatically qualify the console. Whichever input mapping and console a site chooses, the session that carries them must still fit the clearance left after the operator's response.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Delayed Teleoperation</strong>: @ferrell1965remote found that operators working through a transmission delay fall back on a move-and-wait strategy, so task time grows with the delay. Force reflection through the same delay proved unstable until @anderson1989bilateral used scattering theory to make the communication channel passive, a construction that @niemeyer1991stable recast as wave variables. For a force-reflecting console the delay budget is therefore a stability term as well as a latency term.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Admitting the remote-assistant session</h3>\n<p>Every interval priced so far in this chapter now draws from the same finite stopping reserve when a remote session begins. Admitting the remote assistant requires bounding four timing parameters against the machine's limits: the operator reaction budget (the takeover latency $\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ of @eq-intervention-reaction-floor for the takeover mode in use), the <strong>handshake protocol latency</strong> $\\HandshakeProtocolLatency$, the manual lease $\\ManualLease$, and the minimum blending window $\\BlendingTransitionWindow$. @Tbl-14-authority-timing-contract states them for the machine's two command channels, the base and the arm, which one remote-assistant session commands together.</p>\n<p>↪ How many machines one remote assistant can serve before requests wait minutes for a person is developed in @sec-responsibility-supervisor-utilization.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Machine channel</th><th>Command change and chosen rate ceiling</th><th>Smoothstep window $\\BlendingTransitionWindow\\ge1.875\\lvert\\FiniteChange \\ChannelCommand\\rvert/\\CommandRateCeiling$, whole ticks</th><th>Human / protocol time</th><th>Fallback contract</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Base acceleration command</td><td>Zero to <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.delta_a_str]</code>; $\\CommandRateCeiling=$ <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.j_allowable_str]</code>, the resident stop's jerk bound</td><td><code>[InterventionBlendBudget.T_budget_str]</code></td><td><code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_in_str]</code> / <code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_handshake_str]</code></td><td><code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.manual_lease_str]</code> manual lease; <code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.config_str]</code> pending ceiling shortened by clearance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arm joint torque command</td><td><code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_delta_str]</code>; torque rate $\\CommandRateCeiling=$ <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_rate_str]</code></td><td><code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_budget_str]</code></td><td><code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_in_str]</code> / <code>[InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_handshake_str]</code></td><td>The same manual lease; its loss invokes the arm's validated stop</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Blending windows on the mobile manipulator</strong>: One remote-assistant session commands both channels, and each window is rounded up to whole <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.tick_str]</code> permission ticks. The rate ceiling $\\CommandRateCeiling$ bounds the derivative of each <em>command</em>. The base window is the same at every speed, because the jerk bound belongs to the loaded base. At the in-loop request speed a braking blend does not fit the clearance that remains, so the base receives the resident stop and its window applies only to non-braking changes. Every lease and pending deadline must fit the state-dependent stopping reserve. {#tbl-14-authority-timing-contract tbl-colwidths=\"[18,26,15,18,23]\"}</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ REMOTE ASSISTANT SESSION TIMING ON THE MOBILE MANIPULATOR\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-intervention-authority-log, the four timing parameters that\n# │          follow @tbl-14-authority-timing-contract, and the A3 fault case.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Bound an in-loop takeover and its manual lease at the slowed speed.\n# │ Show:    Spare margin at the in-loop request speed, travel during the\n# │          sourced in-loop takeover, the remainder in mm and ms, and the\n# │          travel the manual lease adds once the assistant holds MAN; the\n# │          remainder after an out-of-loop takeover at the crawl; T_latest at\n# │          the slowed speed and at the aisle speed for the A3 rule.\n# │          Ties to InterventionBlendBudget: the reserve a base blend uses\n# │          exceeds the in-loop remainder.\n# │ How:     Finished stopping budget from the WarehouseAisle scenario\n# │          (jerk-limited resident stop, all overheads). In MAN the manual\n# │          lease takes the chunk lease&#x27;s place in tau_delay, so it adds\n# │          v * (lease - chunk lease). The waterfall is the session budget of\n# │          Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRequestSpeed (TakeoverBudgetModel).\n# │          Handshake, manual lease, and T_latest come from InterventionTimeoutBudget.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionAssistantSession:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;In-loop remote-assistant takeover on the machine after it slows down.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    at_request = Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtRequestSpeed  # in-loop session after the slow-down\n    at_crawl = Scenarios.WarehouseOutOfLoopTakeoverAtCrawl  # out-of-loop takeover at the crawl\n    v_in = at_request.speed  # category: derived (Delta_da-charged ceiling, rounded down)\n    v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen\n    t_react = at_request.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport)\n    delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission)\n    v_crawl = at_crawl.speed  # category: chosen\n    t_ool = at_crawl.takeover  # category: derived (sourced human + transport)\n    chunk_lease = AISLE.t_lease  # category: chosen\n    # Chapter values set once in InterventionTimeoutBudget\n    t_handshake = InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_handshake  # category: derived\n    manual_lease = InterventionTimeoutBudget.manual_lease  # category: chosen\n    t_latest = InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest  # category: derived\n    t_latest_in = InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest_in  # category: derived\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    s = at_request.result\n    spare_in = s.machine.spare.to(millimeter)\n    travel_react = s.takeover_travel.to(millimeter)\n    remainder = s.spare_after_takeover.to(millimeter)\n    remainder_time = s.time_left.to(millisecond)\n    remainder_crawl = at_crawl.result.spare_after_takeover.to(millimeter)\n    lease_excess = s.lease_extension.to(millisecond)\n    lease_travel = s.lease_extension_travel.to(millimeter)\n    travel_da = s.admission_travel.to(millimeter)\n    left_da = s.spare_after_admission.to(millimeter)\n    left_da_lease = s.spare_after_lease.to(millimeter)\n    t_latest_check = (Scenarios.WarehouseInLoopTakeoverAtAisleSpeed.result.machine.spare / v_aisle).to(millisecond)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(spare_in.magnitude - 563.706) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;spare margin at the in-loop request speed: {spare_in}&quot;)\n    check(abs(travel_react.magnitude - 453.75) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;in-loop takeover travel: {travel_react}&quot;)\n    check(abs(remainder.magnitude - 109.956) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;remainder after takeover: {remainder}&quot;)\n    check(abs(remainder_time.magnitude - 146.608) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;remainder as time: {remainder_time}&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((remainder - v_in * remainder_time).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The remainder in mm is the remainder in time at the slowed speed.&quot;\n    )\n    check(abs((manual_lease - at_request.manual_lease).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;One manual lease for the chapter.&quot;)\n    check(abs(lease_travel.magnitude - 30.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;travel the manual lease adds in MAN: {lease_travel}&quot;)\n    check(lease_travel &lt; remainder, &quot;In MAN the manual lease must fit the remainder at the in-loop request speed.&quot;)\n    check(abs(travel_da.magnitude - 47.483) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;travel during the allocated Delta_da: {travel_da}&quot;)\n    check(left_da_lease.magnitude &gt; 0, &quot;The remainder pays Delta_da and the manual lease together at the derived slow-down.&quot;)\n    check(t_handshake &lt; t_react, &quot;The handshake is a share of the in-loop takeover delay.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_latest_check.magnitude - t_latest.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;T_latest agrees with the timeout cell.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_latest.magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;T_latest at the aisle speed: {t_latest}&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((t_latest_in - t_react - remainder_time).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9,\n        &quot;The remainder is T_latest at the slowed speed less the takeover.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(remainder_crawl.magnitude &gt; 0, f&quot;remainder after an out-of-loop takeover at the crawl: {remainder_crawl}&quot;)\n    # No base braking blend fits the in-loop remainder.\n    check(InterventionBlendBudget.v0 == v_in, &quot;Both cells describe the same takeover speed.&quot;)\n    check(InterventionBlendBudget.excess_in &gt; remainder, &quot;A base blend at the in-loop request speed must overrun the remainder.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_in_str = fmt_velocity(v_in, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    v_crawl_str = fmt_velocity(v_crawl, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    left_da_str = fmt_length(left_da, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    left_da_lease_str = fmt_length(left_da_lease, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    t_react_str = fmt_latency(t_react, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_ool_str = fmt_latency(t_ool, unit=second, precision=1)\n    t_handshake_str = fmt_latency(t_handshake, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    manual_lease_str = fmt_latency(manual_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    spare_in_str = fmt_length(spare_in, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    travel_react_str = fmt_length(travel_react, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    remainder_str = fmt_length(remainder, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    remainder_time_str = fmt_latency(remainder_time, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    remainder_crawl_str = fmt_length(remainder_crawl, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    chunk_lease_str = fmt_latency(chunk_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    lease_travel_str = fmt_length(lease_travel, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_latest_in_str = fmt_latency(t_latest_in, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n]```\n\nConsider an illustrative remote-assistant in-loop takeover, requested after the mobile manipulator has slowed to `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_in_str]` with the assistant&#x27;s `manual_drive` entry. The four parameters take the values already derived. The reaction budget $\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$ is the `[InterventionAssistantSession.t_react_str]` in-loop takeover of @sec-intervention-reaction-drift, which already includes transport and the `[InterventionAssistantSession.t_handshake_str]` four-phase exchange of @tbl-14-handshake-protocol, so the handshake is part of $\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$, not an addition to it. The manual lease, the longest silence tolerated from the assistant before the permission path falls back, is `[InterventionAssistantSession.manual_lease_str]`, and @tbl-14-authority-timing-contract gives the blending window for each channel.\n\nAn in-loop session at `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_in_str]` closes its complete budget by only a few centimeters, and only because the base takes its resident stop rather than a braking blend. A session that begins at the `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_crawl_str]` crawl or from standstill has far more room, so those are the configurations worth qualifying. @Tbl-14-session-budget charges the in-loop session&#x27;s intervals one at a time against the clearance the finished budget leaves at that speed.\n\n| Interval charged at the in-loop request speed                      |                                                      Travel |                                            Clearance left |\n|:-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------:|----------------------------------------------------------:|\n| None (the finished budget&#x27;s spare)                                 |                                                             |      `[InterventionAssistantSession.spare_in_str]` |\n| In-loop takeover $\\SupervisoryTakeoverLatency$, handshake included |    `[InterventionAssistantSession.travel_react_str]` |     `[InterventionAssistantSession.remainder_str]` |\n| Dispatch-to-admission allocation $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$      | `[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.travel_da_in_str]` |       `[InterventionAssistantSession.left_da_str]` |\n| Manual lease in place of the chunk lease in $\\PrebrakeDelay$       |    `[InterventionAssistantSession.lease_travel_str]` | `[InterventionAssistantSession.left_da_lease_str]` |\n| Base braking blend (reserve it would use)                          |      up to `[InterventionBlendBudget.excess_in_str]` |            Does not fit; the base takes its resident stop |\n\n: **Clearance budget for an in-loop remote-assistant session**: Each row charges one interval at the `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_in_str]` request speed, in the capture-origin design budget, and the last column carries what remains. The first four rows close the complete budget by a few centimeters; the blend row would overrun it. These are screening values for an allocated, unmeasured $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, not a qualified admission result. {#tbl-14-session-budget tbl-colwidths=&quot;[46,27,27]&quot;}\n\nThree rows of the table decide that verdict. The remainder after the takeover, the `[InterventionAssistantSession.remainder_time_str]` by which the takeover beats $\\FallbackDeadline$ (@sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines), bounds the transfer itself. Any further delay before a stop could begin while authority is changing hands must fit inside it, and the enforcer checks the resident stop against it at every tick of the transfer. The lease row exists because, once the assistant holds `MAN`, the manual lease replaces the `[InterventionAssistantSession.chunk_lease_str]` chunk lease inside $\\PrebrakeDelay$, so its extra length becomes travel. The row is the upper bound on the lease set out in @sec-intervention-arbiter-heartbeats, and the chosen value passes it. The blend row fails. Blending the step to braking from `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_in_str]` grows the travel plus the resident stop from the blended state by up to `[InterventionBlendBudget.excess_in_str]`, well past the remainder, so in this session the base receives the resident stop and only the arm blends. This conservative per-tick check charges a fresh resident stop starting from zero deceleration at each blended speed rather than the partial model&#x27;s `[InterventionBlendBudget.d_over_res_str]` constant-deceleration tail, because any fallback triggered during the blend begins from the standard resident profile. The slow-down was derived with $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ charged, so the complete budget closes, but by centimeters, another reason the base should complete its stop before accepting continued manual motion.\n\nAt the crawl, after a `[InterventionAssistantSession.t_ool_str]` out-of-loop takeover, `[InterventionAssistantSession.remainder_crawl_str]` remains before the same charges, and the base&#x27;s stop from the crawl is its resident stop, with no braking blend to reserve. The admission allocation and the lease extension cost travel in proportion to speed, so at the crawl they take only a small part of that remainder. Once the assistant holds `MAN`, the live check of @tbl-14-clock-origins uses the current remaining clearance, the measured speed, and only the unelapsed portions of those intervals; commit does not restore the distance spent waiting or blending. These figures screen the candidates. Qualification must still check the rounded executable profile and the state-matched fallback, including delay travel, over the full transition, against a measured $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$.\n\nEvery command in that session, whether a streamed pose, a force-guided target, or a heartbeat that renews the manual lease, must also come from the authenticated operator that the `manual_drive` entry names. A budget that closes by a few centimeters leaves no room for a forged or replayed packet, which makes identity and packet admission the next constraint.\n\n## Authenticating an Intervention {#sec-intervention-authenticating-intervention}\n\nOn a shared wireless link, a legitimate `veto_path` intervention packet and an unauthorized drive-torque packet can arrive within the very same control cycle. The permission architecture must prevent an adversary or malformed network frame from seizing kinetic authority, yet it must never obstruct an authorized operator from arresting the machine before a collision. Systems engineers reconcile the two demands by classifying intervention operations according to the mechanical work they produce. Stopping the machine by removing power or engaging a mechanical holding brake is not symmetrical with injecting active motion commands, so the two actions take different verification paths. A protective stop brings the machine to a controlled halt along a braking or holding rung of the fallback ladder rather than abruptly cutting actuator torque. Commanding base velocities, wheel torques, or joint trajectories, by contrast, injects fresh mechanical energy into the plant: a forged or corrupted trajectory command can drive a moving mass into an obstacle, whereas an authenticated out-of-band protective input only requests a plant-specific minimum-risk stop.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ AUTHENTICATION DEADLINE ON THE MOBILE MANIPULATOR</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-authenticating-intervention, the verification</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          deadline, the two primitives, and the freshness window.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Price message authentication and signature verification against</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the finished budget's spare.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    T_auth,max = T_latest at the aisle speed; the time and travel of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          an AES-128 CMAC over one frame and of one Ed25519 verification on</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the safety microcontroller; the displacement a replay at the edge</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          of the freshness window carries.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseRemoteCommandAuthentication</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (CommandAuthenticationModel): published Cortex-M4 cycle counts</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (cited literature) divided by the permission MCU's clock</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (RM.permission_mcu); verification adds to tau_delay, so each</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          millisecond is travel at the aisle speed against the spare of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          InterventionTimeoutBudget.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond, microsecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_frequency, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>class InterventionAuthBudget: \"\"\"Takeover authentication as one more pre-brake delay on the machine.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>auth = Scenarios.WarehouseRemoteCommandAuthentication  # Ed25519 and AES-128 cycle counts at the MCU clock v = auth.budget.speed  # category: chosen spare = InterventionTimeoutBudget.spare  # category: derived t_auth_max = InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest  # category: derived mcu_clock = auth.clock  # category: illustrative (reference class, RM.permission_mcu)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">Published cycle counts on an ARM Cortex-M4 (cited literature); the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">reference core is assumed to retire them in the same number of cycles.</h1>\n<p>ed25519_verify_cycles = auth.verify_cycles  # category: sourced (Owens et al. 2024, optimized assembly) aes_block_cycles = auth.mac_block_cycles  # category: sourced (Schwabe and Stoffelen, AES-128 single block) mac_blocks = auth.mac_blocks  # category: illustrative (48-byte frame: counter, timestamp, role, channel mask, command) tau_fresh = auth.freshness_window  # category: illustrative (freshness window)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>r = auth.result t_sig = r.verify_time.to(millisecond) t_mac = r.mac_time.to(microsecond) travel_sig = r.verify_travel.to(millimeter) left_sig = r.spare_after_verify.to(millimeter) travel_mac = r.mac_travel.to(millimeter) dx_fresh = r.freshness_travel.to(millimeter) left_fresh = r.spare_after_freshness.to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs((r.deadline - t_auth_max).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The verification deadline is T_latest of the timeout cell.\") check(abs((spare - auth.budget.result.machine.spare).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"One spare for the chapter.\") check(abs(t_auth_max.magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, f\"verification deadline: {t_auth_max}\") check(abs(t_sig.magnitude - 1.8) &lt; 1e-9, f\"one Ed25519 verification: {t_sig}\") check(abs(t_mac.magnitude - 4.96275) &lt; 1e-6, f\"one AES-128 CMAC frame: {t_mac}\") check(t_sig &lt; t_auth_max, \"One signature verification must fit the deadline.\") check(t_sig &gt; 300 * t_mac, \"A signature must cost hundreds of MACs.\") check(abs(left_sig.magnitude - 128.67) &lt; 5e-3, f\"spare left after one signature: {left_sig}\") check(dx_fresh &lt; spare and left_fresh.magnitude &lt; 2.0, \"A replay at the freshness edge must consume nearly the whole spare.\") check(tau_fresh &gt; InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle, \"Charged with Delta_da, a replay at the freshness edge must overrun the spare.\") check(t_sig &lt; InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle, \"One signature must still fit once Delta_da is charged.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1) t_auth_max_str = fmt_latency(t_auth_max, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_sig_str = fmt_latency(t_sig, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_mac_str = fmt_latency(t_mac, unit=microsecond, precision=1) travel_sig_str = fmt_length(travel_sig, unit=millimeter, precision=2) left_sig_str = fmt_length(left_sig, unit=millimeter, precision=1) tau_fresh_str = fmt_latency(tau_fresh, unit=millisecond, precision=0) dx_fresh_str = fmt_length(dx_fresh, unit=millimeter, precision=0) left_fresh_str = fmt_length(left_fresh, unit=millimeter, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Authentication adds delay to the remote intervention path, so it is priced against the same deadline as every other latency source, the latest fallback deadline $\\FallbackDeadline$ of @eq-intervention-latest-fallback. At the warehouse aisle velocity of <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.v_str]</code>, the partial spare permits at most <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.t_auth_max_str]</code> of added delay before charging the admission latency $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, which drops to <code>[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle_str]</code> once the placement allocation is charged. Transferring published Cortex-M4 cycle counts to the reference microcontroller clock gives an illustrative estimate of <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.t_sig_str]</code> for one Ed25519 signature verification [@owens2024ed25519], which translates into <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.travel_sig_str]</code> of physical travel and leaves <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.left_sig_str]</code> of spare displacement margin. The same cycle-count and clock-rate estimate for an AES-128 CMAC over one command frame is <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.t_mac_str]</code> [@dworkin2005cmac; @schwabe2016aes]. Per packet, both estimates fit within the deadline; loaded measurements on the target must qualify them. The design problem is therefore not choosing an algorithm but meeting five requirements on the admission path that carries the packets:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Protective stops never wait for verification.</strong> The local hardware fallback timer and independently wired emergency inputs act unconditionally, regardless of whether cryptographic routines have completed or stalled. If a verification routine overruns its deadline, the permission subsystem marks remote assistance as unavailable and cedes control to the autonomous protection layer; it never postpones an emergency deceleration. As analyzed in the verifier-flooding pitfall of @sec-intervention-fallacies-pitfalls, what causes a cryptographic verifier to miss real-time deadlines is rarely the algorithmic cost of an individual packet, but rather the ingress volume of hostile or unvetted network traffic saturating the execution queue.</li>\n<li><strong>Takeover requests must be attributable to an individual.</strong> Because a symmetric Message Authentication Code (MAC) relies on a shared key known to both endpoints, a valid tag proves frame integrity but cannot prove which endpoint originated the transmission. An asymmetric digital signature, however, cryptographically binds the takeover request to the teleoperator's private key, supporting attribution when credential enrollment, key custody, and compromise evidence also identify the operator. The architecture therefore mandates an asymmetric digital signature on the session-initiating <code>manual_drive</code> request, while subsequent streaming commands and periodic heartbeats rely on the lower-overhead symmetric MAC.</li>\n<li><strong>Freshness represents a spatial displacement budget, not merely a clock tolerance.</strong> In embodied systems, packet latency translates directly into unmodeled vehicle travel. A replayed or delayed motion packet arriving at the boundary of a <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.tau_fresh_str]</code> freshness acceptance window commands an action based on an obsolete vehicle pose. Over that interval the mobile base travels <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.dx_fresh_str]</code>, consuming nearly the entire safety margin and leaving only <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.left_fresh_str]</code> of the <code>[InterventionAuthBudget.spare_str]</code> partial spare. Once the admission latency $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ is factored in, such a replay completely overruns the available spare, so the allowable freshness window must shrink below <code>[InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle_str]</code>. The receiver drops counters no newer than the last authenticated, accepted counter before spending processor cycles on cryptography. It advances that stored counter only after authentication and freshness checks pass, so an unauthenticated high counter cannot invalidate later legitimate traffic. If heartbeat packets cease for longer than the lease duration $\\ManualLease$, the controller immediately revokes manual authority and engages the certified local fallback ladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Malformed traffic never alters control authority.</strong> Any packet with a corrupted checksum, an out-of-order sequence counter, or an invalid authentication tag is immediately rejected and recorded according to Rule A4 of @sec-intervention-authority-log. A corrupted packet neither modifies existing actuator channel assignments nor triggers a spurious emergency stop. Rejecting an isolated invalid packet preserves still-valid authority; interference or dropouts that prevent timely renewal still expire the lease and invoke the qualified fallback.</li>\n<li><strong>Every actuator-write path must be accounted for.</strong> All side channels capable of driving physical hardware, including diagnostic ports, hardware bootloaders, debug interfaces, and direct fieldbus wiring, must be gated by the hardware permission authority or guarded by dedicated interlocks. The intervention threat model explicitly accounts for unauthorized remote packets injected across wireless links, replay attacks traversing internal communication buses, and rogue commands originating from a compromised high-level application processor. Physical tampering, by contrast, enters the safety assurance case only when explicitly mandated by the operational threat model.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>: <strong>Ed25519</strong>: The Edwards-curve signature scheme of @bernstein2012highspeed provides rapid verification alongside compact 32-byte public keys and 64-byte signatures. Constant-time implementations eliminate secret-dependent branches and memory lookups to protect against side-channel timing attacks. While that defense primarily shields the signing teleoperation console, the receiving safety microcontroller only verifies incoming frames. Well-formed invalid signatures can still require the expensive verification path, even when malformed inputs admit early rejection. The verifier's processing load therefore depends on admitted traffic and its verification paths, the vulnerability that the verifier-flooding analysis of @sec-intervention-fallacies-pitfalls prices.</p>\n<p>These authentication and transport latencies directly impact policy learning and sim-to-real transfer. An embodied simulator that provides instantaneous human takeover underprices the danger of visiting marginal states. If an autonomous policy learns that intervention occurs instantaneously, it will tolerate trajectories that are physically unrecoverable once realistic communication, cryptographic verification, and actuator fallback latencies are introduced. Training and hardware-in-the-loop evaluation pipelines must therefore inject these bounded authentication, transport, and mechanical reaction delays.</p>\n<p>Verifying an incoming packet authenticates its credential; the capability manifest separately determines its privileges. Cryptographic validity alone cannot guarantee dynamic stability during control transfer. When authority shifts while the chassis carries linear momentum and the manipulator actuators exert active joint torques, the permission path must commit one selected source per channel and announce that ownership to the operator. Local mutual exclusion and human mode awareness are separate requirements; a valid packet establishes neither by itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h2\">Authority Handover Failure Modes</h2>\n<p>When the warehouse mobile manipulator's perception policy meets an unmodeled specular reflection from wet shrink-wrap on a pallet, depth artifacts drop its confidence and the runtime asks a remote assistant to reposition the gripper. If the console grants manual joystick authority over the arm without locking the base channel or announcing that the base is still moving, an assistant focused on aligning the fingers assumes the chassis is stationary. Meanwhile the autonomous navigation thread keeps driving the wheels into a rack upright. Every individual hardware and software component in that handover operates within its specification. The collision comes from mode confusion, the failure class that Sarter and Woods identified in automated aircraft cockpits [@sarter1995mode]. Every operating mode added to a control loop multiplies the state a human supervisor must track. When an interface changes modes without unambiguous telemetry of which agent holds each channel, operators take actions that are right for their assumed mode and hazardous in the actual one [@sarter1995mode]. In embodied physical systems, the most dangerous form of this failure is <strong>authority ambiguity</strong>: a state in which the autonomous runtime assumes the human has taken over a physical degree of freedom, while the human assumes the automated controller still guides it.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Mode confusion</strong></em> is a mismatch between the operational state a human supervisor believes an automated system occupies and its actual execution mode, leading the supervisor to take actions that would be correct for the presumed state but are hazardous in the real one.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: When control channels transfer authority independently, an operator's mental model of one channel can easily go stale while their attention is consumed by another. For example, an operator dedicates the <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]</code> in-loop response window entirely to an arm alignment, under the false belief that the base is parked. The base, running under the autonomous policy at <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.v_str]</code> aisle speed, then travels <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_in_str]</code>, which is <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.travel_over_spare_mult_str]</code> the <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.spare_da_str]</code> margin the complete budget leaves for $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Conflicting command streams between concurrent software modules represent an arbitration fault, which single-owner bus gating resolves by construction. Mode confusion, by contrast, resides within the supervisor's mental model of the machine. Resolving it requires committed channel masks and explicit per-channel status annunciation at the console, because deterministic ownership on the CAN bus or Ethernet backplane cannot correct a human's false assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Telemetry confirming that an operator's gaze is directed at a primary task monitor does not guarantee the absence of mode confusion. Tracking an operator's visual focus onto an end-effector camera stream reveals nothing about whether that operator realizes the autonomous navigation stack still holds kinetic authority over the drive chassis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On a machine with several axes, control authority splits naturally across distinct groups of joints, such as the mobile base's planar velocity ($\\BaseLongitudinalVelocity, \\BaseLateralVelocity, \\BaseHeadingRate$) and the manipulator arm's joint positions. In the wet-shrink-wrap collision, those channels transferred independently. Telemetry logs reveal the fault as disjointed control flags toggling asynchronously across execution threads. At the human-machine interface, however, the split state stays invisible until physical clearance is exhausted, because neither the policy nor the human receives an immediate, unambiguous indicator of the other's partial withdrawal.</p>\n<p>To prevent this desynchronization, handover protocols must enforce explicit, per-channel bitmasks across every phase of negotiation. Every <code>Request</code>, <code>Acknowledge</code>, and <code>Commit</code> message must declare the exact subset of degrees of freedom being transferred. The formal transition state machine of @sec-intervention-authority-transitions moves proposal tokens exclusively for those designated channels, and the subsequent <code>Confirm</code> phase explicitly annunciates active ownership on each channel back to the operator console. A channel left out of the bitmask may keep its autonomous owner, so the base can keep navigating while the operator takes the arm. This holds only while the console annunciates that the base is still moving under the policy and the operator's acknowledgment covered that state. Without that annunciation, the commit places the untransferred channel in a certified holding state, such as an active brake hold, rather than letting autonomous execution continue silently. The wet-shrink-wrap collision is the second rule's case, an untransferred base left running without annunciation.</p>\n<p>Explicit channel ownership protocols solve arbitration on the digital bus, but they cannot restore the situational awareness of an out-of-loop human. For this reason, the in-loop and out-of-loop takeovers of @sec-intervention-reaction-drift must be treated as distinct operational states with different timing budgets, the <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_in_str]</code> and <code>[InterventionTakeoverBudget.t_ool_str]</code> that set different request speeds. While gaze tracking can verify where an operator directs their eyes, it cannot verify cognitive comprehension or justify truncating the required takeover latency budget. Local stopping envelopes must remain continuously armed and active, even when the console reports an attentive supervisor. Two field incidents show what happens when either half of this two-layer protection is missing.</p>\n<p>In the Tempe, Arizona collision, the National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) determined that the safety driver was visually distracted by a personal smartphone, identifying operator complacency and inadequate monitoring policies as primary contributing factors [@ntsb2019uber]. The vehicle architecture had disabled automatic emergency braking during testing and relied entirely on the human supervisor to perceive hazards and intervene. In the San Francisco Cruise incident, an autonomous vehicle executed an emergency stop after striking a pedestrian who had been thrown into its path by an adjacent human-driven car. The onboard planner, however, subsequently initiated an automated pull-over maneuver, dragging the pedestrian beneath the chassis [@quinnemanuel2024cruise]. The Tempe disaster demonstrates why an operator-monitoring contract must always be backed by an autonomous, local stopping envelope capable of bringing the machine to a halt within the remaining sensor clearance, regardless of operator attentiveness. The Cruise incident demonstrates that unexpected physical contact, tactile sensor anomalies, or sudden external force spikes must immediately lock out all motion authority into a verified physical inspection hold, rather than allowing automated post-collision clearing routines to execute.</p>\n<p>↩ The Tempe timeline, including the action suppression that withheld emergency braking, is established in @sec-boundary-when-fails.</p>\n<p>Even when channel ownership and local stops are properly configured, a human operator's manual command can oppose an active safety barrier without creating a multi-writer conflict on the actuator bus. When an operator commands a manipulator toward a prohibited obstacle, the downstream safety enforcer either projects the trajectory onto the safe half-space or rejects the command entirely, dispatching only the admitted control action to the motor drives. However, the supervisory console must make this active constraint immediately visible to the human. If the interface conceals why the robot has stopped responding, the operator will instinctively command higher control effort or larger joystick displacements, leading to severe command saturation and instability once the constraint clears. In addition, when the console incorporates bilateral teleoperation or force-feedback interfaces, system stability depends directly on the passivity-based energy bounds established in @sec-intervention-haptic-passivity; ownership tokens alone cannot prevent feedback oscillations across a physical-virtual coupled loop.</p>\n<p>Authority ambiguity also corrupts downstream imitation learning. If the record cannot say who held each channel, interventions are mislabeled as autonomous behavior or the reverse, and a behavioral-cloning policy learns to reproduce the very errors that triggered the takeover. A low offline validation loss cannot catch this, because it only shows that the policy memorized the wrong labels. Causal intervention masking, developed in @sec-intervention-recovery-eligibility, depends on ownership bits that the authority record stamps at commit time.</p>\n<p>\\clearpage</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ FLIGHT 2904 GROUND-CONTACT TIMING STRIP (FIGURE GUARD)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: ws-intervention-when-automation-holds-authority-crew-cannot\n# │          (ws_flight2904_authority_lockout.svg).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Keep the hand-drawn, to-scale strip in step with its source.\n# │ Show:    Left-gear contact and the start of wheel braking after right-gear\n# │          contact; the strip&#x27;s time scale.\n# │ How:     BFU 1994 report: the left gear touched the runway 9 s after the\n# │          right; the wheel brakes began to act about 4 s after the left gear\n# │          touched. The SVG places t at x = 190 + 30 px/s; this cell reads the\n# │          drawn guide lines back from the file.\n# │\n# │ Imports: (none)\n# │ Exports: (none; guard only)\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport os as _os\nimport re as _re\nfrom mlsysim import Literature\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\n\nclass InterventionFlight2904Strip:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Timing values drawn in ws_flight2904_authority_lockout.svg.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants) ──────────────────────\n    t_left_gear_s = 9  # category: sourced (bfu1994warsaw)\n    t_brake_after_left_s = 4  # category: sourced (bfu1994warsaw, &quot;about 4 seconds&quot;)\n    x0_px, px_per_s = 190, 30  # category: chosen (drawing scale)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (The Compute) ─────────────────\n    t_brake_s = t_left_gear_s + t_brake_after_left_s\n    _svg_rel = &quot;images/svg/ws_flight2904_authority_lockout.svg&quot;\n    _svg_path = next(_p for _p in (_svg_rel, &quot;books/vol4/14_intervention/&quot; + _svg_rel, &quot;vol4/14_intervention/&quot; + _svg_rel) if _os.path.exists(_p))\n    with open(_svg_path) as _f:\n        _svg = _f.read()\n    _guides = {int(m) for m in _re.findall(r&#x27;&lt;line x1=&quot;(\\d+)&quot; y1=&quot;30&quot;&#x27;, _svg)}\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────\n    check(_guides == {x0_px + px_per_s * t_left_gear_s, x0_px + px_per_s * t_brake_s}, &quot;flight 2904 strip guides are stale&quot;)\n    check(f&quot;left gear contact, {t_left_gear_s} s&quot; in _svg, &quot;flight 2904 left-gear label is stale&quot;)\n    check(f&quot;brakes act, {t_brake_s} s&quot; in _svg, &quot;flight 2904 brake label is stale&quot;)\n]```\n\n::: {#ws-intervention-when-automation-holds-authority-crew-cannot .callout-war-story title=&quot;Ground-contact interlocks on flight 2904&quot;}\n\n\n\n**Context**: On Lufthansa Flight 2904 at Warsaw, the flight crew executed an approach based on outdated wind reports, encountering an unreported tailwind that caused the aircraft to touch down at high speed far down a waterlogged runway. Upon touchdown, the crew immediately commanded maximum manual braking and deployed thrust reversers, but the aircraft&#x27;s automated flight-control logic refused to grant deceleration authority [@bfu1994warsaw].\n\n**Mechanism**: Crosswinds and runway hydroplaning delayed the left gear compression: the right main gear made contact 9 s before the left. The aircraft&#x27;s deceleration interlocks were rigid. Ground spoiler deployment required weight-on-wheels shock-strut compression on both main gears **or** wheel spin-up above 72 knots. Thrust reversers required weight compression on both main struts. Mechanical wheel braking was locked out until the main wheels achieved synchronized spin-up. Because hydroplaning prevented wheel spin-up and an asymmetric roll angle kept the left strut uncompressed, the control logic determined the aircraft was still airborne. As a result, the flight computer locked out spoilers and reversers for 9 s and inhibited wheel brakes for even longer.\n\n**Impact**: Wheel braking was inhibited until 13 s after initial touchdown. With braking authority locked out across the primary deceleration zone, the aircraft could not dissipate its kinetic energy along the remaining wet runway. It overran the runway threshold at 72 knots, struck an airport embankment, and caught fire, resulting in two fatalities and total hull loss.\n\n**Response**: Official accident investigators cited the ground-contact interlock logic as a critical contributing cause alongside crew decision-making and delayed meteorological updates. The crew could not override the spoiler and reverser lockouts. The report identifies those design constraints alongside crew actions and incomplete landing-distance guidance; it does not establish that a manual override alone would have prevented the overrun.\n\n**Systems lesson**: Embodied robots share this exact failure topology whenever automated sensor preconditions, such as docking latch checks, wheel-slip traction observers, or ground-contact switches, gate emergency braking or reverse-torque commands issued by human supervisors. Unmodeled wheel slippage or uneven terrain can violate a sensor predicate precisely when deceleration is urgently required. Any control architecture that gates physical actuation behind automated preconditions must satisfy three rules. First, it must immediately annunciate unsatisfied interlocks across the operator console. Second, it must explicitly identify the unfulfilled sensor predicate. Third, it must provide a deterministic fallback mode for sensor disagreement. Rather than bypassing safety interlocks blindly, the supervisory architecture must define a certified fallback deceleration path that executes based on conservative, physically verifiable stops.\n:::\n\nThe formal authority arbiter detailed in @sec-intervention-authority-transitions exists to prevent these failures. Safe embodied control architectures cannot rely on implicit authority handoffs, assumed human vigilance, or ad hoc torque summing on the actuator bus. Evaluating post-incident behavior requires a tamper-evident, channel-by-channel telemetry record captured at every control cycle, with explicit gaps and bounded event timing. The surviving record can help distinguish a safety boundary breach, an invalid token exchange, and electrical noise on a safety bus only to the extent that its independent measurements, capture coverage, and timing bounds separate those events. Its storage path must therefore survive unexpected power interruptions, kernel panics, and real-time scheduling stalls.\n\n## Trustworthy Operational Logs {#sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs}\n\nConsider a robotic arm striking a warehouse racking upright during a supervisory takeover. If the operational log contains an unrecorded gap, investigators cannot determine whether the policy proposed the damaging trajectory, the enforcer admitted it, or the operator received authority too late to intervene. Rigorous incident reconstruction requires two foundational properties: declared loss handling and an operational record engineered to survive physical failure.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ VEHICLE EVENT DATA RECORDER BASELINE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs, footnote fn-std-edr-baseline.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Contrast the regulated vehicle recorder's pre-crash sampling with</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the machine's permission tick.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Part 563 pre-crash window and rate before and after the 2024 rule;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the new sample period in permission ticks.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Window and rate from 49 CFR Part 563 (cited literature);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          period = 1 / rate; ticks = period / AISLE.t_tick.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import second, millisecond, hertz from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, fmt_count, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionRecordBaselines: \"\"\"Regulated vehicle pre-crash recording against the permission tick.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">NHTSA, 49 CFR Part 563 (Sec. 563.7 Table I and footnote 4), via cited literature.</h1>\n<p>edr_old_window = 5 <em> second  # category: sourced edr_old_rate = 2 </em> hertz  # category: sourced edr_new_window = 20 <em> second  # category: sourced edr_new_rate = 10 </em> hertz  # category: sourced</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">UN Regulation No. 157 (ALKS), para. 8.3.1(d)(ii): DSSAD timestamp accuracy +/- 1.0 s.</h1>\n<p>dssad_accuracy = 1 * second  # category: sourced tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>edr_new_period = (1 / edr_new_rate).to(millisecond) edr_ticks = (edr_new_period / tick).to(\"\").magnitude dssad_ticks = (dssad_accuracy / tick).to(\"\").magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(edr_new_period.magnitude - 100.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"new recorder sample period: {edr_new_period}\") check(abs(edr_ticks - 100.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"recorder period in permission ticks: {edr_ticks}\") check(abs(dssad_ticks - 1000.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"DSSAD accuracy in permission ticks: {dssad_ticks}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>edr_old_window_str = fmt_latency(edr_old_window, unit=second, precision=0) edr_old_rate_str = fmt_frequency(edr_old_rate, unit=hertz, precision=0) edr_new_window_str = fmt_latency(edr_new_window, unit=second, precision=0) edr_new_rate_str = fmt_frequency(edr_new_rate, unit=hertz, precision=0) edr_new_period_str = fmt_latency(edr_new_period, unit=millisecond, precision=0) edr_ticks_str = fmt_count(edr_ticks, precision=0) dssad_accuracy_str = fmt_latency(dssad_accuracy, unit=second, precision=0) dssad_ticks_str = fmt_count(dssad_ticks, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>A trustworthy record must capture state on both sides of the causal boundary, paired with independent plant observations. Motor phase currents, optical encoder positions, and wrist force-torque measurements provide physical observations distinct from software setpoints, subject to their calibration, bandwidth, and uncertainty bounds. Dispatched commands alone cannot prove delivered actuator torque or establish mechanical collision causality.</p>\n<p>↩ A record of what was proposed, evaluated, dispatched, and measured is the third demand on the permission path established in @sec-boundary-safety-convergence.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Event Data Recorder Baseline</strong>: A passenger-vehicle event data recorder captures <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_old_window_str]</code> of pre-crash data at <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_old_rate_str]</code> (phasing in from September 2028 to <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_new_window_str]</code> at <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_new_rate_str]</code>), which even under the modernized rule yields only one sample per <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_new_period_str]</code> across <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.edr_ticks_str]</code> permission ticks [@nhtsa2024edr]. Similarly, the data storage system mandated for automated lane-keeping systems under UN Regulation No. 157 logs system activations, overrides, and transition demands to establish driver attribution, but its timestamps require accuracy only within <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.dssad_accuracy_str]</code>, or <code>[InterventionRecordBaselines.dssad_ticks_str]</code> permission ticks [@unece2021r157]. Because neither regulatory standard provides the time resolution needed to order an intervention against physical contact within a single control cycle, the autonomous machine must maintain its own deterministic, per-tick operational record.</p>\n<p>At every cycle, the <code>[InterventionSharedAuthorityScene.permission_rate_str]</code> deterministic control loop records a structured authority and action tuple under declared loss handling. The minimal <strong>evidence tuple</strong> takes the form: $$\\langle \\MicrocontrollerClockTime, \\RecordedAuthorityState, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\NominalControlCommand, \\CertifiedControlAction, \\JointTorque, \\text{ID}_{\\text{operator}}, \\RemainingExecutionLease\\rangle$$ This tuple records both sides of the proposal-permission boundary. Its command fields are the action taps of @sec-data-four-stream-action-tap taken at intervention time: $\\NominalControlCommand$ is the requested or mapped command ($\\RequestedActionTap$ or $\\MappedCommandTap$, whichever the source emits), $\\CertifiedControlAction$ is the enforced command $\\EnforcedCommandTap$, and the measured response $\\MeasuredResponseTap$ lives in $\\MechanicalPlantState$. The fields are:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>$\\MicrocontrollerClockTime$ is the monotonic event timestamp recorded in nanoseconds by the safety microcontroller hardware clock, mapped to other subsystem clocks through a qualified clock-conversion method with an explicit error bound. Expressing values in nanosecond units does not imply nanosecond-level inter-device synchronization accuracy.</li>\n<li>$\\RecordedAuthorityState$ records the active discrete authority state (<code>AUTO</code>, <code>PEND</code>, <code>BLEND</code>, <code>MAN</code>, <code>FALLBACK</code>, or <code>PROTECTIVE</code>).</li>\n<li>$\\MechanicalPlantState$ captures the instantaneous measured plant state, including joint angles, velocities, motor phase currents, and IMU linear accelerations.</li>\n<li>$\\NominalControlCommand$ is the proposed plant control setpoint requested by the cognitive policy or human operator console.</li>\n<li>$\\CertifiedControlAction$ is the control command admitted by the enforcer, including a feasible CBF-filtered proposal or the selected qualified fallback. Associated event metadata must distinguish admission, modification, and rejection; @sec-enforcement-qp-projection specifies the projection path.</li>\n<li>$\\JointTorque$ is the actuator torque command dispatched across the real-time fieldbus to the motor inverters. It is still a command; delivered motor torque can be estimated from the measured phase currents in $\\MechanicalPlantState$ using a qualified motor model and explicit uncertainty bounds.</li>\n<li>$\\text{ID}_{\\text{operator}}$ identifies the authenticated cryptographic entity holding active proposal rights.</li>\n<li>$\\RemainingExecutionLease$ records the remaining time on the active execution lease ($\\ChunkLease$ or $\\ManualLease$, minus the elapsed duration $\\MicrocontrollerClockTime - \\LeaseRefreshInstant$).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Omitting any of these fields breaks post-incident reconstruction. Without the proposal $\\NominalControlCommand$, a dispatched torque $\\JointTorque$ cannot show whether the policy or operator asked for an erratic action. Without the enforcer output $\\CertifiedControlAction$, the log cannot show whether the safety barrier modified that request. Without $\\RemainingExecutionLease$, it cannot show whether authority had already lapsed into a fallback mode when the command was evaluated. Chained with cryptographic integrity proofs and clock-synchronization metadata, these tuples form the per-tick half of the authority record that @sec-intervention-authority-log specifies.</p>\n<p>Three architectural requirements ensure that logged tuples serve as reliable forensic evidence. First, timestamps must date physical events rather than software handling. A software interrupt handler that samples the clock only after being scheduled records its own execution delay rather than the event time. Under variable scheduling jitter and interrupt latency, this delay can easily invert the apparent chronological order of an intervention command and a mechanical contact. Protective inputs and fieldbus ingress packets must therefore be timestamped directly at the hardware boundary through timer edge capture and network controller hardware counters. When clock-conversion error, sensor exposure windows, and sampling intervals overlap between two events, the log must explicitly record their relative order as indistinguishable rather than asserting an unverified sequence.</p>\n<p>Second, the storage pipeline must be strictly tiered by guarantee level. Minimal evidence tuples stream to a bounded ring buffer in non-volatile memory on the safety microcontroller. The serialized tuple size multiplied by the control tick rate sets a continuous write bandwidth that hardware qualification tests must prove the device sustains under all operating regimes, with buffer overflows explicitly flagged rather than silently dropped. High-bandwidth perception streams, including camera frames, point clouds, and policy internal activations, flow to a best-effort buffer on the primary host. This high-volume traffic is never permitted to contend for the safety microcontroller's memory or bus bandwidth.</p>\n<p>↩ The multi-camera ingestion rate that this best-effort buffer must absorb is established in @sec-data-telemetry-ingestion-storage.</p>\n<p>Third, loss must be declared explicitly. When buffer exhaustion or bus stalls occur, the logger must record a missing entry or overwritten frame as a declared gap in the evidence, never as an implied safe pass. A declared gap alerts both post-incident auditors and downstream training pipelines that the trajectory continuity was broken, whereas a silent drop makes two separated ticks appear contiguous in time.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PRE-TRIGGER LOGGING WINDOW\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs, the pre-trigger paragraph.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Size the frozen pre-trigger window from the sourced takeover time.\n# │ Show:    The design out-of-loop takeover and the window it sets.\n# │ How:     AISLE.pre_trigger_window, the design takeover rounded up to whole\n# │          seconds (the same window @sec-data-intervention-recovery uses).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import second\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionPreTriggerWindow:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Pre-trigger window that reaches back past the design takeover.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    t_ool = AISLE.t_takeover_out_of_loop  # category: derived (sourced p90 take-over time + transport)\n    t_pre = AISLE.pre_trigger_window  # category: derived\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    headroom = (t_pre - t_ool).to(second)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(t_pre &gt;= t_ool, &quot;The window must reach back past the design takeover.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_pre.to(second).magnitude - 5.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;pre-trigger window: {t_pre}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_ool_str = fmt_latency(t_ool, unit=second, precision=1)\n    t_pre_str = fmt_latency(t_pre, unit=second, precision=0)\n]```\n\nUpon receiving an intervention trigger, such as an operator takeover command, an emergency stop assertion, or an enforcer barrier violation, the logger freezes a configured **pre-trigger window** ($\\PreTriggerWindowDuration =$ `[InterventionPreTriggerWindow.t_pre_str]`) and transitions to recording a dedicated post-trigger segment. The pre-trigger duration matches the `[InterventionPreTriggerWindow.t_ool_str]` design out-of-loop takeover latency characterized in @sec-intervention-reaction-drift, rounded up to whole seconds. This window preserves the design takeover interval, but policy divergence may begin before the operator notices it, so its duration alone does not establish that the complete causal chain remains in the log. Operational sites that measure longer takeover tails must lengthen $\\PreTriggerWindowDuration$ accordingly. Overwritten frames or dropped samples within this interval remain declared gaps in the evidence. @Sec-data-intervention-recovery uses this same window as its lookback horizon, searching it for the divergence point $\\DivergenceInstant$ and detailing why learner-visited states during drift are essential for offline policy training. The authority record provides the cryptographic ownership stamps that allow the training pipeline to distinguish unprivileged policy proposals from enforcer interventions and operator overrides.\n\nFinally, the operational record must reveal any subsequent tampering or truncation. Each entry carries a keyed cryptographic link over its predecessor, while periodic authenticated anchors are written to replay-protected storage located outside the logger&#x27;s primary write path. Rewritable non-volatile storage with authenticated writes satisfies this requirement, avoiding the need for dedicated write-once media. Under this design, investigators can determine exactly which log prefix each anchor authenticates, verify that cryptographic keys and anchors remained in trusted custody, and flag any unanchored tail or missing block as a declared gap. Hardware-level tamper resistance and loss declaration protect record integrity and expose missing evidence; they do not establish that a sensor reading is accurate. To serve post-incident reconstruction and downstream policy training, however, these raw evidence streams must be bound into a formal, machine-checkable artifact: the authority record.\n\n: **Linked Hash Chains**: @haber1991time proposed linking each timestamp to the cryptographic hash of its predecessor, ensuring that no historical record can be backdated or inserted without invalidating every subsequent link. The authority record inherits both this tamper-evident property and its limit, since an adversary possessing the signing keys and complete control over the storage device can recompute the entire chain. To protect against retroactive tampering, commitment anchors must be mirrored to an independent, replay-protected storage domain outside the logger&#x27;s mutable write path.\n\n## The Authority Record {#sec-intervention-authority-log}\n\nWhen an incident investigator asks, weeks after an abrupt blocked-aisle takeover, which entity drove the mobile base as it neared the rack end, the machine&#x27;s recorded telemetry provides evidence bounded by its capture coverage, clock alignment, independent plant measurements, and trusted custody. The **authority record** bridges real-time supervisory arbitration with downstream verification and release claims. To establish this audit trail, the authority record consumes two upstream contracts. From the enforcement record of @sec-enforcement-telemetry, it ingests the rungs of the fallback ladder so that every logged fallback explicitly identifies the rung executed by the permission path. From the placement record of @sec-placement-hardware-allocation, it incorporates the permission path&#x27;s declared timing bounds and bus arbitration settings, which every operator command also crosses, together with the placement record&#x27;s loaded-test field, which stays empty until verification fills it. The record shows who held each channel at each tick; it does not show who decided the speed, the staffing, or the operating domain that put the machine there.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The record of who decided that the machine could be in that situation at all, with a named owner for each decision, is developed in @sec-responsibility-approval-record.\n:::\n\nTo these upstream contracts, the authority record adds two deliverables of its own. The static authority manifest translates the capability matrix of @sec-intervention-human-authority into a machine-checkable contract. It binds every actuator channel to its authorized actors, operational preconditions, preemption tiers, and transition deadlines, and the safety microcontroller verifies and seals this manifest before the actuator drivers energize. Complementing the manifest, the record&#x27;s per-tick log forms an independent stream that records, for every control cycle, which entity held kinetic authority alongside the commands proposed, admitted, and dispatched, paired with independently measured physical response. In this stream, a missing cycle registers as an explicit communication or capture gap rather than an implied pass. The static manifest establishes the legal rules of interaction, while the per-tick log supports checking recorded runtime adherence under the capture, synchronization, custody, and declared-gap assumptions of @sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs.\n\n### The static authority manifest and capability binding {#sec-intervention-manifest-binding}\n\nIf an operator&#x27;s permissions or a channel&#x27;s rate limits can be edited in software while the machine runs, a post-incident log cannot prove which rules the arbiter was supposed to enforce at the moment of a takeover. The static authority manifest fixes those rules before the actuators energize: loading and cryptographically sealing the manifest during system initialization binds every subsequent event to the permissions and physical limits in force at boot. It specifies every permitted interaction as a verifiable contract between the physical platform and potential command sources. Each entry delineates a distinct physical authority domain using a hardware actuator mask, an authorized entity class (such as a local physical operator, a remote teleoperation console, an autonomous trajectory policy, or an emergency stop loop), and an integer **preemption tier**. Before any command vector enters the actuation pipeline, the permission path verifies the sender&#x27;s cryptographic role credentials against this manifest. Over the network, an operator&#x27;s command arrives as an `OPERATOR` payload under the same proposal header, carrying the operator&#x27;s role, the requested channel mask, the command vector, a monotonically increasing nonce, and a keyed authentication tag.\n\nIn addition to credential validation, each manifest entry enforces four operational invariants on its assigned channel:\n\n1. *Single-writer mutual exclusion*: Hardware access controls guarantee that exactly one entity writes to an actuator channel at any given instant.\n2. *Execution lease duration ($\\ManualLease$)*: For human operators, the lease defines the maximum tolerable silence before the enforcer revokes authority and triggers a state-dependent fallback.\n3. *Command rate ceiling ($\\CommandRateCeiling$)*: A hardware slew limit caps how rapidly a requested command vector may change between successive cycles, preventing abrupt step inputs.\n4. *Bumpless blending duration ($\\BlendingTransitionWindow$)*: A transition window enforces a $\\Glyph{C}^2$ continuous authority transfer ramp that preserves the kinodynamic feasibility bounds established in @sec-planning-kinodynamic-feasibility, requiring a qualified executable profile and a feasible state-matched fallback at every transition state if communication severs. The fallback depends on surviving sensing, controller, and energy premises; duration and continuity alone do not establish it.\n\n### Per-tick evidence tuples and the fault injection suite {#sec-intervention-evidence-tuples}\n\nA general telemetry tuple that records only a single machine-wide mode cannot show which channels were mid-blend, which fallback rung fired, or why a higher-tier source preempted a lower one. Every entry in the authority record&#x27;s per-tick log therefore expands the evidence tuple of @sec-intervention-trustworthy-logs with further fields. Specifically, one field names the specific actuator channels governed by the arbitration decision, another gives the ladder rung of any active `FALLBACK`, others record the root cause of any preemption and the instantaneous blend factor $\\BumplessTransferBlend(\\TimeInstant)$ of the enforcer-managed profile, and the last stores a cryptographic hash link to the preceding log block, authenticated through the platform&#x27;s protected anchoring scheme. The versioned static manifest supplies the decoding context, specifying physical units, field serialization order, clock synchronization mappings with their associated error bounds, credential validation rules, and hardware access control masks.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ **Byte layout:** The operator payload&#x27;s and the authority record&#x27;s fields, types, and offsets are given in @sec-appendix-systems-wire-authority.\n:::\n\nVerification draws its test inputs from the static manifest and its evidence from the event schema. The manifest&#x27;s `authority_claims` field hands @sec-verification-forward-analysis four authority rules by identifier, each of which becomes a hardware-in-the-loop fault case on the machine:\n\n- **A1.** Exactly one qualified enforcer output path writes each actuator channel. Source arbitration and hardware access controls prevent a second sender from bypassing that path while the policy and remote assistant both propose commands; successful bus arbitration alone does not satisfy this claim.\n- **A2.** No lower-tier source bypasses an active higher-tier source or the independent permission check. An autonomous policy proposal during `MAN` is ignored, and an assistant command that opposes the enforcer&#x27;s admitted barrier limit is clamped or rejected.\n- **A3.** An unacknowledged handover or stalled manual lease decays authority by $\\HandoverTimeout$ of @sec-intervention-clearance-deadlines, initiating controlled fallback by the earlier of the configured lease ceiling and the clearance-derived deadline of @eq-intervention-latest-fallback. This clearance deadline has a partial-budget upper bound of `[InterventionAssistantSession.t_latest_in_str]` at the `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_in_str]` slow-down before an in-loop takeover, and `[InterventionAssistantSession.t_latest_str]` for a handover requested by the policy at the `[InterventionAssistantSession.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed. Both values represent additional-delay allocations in the capture-origin budget. The runtime deadline evaluates the actual event history and future delay budgets without double-charging for elapsed capture-to-admission transit.\n- **A4.** The enforcer rejects replayed `veto_path`, `manual_drive`, or `accept_item` packets, and packets with invalid authentication tags or expired sequence counters, without modifying still-valid channel authority. It logs each rejection in the evidence tuple while allowing the active lease to age normally.\n\nThese authority rules turn the event log into a checkable contract. The same synchronized hardware timestamps also mark which controller generated each segment of an intervention trajectory, and that separation must exist before any segment can serve as a training target for imitation learning.\n\n### Causal masking and recovery eligibility {#sec-intervention-recovery-eligibility}\n\nIf a training pipeline feeds an entire rescued episode into behavioral cloning without filtering, the policy learns to imitate the autonomous drift that caused the near-miss, the enforcer&#x27;s synthetic blend spline, and the operator&#x27;s initial startled oscillations. The curation rules of @sec-data-intervention-recovery prevent that contamination by deciding which logged samples may train a policy: drift commands are quarantined, drift states are relabeled only by an automated expert, and only stabilized, reviewed recovery is admitted. Each rule keys on a timestamp that the authority record supplies, so a logging system that records intervention episodes for downstream policy retraining must preserve controller ownership, target meaning, and declared sample eligibility. This chapter&#x27;s conservative direct-imitation policy applies **causal intervention masking** to that record. Its optimization objective over recorded trajectories is formulated as:\n$$\\MaskedImitationLoss(\\ModelParameters) = \\mathbb{E}_{(\\MechanicalPlantState, \\ImitationTargetCommand, \\CausalInterventionMask) \\sim \\MaskedImitationDataset} \\left[ \\sum_{k=0}^{\\BehaviorCloningHorizon} \\CausalInterventionMask(\\ControlSampleIndex) \\cdot \\ell\\left(\\Policy(\\MechanicalPlantState_k), \\ImitationTargetCommand(\\ControlSampleIndex)\\right) \\right]$$ {#eq-intervention-causal-masking-loss}\nwhere $\\ell(\\Policy(\\MechanicalPlantState_k), \\ImitationTargetCommand(\\ControlSampleIndex))$ denotes the policy prediction loss (such as mean squared error across action chunks or score-matching denoising loss for diffusion policies), $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\ControlSampleIndex) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ is a binary causal mask, and $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\ControlSampleIndex)$ is the supervised training target. Each sample&#x27;s mask and target are evaluated at that sample&#x27;s logged hardware timestamp. The target carries its own symbol because it is not the admitted command $\\CertifiedControlAction$ of the evidence tuple; in each interval it is chosen from the logged fields, or supplied by an expert, as the following interval list states. Constructing the training dataset $\\MaskedImitationDataset$ requires selective transformation rather than wholesale ingestion of raw telemetry. While every logged state preserves its chronological position in the trajectory, its supervision target $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ and mask $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant)$ depend on the physical regime of the recording interval. As a result, the command dispatched to the hardware is not necessarily the training target. Deterministic authority transitions stamped by the safety microcontroller establish controller ownership. Empirical stabilization criteria, task validity checks, and the availability of expert labels, in turn, determine which samples receive $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 1$.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ INTERVAL LENGTHS FROM THE HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP LITERATURE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-recovery-eligibility, interval 3 and the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          paragraph before @tbl-14-intervention-learning.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Set two published interval lengths against the machine's own.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Sirius's fixed pre-intervention window (robot actions and time);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the design out-of-loop takeover as a multiple of it; the time</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          drivers needed to stabilize lateral control after resuming it,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          as a multiple of the pre-trigger window.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Literature constants (below) against AISLE.t_takeover_out_of_loop</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and AISLE.pre_trigger_window.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import second from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_count, fmt_multiple, fmt_time_range, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class InterventionLearningEvidence: \"\"\"Published interval lengths against the machine's takeover and window.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">Liu et al., RSS 2023 (Sirius), Sec. IV-D: pre-intervention class of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">l = 15 robot actions, matching an observed operator reaction time of 2 s.</h1>\n<p>sirius_actions = 15  # category: sourced sirius_window = 2 * second  # category: sourced</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">Merat et al., TR Part F 27 (2014), abstract: 35-40 s to stabilize</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">lateral control after resuming manual driving.</h1>\n<p>merat_lo = 35 <em> second  # category: sourced merat_hi = 40 </em> second  # category: sourced t_ool = AISLE.t_takeover_out_of_loop  # category: derived (sourced) t_pre = AISLE.pre_trigger_window  # category: derived</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_ool_over_sirius = (t_ool / sirius_window).to(\"\").magnitude merat_over_pre = (merat_lo / t_pre).to(\"\").magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_ool_over_sirius - 2.23) &lt; 0.01, f\"design takeover over Sirius window: {t_ool_over_sirius}\") check(abs(merat_over_pre - 7.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"stabilization over pre-trigger window: {merat_over_pre}\") check(t_ool &gt; sirius_window, \"The design takeover must outlast the fixed window.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>sirius_actions_str = fmt_count(sirius_actions, precision=0) sirius_window_str = fmt_latency(sirius_window, unit=second, precision=0) t_ool_over_sirius_mult_str = fmt_multiple(t_ool_over_sirius, precision=1) merat_stable_str = fmt_time_range(merat_lo, merat_hi, unit=second) merat_over_pre_mult_str = fmt_multiple(merat_over_pre, precision=0) t_pre_str = fmt_latency(t_pre, unit=second, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>A supervisory intervention does not occur as a single monolithic demonstration. Instead, it spans four distinct physical intervals along the execution timeline:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Compounding policy drift ($\\DivergenceInstant \\le \\TimeInstant &lt; \\CommitmentInstant$)</strong>: The autonomous policy $\\Policy$ drifts away from the expert state distribution, accumulating compounding errors while the human supervisor perceives the anomaly, orients, and decides to intervene (the pre-commit latency modeled in @eq-intervention-reaction-floor).</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><em>Masking policy</em>: The autonomous proposals $\\NominalControlCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ executed during this drift are quarantined from training targets. Without an external expert label, the system sets $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 0$.</li>\n<li><em>Relabeling</em>: The <em>learner-visited states</em> $\\MechanicalPlantState(\\TimeInstant)$ remain in the log for the corrective aggregation of @sec-data-intervention-recovery, whose relabeling rule accepts only an automated expert's labels. A drift state therefore enters training with $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 1$ and target $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\TimeInstant) = \\pi^*(\\MechanicalPlantState_t)$ only when such an expert, for example an omniscient trajectory planner with privileged simulation state, supplies the correction.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Atomic handover and enforcer blend ($\\CommitmentInstant \\le \\TimeInstant &lt; \\CommitmentInstant + \\BlendingTransitionWindow$)</strong>: The safety microcontroller latches the channel endpoints and computes a $\\Glyph{C}^2$ continuous smoothstep trajectory between controllers.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><em>Masking policy</em>: The system sets $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 0$ unconditionally. Dispatched setpoints $\\ControlCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ during this window are synthetic interpolation splines generated by the enforcer's deterministic quintic polynomial profile. Because they reflect neither learned neural policy intent nor human cognitive demonstration, training on this interval would condition the network to emit open-loop kinematic ramps detached from environmental feedback.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Operator re-orientation and hunting ($\\CommitmentInstant + \\BlendingTransitionWindow \\le \\TimeInstant &lt; \\OperatorStabilizationInstant$)</strong>: Upon receiving the active manual lease ($\\ManualLease$), the operator experiences neuromuscular latency, visual parallax, and control re-orientation, producing high-jerk, oscillatory corrections while regaining situational awareness.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><em>Masking policy</em>: The system enforces $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 0$. Bainbridge documented this control hunting directly. An operator whose active manual skill has lapsed during passive monitoring makes abrupt step corrections, oscillating around the setpoint before settling into smooth tracking [@bainbridge1983ironies]. Control stabilization often takes significantly longer than the physical handover itself. In driving simulator studies, operators resuming control from automated systems required approximately <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.merat_stable_str]</code> before their lateral steering stabilized [@merat2014transition], at least <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.merat_over_pre_mult_str]</code> the length of the machine's <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.t_pre_str]</code> pre-trigger window. Post-trigger masking therefore cannot rely on a fixed time constant; logging systems must evaluate empirical stabilization criteria. Discarding these initial frames prevents hesitation and oscillatory hunting from corrupting the imitation buffer. This exclusion involves an engineering trade-off, because interval 3 also contains the operator's initial corrective inputs, which certain human-gated methods retain (@tbl-14-intervention-learning). The enforcer marks the stabilization boundary $\\OperatorStabilizationInstant$ only after tracking error and command slew have remained within calibrated limits for a declared dwell interval. Later violations mask the affected samples again. The limits are:</li>\n<p>$$\\lVert \\TakeoverTrackingError(\\TimeInstant) \\rVert \\le \\StabilizationErrorThreshold \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\lVert \\HumanCommandRate(\\TimeInstant) \\rVert \\le \\StabilizationSlewCeiling.$$ Here, $\\TakeoverTrackingError(\\TimeInstant)$ is the difference between the admitted manual setpoint and the measured plant response, $\\HumanCommandRate(\\TimeInstant)$ is the time derivative of the operator's command, and the parameter $\\StabilizationSlewCeiling$ denotes a channel-specific slew ceiling, representing an acceleration derivative (jerk) only when the underlying command vector represents acceleration. Both thresholds must hold continuously across the dwell window before the logger lifts the mask.</p>\n</ul>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Stabilized expert recovery ($\\TimeInstant \\ge \\OperatorStabilizationInstant$)</strong>: The operator executes coherent, task-directed recovery maneuvers once measured control metrics stabilize. Smooth motion alone does not validate a recovery; the episode must also satisfy formal task-completion criteria before its samples qualify as expert targets.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><em>Masking policy</em>: The system sets $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant) = 1$ with imitation target $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\TimeInstant) = \\HumanControlCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ exclusively for stabilized segments that pass task-validity review. All other telemetry frames remain masked from direct imitation, so this interval is the only post-takeover segment the curation rules admit as expert recovery data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Feeding unsegmented telemetry directly into behavioral cloning or diffusion policies injects synthetic enforcer ramps and operator control transients into the training distribution. The causal mask $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant)$ and target vector $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ use the commit and state timestamps recorded by the safety microcontroller to enforce controller ownership boundaries, bounded by hardware clock synchronization and sampling limits.</p>\n<p>The binary causal mask represents the most conservative member of a broader algorithmic spectrum. Contemporary human-in-the-loop learning methods treat interventions in fundamentally different ways: as direct state labels, as importance weights over surrounding trajectories, or as negative reinforcement signals indicating policy failure. @Tbl-14-intervention-learning maps five representative paradigms onto these four operational intervals. For instance, Human-Gated DAgger (HG-DAgger) grants the human supervisor full authority upon takeover and aggregates only the human's executed commands, leaving interval 1 unmasked but unlabeled [@kelly2019hgdagger]. Sirius generalizes $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant)$ by assigning categorical importance weights across sample classes; it assigns zero weight to a fixed pre-intervention window while upweighting human recovery trajectories [@liu2023sirius]. Specifically, Sirius sizes its pre-intervention window to <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.sirius_actions_str]</code> robot actions (approximately <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.sirius_window_str]</code>), derived from observed human reaction times. While Sirius fixes this window a priori, the authority record timestamps the commit and supports reconstruction of the drift interval once $\\DivergenceInstant$ is identified from the retained evidence, a duration that the out-of-loop takeover budget of @sec-intervention-reaction-drift shows can be <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.t_ool_over_sirius_mult_str]</code> longer. Meanwhile, Reinforcement Learning from Intervention Feedback (RLIF) eliminates the assumption of human optimality altogether, treating the intervention event as a negative reward penalty within an off-policy reinforcement learning framework [@luo2024rlif]. Similarly, Human-in-the-Loop Sample-Efficient Robot Learning (HIL-SERL) incorporates human interventions as targeted correction buffers for real-world reinforcement learning [@luo2025hilserl]. Finally, RECAP, which trains the $\\pi^{*}_{0.6}$ vision-language-action model, blends autonomous rollouts, teleoperated interventions, and demonstrations using advantage conditioning, explicitly labeling human takeover corrections as positive-advantage actions [@physicalintelligence2025recap].</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Method (year)</th><th>What the intervention becomes</th><th>Interval 1 (policy drift)</th><th>Interval 3 (re-orientation)</th><th>Needs from the authority record</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Causal mask (this chapter)</td><td>Boundary between owners</td><td>Excluded unless an automated expert labels it</td><td>Excluded until measured stabilization</td><td>Commit, blend end, stabilization times</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HG-DAgger (2019)</td><td>Label: human commands while in control</td><td>Not labeled</td><td>Kept with the human's commands</td><td>Owner per sample</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sirius (2023)</td><td>Weight per sample class</td><td>Fixed pre-intervention window, weight zero</td><td>Kept, upweighted</td><td>Commit time; window measured to it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>RLIF (2024)</td><td>Reward: the takeover itself is a penalty</td><td>Kept; the takeover penalizes it</td><td>Human samples added to the data</td><td>Commit time as the reward event</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HIL-SERL (2025)</td><td>Correction data for a reinforcement learner</td><td>Kept as the learner's own experience</td><td>Kept as correction data</td><td>Owner per sample</td></tr>\n<tr><td>RECAP, $\\pi^{*}_{0.6}$ (2025)</td><td>Correction marked as a positive-advantage action</td><td>Kept; a value function scores it</td><td>Kept, marked positive</td><td>Owner per sample; episode outcome</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Human-in-the-loop learning methods as of 2026, placed on the four intervals</strong>: Every method needs the ownership boundary the authority record holds; they differ in what the intervention becomes and in which intervals they train on. None trains on the enforcer's blend as a demonstration. Entries summarize each method's published design [@kelly2019hgdagger; @liu2023sirius; @luo2024rlif; @luo2025hilserl; @physicalintelligence2025recap]; a later edition should refresh the list. {#tbl-14-intervention-learning tbl-colwidths=\"[17,24,21,19,19]\"}</p>\n<p>Individual learning algorithms change, but every one of them must answer the same three systems questions: who held write authority over each sample, whether the takeover serves as a supervised label, an importance weight, or a reward penalty, and which physical intervals enter the training set. Downstream training pipelines cannot recover those ownership boundaries from trajectory kinematics alone. Recording the commit, blend-end, and stabilization timestamps on the safety microcontroller's clock grounds dataset curation and fleet-level intervention rates directly in hardware measurements.</p>\n<p>When an engineering team assembles the safety case for release (@sec-release-argument-plant-alignment), the authority record also supplies the primary evidence that runtime handovers obey rules A1 through A4 (claim C6 in @tbl-16-cae-scorecard). That release dossier pairs the static authority manifest with the loaded stopping and blending measurements from @sec-intervention-bumpless-transfer and hardware-in-the-loop traces that verify an unbroken audit chain. Because a handover that looks clean on the communication bus can still slip a wheel on a wet floor or jolt a loaded tote rack, the dossier always checks bus timestamps against independent plant sensors. It treats measured preemption times as empirical percentiles rather than analytical worst-case bounds.</p>\n<p>↩ The distinction between an empirical latency percentile and a response-time bound is established in @sec-nervous-deterministic-fieldbuses.</p>\n<p>The authority record is useful twice, once to an investigator and once to a learner, and both uses depend on the same ownership timestamps.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Evidence tuple</strong>: Can you name the incident question that becomes unanswerable when the log drops $\\NominalControlCommand$, when it drops $\\CertifiedControlAction$, and when it drops $\\RemainingExecutionLease$?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Interval masks</strong>: For one takeover, can you assign $\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant)$ and $\\ImitationTargetCommand(\\TimeInstant)$ to each of the four intervals and name the logged event that bounds each?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Sample count estimate</strong>: An out-of-loop takeover commits <code>[InterventionPreTriggerWindow.t_ool_str]</code> after the divergence, and the arm then blends for <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.arm_budget_str]</code>. At the <code>[InterventionBlendBudget.tick_str]</code> permission tick, can you estimate how many logged ticks fall in intervals 1 and 2, and how many of them may train an imitation policy when no automated expert is available?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Fixed windows</strong>: Can you explain what a fixed <code>[InterventionLearningEvidence.sirius_window_str]</code> pre-intervention window misclassifies for a takeover that took the design out-of-loop time?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>Flipping a bit in an authority register does not complete a safe supervisory handover. A real-time physical AI system must preserve its dynamic stopping margin and protective enforcement functions throughout every stage of transfer. In addition, downstream imitation learning pipelines cannot treat intervention logs as unblemished expert recoveries without explicit causal filtering. When systems fail in deployment, the breakdown almost always traces back to one of four subtle architectural traps.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ONE FIXED ACKNOWLEDGMENT WINDOW AT TWO SPEEDS\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-intervention-fallacies-pitfalls, the fixed-timeout pitfall.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show that one configured window fits at the crawl and overruns at\n# │          the aisle speed.\n# │ Show:    Travel during the window and the overrun at 1.3 m/s; the spare\n# │          and T_latest at the out-of-loop crawl.\n# │ How:     Finished stopping budget (jerk-limited resident stop, all\n# │          overheads) at each speed, the window added to tau_delay.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second, millimeter, millisecond\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass InterventionFixedWindowPitfall:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;A configured acknowledgment window judged against the budget at two speeds.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    at_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseFixedWindowAtAisleSpeed  # the 500 ms window waited out at the aisle speed\n    at_crawl = Scenarios.WarehouseFixedWindowAtCrawl  # the same window at the crawl\n    v = at_aisle.speed  # category: chosen\n    v_crawl = at_crawl.speed  # category: chosen\n    d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative\n    window = InterventionTimeoutBudget.config  # category: chosen\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    fast, slow = at_aisle.result, at_crawl.result\n    budget = fast.machine.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    travel = fast.takeover_travel.to(millimeter)\n    overrun = (-fast.with_takeover.spare).to(millimeter)\n    spare_crawl = slow.machine.spare.to(millimeter)\n    t_latest_crawl = (spare_crawl / v_crawl).to(millisecond)\n    # Stop with the window waited out, at each speed (margin figure).\n    d_stop = fast.with_takeover.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    d_stop_crawl = slow.with_takeover.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(budget.magnitude - 968.99) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;finished budget at the aisle speed: {budget}&quot;)\n    check(abs((at_aisle.takeover - window).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, &quot;The waited window is the configured ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(abs((t_latest_crawl - slow.deadline).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;The crawl&#x27;s spare as time is its fallback deadline.&quot;)\n    # margin_fixed_window_stop_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 500 ms, 1619 mm, 307 mm, 1100 mm\n    check(round(d_stop.magnitude) == 1619 and round(d_stop_crawl.magnitude) == 307, f&quot;window stops: {d_stop}, {d_stop_crawl}&quot;)\n    check(\n        round(window.to(millisecond).magnitude) == 500 and round(d_clear.to(millimeter).magnitude) == 1100, &quot;window and clear distance in the fig-alt&quot;\n    )\n    check(abs(v_crawl.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.15) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;crawl speed in the fig-alt: {v_crawl}&quot;)\n    check(overrun.magnitude &gt; 0, &quot;The window must overrun the clear distance at the aisle speed.&quot;)\n    check(t_latest_crawl &gt; window, &quot;The same window must fit at the crawl.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    v_crawl_str = fmt_velocity(v_crawl, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    window_str = fmt_latency(window, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    travel_str = fmt_length(travel, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    overrun_str = fmt_length(overrun, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    t_latest_crawl_str = fmt_latency(t_latest_crawl, unit=second, precision=1)\n]```\n\n\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n\n**Pitfall**: *Treating human takeover timeout as a static time delay rather than a dynamic spatial stopping budget.*\n\nConsider a mobile manipulator that initiates a takeover request at its operational `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.v_str]` aisle speed under a statically configured `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.window_str]` acknowledgment window. If the controller waits out that full window before commanding brakes, the vehicle covers `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.travel_str]` of unbraked travel. When combined with the jerk-limited resident stop profile and actuator execution overheads, the total stopping distance ends `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.overrun_str]` beyond the `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.d_clear_str]` rack-end clearance, causing a physical collision. Conversely, at an out-of-loop crawl speed of `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.v_crawl_str]`, that same window consumes negligible distance, leaving `[InterventionFixedWindowPitfall.t_latest_crawl_str]` of margin before fallback braking must begin. The wait costs travel in proportion to speed, and the stop that follows grows with its square, so a timeout that leaves ample response time at a crawl overruns the clear distance at aisle speed. Real-time supervisory handovers cannot treat human response intervals as fixed wall-clock durations. The permissible wait time must be computed dynamically from instantaneous velocity, remaining obstacle clearance, and validated brake deceleration envelopes. Autonomous fallback must begin the moment the remaining clearance matches the required stopping profile.\n\n**A supervisory timeout is not a static timer; it is a dynamically bounded spatial margin that must fit the instantaneous stopping budget.**\n\n**Architect&#x27;s countermeasure:** On every control cycle, evaluate the instantaneous obstacle clearance against a complete stopping budget composed solely of future sensing delays, brake actuation lag, and jerk-limited deceleration. Dynamically recompute a candidate absolute deadline, anchored to a consistent time origin, from @eq-intervention-latest-fallback, which gives the longest wait whose delay travel and stop suffix still fit inside the remaining clearance. If a newly calculated deadline is earlier than the currently active deadline, tighten the expiration threshold immediately; never extend or restart an active countdown based on optimistic recalculations. Account for sensor pipeline latency consistently, and do not subtract historical capture-to-enforcement delay from clearance measurements that have already physically reflected that travel. If supervisory acknowledgment does not arrive before the deadline expires, immediately trigger the validated autonomous fallback brake sequence.\n\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n\n**Fallacy**: *Human manual takeover should bypass automated safety barriers and grant unconstrained, open-ended actuator authority.*\n\nWhen a remote operator takes manual control of a mobile manipulator to steer it around an unexpected obstacle, the chosen steering commands might vector the chassis directly toward a warehouse rack upright. Granting manual control alters only the origin of proposed motion setpoints; it cannot alter chassis momentum, tire-floor friction coefficients, or environmental obstacle boundaries. The low-level safety enforcer must therefore evaluate human manual proposals against the formal admissible motion set on every cycle. If the operator commands a trajectory that risks collision or dynamic instability, the enforcer projects the command onto the nearest safe, kinodynamically feasible trajectory. If no safe trajectory exists within the admissible envelope, the enforcer rejects the proposal entirely and triggers an immediate autonomous fallback. Manual intervention grants the operator authority to guide high-level intent, but mechanical stresses, obstacle clearance, and brake margins remain hard physical constraints enforced at the joint actuator boundary.\n\nIn addition, manual intervention must operate under strictly bounded, perishable leases rather than open-ended authority grants. Transient wireless packet loss or operator distraction can easily leave stale velocity vectors active in flight. To prevent uncommanded runaway motion, every intervention session requires a cryptographically renewed manual lease calibrated to the active operational mode&#x27;s stopping reserve. In this warehouse aisle scenario, the system enforces a `[InterventionTimeoutBudget.manual_lease_str]` manual lease. If periodic, authenticated heartbeat renewals fail to arrive before the lease expires, the safety microcontroller immediately revokes proposal authority and executes an autonomous fallback stop independently of host software or operator intervention.\n\n**Manual intervention transfers the authority to propose setpoints, but never the authority to bypass physical barrier certificates or operate on stale communication.**\n\n**Architect&#x27;s countermeasure:** Channel all teleoperation and manual override inputs through the enforcer&#x27;s control barrier certificates and kinodynamic clamping filters. When an operator command exceeds dynamic rollover thresholds, threatens structural shear limits, or breaches clearance buffers, the enforcer automatically clamps the actuation setpoint to the boundary of the safe set or initiates an emergency stop. Subordinate all manual authority to a hardware-timed watchdog lease sized to the active mode&#x27;s stopping reserve, guaranteeing that lease expiration automatically executes a deterministic brake or hold sequence without software intervention.\n\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ FORGED PACKETS QUEUED AHEAD OF A GENUINE VETO</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-intervention-fallacies-pitfalls, the verifier-flooding</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          pitfall.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that authentication alone leaves an availability hazard:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          forged packets that the verifier must reject still spend its time.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    The signature verification time per packet; the fewest forged</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          signed takeover requests queued ahead of a genuine veto that push</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          its admission past T_latest at the aisle speed; the admission time</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          they cause and the arrival rate they need; the same count for</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          MAC-only frames.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Serial verification on the permission processor, each signed</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          packet taking t_sig of InterventionAuthBudget; the genuine veto</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          finishes after (n + 1) verifications; the forgeries must arrive</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          within that interval (Scenarios.WarehouseRemoteCommandAuthentication).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import millisecond, second, hertz from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_int, fmt_latency, fmt_count, check</p>\n<p>class InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall: \"\"\"Forged takeover packets that delay a genuine veto past T_latest.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_verify = InterventionAuthBudget.t_sig  # category: derived (sourced cycles / MCU clock) t_mac = InterventionAuthBudget.t_mac  # category: derived t_latest = InterventionTimeoutBudget.t_latest  # category: derived</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>flood = Scenarios.WarehouseRemoteCommandAuthentication.result</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">Fewest forged packets n with (n + 1) * t_verify &gt; t_latest.</h1>\n<p>n_forged = flood.forged_to_miss t_admit = flood.veto_verified_at.to(millisecond)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">Whole packets per second the forger must sustain, rounded up.</h1>\n<p>arrival_rate = flood.forged_rate n_forged_mac = flood.forged_mac_to_miss</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">The same count once the allocated Delta_da of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission is charged.</h1>\n<p>n_forged_da = flood.forged_to_miss_after_admission</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(n_forged == 55, f\"forged packets that push the veto past T_latest: {n_forged}\") check(t_admit &gt; t_latest, \"The queued forgeries must push the veto past T_latest.\") check(n_forged <em> t_verify &lt;= t_latest, \"One fewer forgery must still let the veto fit.\") check(n_forged_mac &gt; 100 </em> n_forged, \"A MAC-only flood needs far more frames than a signature flood.\") check(n_forged_da == 20, f\"forged packets once Delta_da is charged: {n_forged_da}\") check(abs((flood.verify_time - t_verify).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-12, \"The flood prices the same verification as the authentication cell.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_verify_str = fmt_latency(t_verify, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=millisecond, precision=2) t_admit_str = fmt_latency(t_admit, unit=millisecond, precision=2) n_forged_str = fmt_count(n_forged) arrival_rate_str = fmt_int(arrival_rate) n_forged_mac_str = fmt_count(n_forged_mac) n_forged_da_str = fmt_count(n_forged_da) left_da_str = fmt_latency(InterventionAdmissionAllocation.left_aisle, unit=millisecond, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Relying on cryptographic authentication without rate limiting the traffic that reaches the verifier.</em></p>\n<p>Cryptographic authentication guarantees message authenticity and integrity, but it introduces an asymmetric computation cost that an adversary or a misbehaving transceiver can exploit to starve real-time processing. Suppose an admission controller validates incoming signed remote takeover requests (@sec-intervention-authenticating-intervention) using an embedded microcontroller that requires <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.t_verify_str]</code> to verify each Ed25519 digital signature. Malformed inputs may be rejected cheaply, but well-formed invalid signatures can still reach the expensive verification path. The following queue model charges every such request the stated verification time.</p>\n<p>If a malfunctioning or compromised device on the network link floods the interface with bogus signed <code>manual_drive</code> packets, as few as <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.n_forged_str]</code> forged requests queued ahead of a critical <code>veto_path</code> message from a remote supervisor will delay its verification to <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.t_admit_str]</code>. At aisle speed, this delay exceeds the fallback deadline $\\FallbackDeadline$ of <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.t_latest_str]</code>. When charging the allocated downstream admission delay $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, the usable admission margin shrinks to <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.left_da_str]</code>, meaning just <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.n_forged_da_str]</code> forged packets suffice to cause a deadline violation. Sustaining this denial-of-service attack requires an injection rate of only <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.arrival_rate_str]</code> packets per second, a trivial load for even a low-bandwidth industrial wireless channel.</p>\n<p>Substituting symmetric MACs shifts the computational bottleneck to the network transceiver without eliminating the underlying vulnerability. Validating a MAC requires far fewer cycles, so roughly <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.n_forged_mac_str]</code> forged frames within the same window are needed to induce a miss, yet the queue remains susceptible to line-rate saturating traffic. The robot's independently wired protective stops and hardware lease timers must never share queues with network ingress. Those local safety channels must remain fully operational even when the cryptographic verifier is saturated. To protect real-time responsiveness, the ingestion pipeline must deploy hardware mailbox filters and per-source rate limiters ahead of the verification queue, and safety qualification tests must evaluate worst-case intervention latency under full adversarial packet inundation.</p>\n<p><strong>Cryptographic authentication protects command integrity but introduces an asymmetric availability risk, because an admission verifier spends finite compute cycles rejecting forged traffic before it can service critical safety messages.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure:</strong> Deploy hardware mailbox filtering and per-source token-bucket rate limiters on the fieldbus interface ahead of the software verification queue. In addition, route emergency protective stops and fail-safe interlocks through dedicated, hardwired electrical circuits that bypass digital networking and cryptographic parsing entirely.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Token Bucket</strong>: A limiter holds up to $\\TokenBucketDepth$ tokens, refills at rate $\\TokenBucketRefillRate$, and admits a packet only by spending a token, so a source may burst $\\TokenBucketDepth$ packets but never sustain more than $\\TokenBucketRefillRate$. Placed before the verifier, it bounds the forged signatures that can arrive within $\\FallbackDeadline$ at $\\TokenBucketDepth + \\TokenBucketRefillRate\\,\\FallbackDeadline$. For the single-source queue modeled here, keeping that bound below the <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.n_forged_str]</code> forgeries that push a genuine veto past its deadline, or <code>[InterventionVerifierFloodPitfall.n_forged_da_str]</code> once $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ is charged, sizes $\\TokenBucketDepth$ and $\\TokenBucketRefillRate$ from the stopping budget rather than from link capacity. A deployed ingress must also bound aggregate traffic across sources, include already queued work and legitimate traffic, and prevent forged source identifiers from bypassing those limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Training directly on raw human intervention logs captures expert demonstrations.</em></p>\n<p>Training on raw intervention logs commits the learner-action contamination of @sec-data-intervention-recovery. The raw telemetry of a rescued episode is not a continuous expert demonstration. It stitches the policy's diverging trajectory to the operator's reaction latency, the corrective jerks as the operator regains situational awareness, and the overshoots before stabilization, and a policy cloned from it reproduces the oscillations that made the takeover necessary.</p>\n<p>The methods in @tbl-14-intervention-learning require attribution of who generated each state-action pair and a declared interpretation of the sample as a target, a weight, or a reward. Neither a coarse session-level takeover timestamp nor an active manual authority bit guarantees that recorded telemetry is fit for policy training. This chapter's conservative direct-imitation policy segments intervention trajectories into distinct causal regimes and masks transitional dynamics; the alternative objectives in @tbl-14-intervention-learning retain some early corrections.</p>\n<p><strong>Unsegmented intervention telemetry records human cognitive lag and abrupt corrective overshoots; naively cloning these trajectories trains policies to reproduce the instability that triggered the takeover.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure:</strong> For this chapter's conservative direct-imitation corpus, apply causal intervention masking (@eq-intervention-causal-masking-loss), taking the interval boundaries from the commit, blend-end, and stabilization timestamps on the safety microcontroller's monotonic hardware clock. Only Interval 4, validated post-stabilization guidance, trains as a human demonstration ($\\CausalInterventionMask(\\TimeInstant)=1$); Interval 1 drift states train only under an automated expert's labels, and everything else stays in the record for forensics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>", "quizzes": []};
